WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.890
&gt;&gt; My name is Stefan Saroiu,

00:00:05.891 --> 00:00:07.740
and together with
my colleague Alec Wolman,

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:12.420
we're the session co-chairs
for the security session.

00:00:12.421 --> 00:00:15.180
I'm sure all of you
are here to find

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:17.615
out the work that both
Microsoft and Academia

00:00:17.616 --> 00:00:20.805
has been doing on these attacks

00:00:20.806 --> 00:00:23.310
that stemmed from
speculative executions for CPUs

00:00:23.311 --> 00:00:25.830
like Meltdown and Spectre or from

00:00:25.831 --> 00:00:29.530
the density of the cells
backed in DRAM like RowHammer.

00:00:29.531 --> 00:00:31.350
I was really hoping to have

00:00:31.351 --> 00:00:33.690
a discussion throughout the talk.

00:00:33.691 --> 00:00:35.310
We're going to have
three speakers

00:00:35.311 --> 00:00:37.690
and we're going to
introduce each of them.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.880
I think it's going
to be difficult

00:00:40.881 --> 00:00:42.440
and the reason for that
is because I think

00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:44.330
the dynamics of
these ROM really is not

00:00:44.331 --> 00:00:47.290
amenable to the discussions
I would like to have.

00:00:47.291 --> 00:00:50.300
Nevertheless, I strongly
encourage you to

00:00:50.301 --> 00:00:51.680
raise your hand and
ask questions if you

00:00:51.681 --> 00:00:53.180
like in the middle of the talk,

00:00:53.181 --> 00:00:55.480
and we have ample time for Q&amp;A at

00:00:55.481 --> 00:00:57.945
the end of each presentation.

00:00:57.946 --> 00:01:04.250
Okay. So, it's my pleasure

00:01:04.251 --> 00:01:07.715
to introduce to you the first
speaker, Christopher Ertl.

00:01:07.716 --> 00:01:10.830
He's a Security Software
Engineer in what's

00:01:10.831 --> 00:01:14.270
called MSRC at Microsoft.

00:01:14.271 --> 00:01:17.330
MSRC stands for
Microsoft Response Center,

00:01:17.331 --> 00:01:19.255
and this is the team that

00:01:19.256 --> 00:01:22.940
deals with security vulnerabilities
especially in Azure,

00:01:22.941 --> 00:01:25.270
but is your guys' mandate

00:01:25.271 --> 00:01:27.580
just for Azure or for
the whole Microsoft?

00:01:27.581 --> 00:01:29.950
&gt;&gt; No, so it's for
all Microsoft products

00:01:29.951 --> 00:01:33.470
including browser,
Office, et cetera.

00:01:33.471 --> 00:01:37.295
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, he's

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:39.820
going to talk a little
bit about some of

00:01:39.821 --> 00:01:42.580
the things that Microsoft
has been doing to

00:01:42.581 --> 00:01:46.440
mitigate Meltdown and
Spectre. Thank you.

00:01:46.441 --> 00:01:47.960
&gt;&gt; All right. Thank you, Stefan.

00:01:47.961 --> 00:01:49.655
So, good morning everyone.

00:01:49.656 --> 00:01:52.005
Once again, my name
is Christopher Etrl,

00:01:52.006 --> 00:01:53.310
I'm going to be talking about

00:01:53.311 --> 00:01:55.119
the Spectre and Meltdown
vulnerabilities

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.550
and how we're able
to mitigate them.

00:01:57.551 --> 00:02:00.990
All right, so Spectre
and Meltdown,

00:02:00.991 --> 00:02:04.640
these issues gained
a huge amount of interest from

00:02:04.641 --> 00:02:06.860
the research community when

00:02:06.861 --> 00:02:08.860
they were disclosed
in January this year.

00:02:08.861 --> 00:02:10.130
The reason for that is

00:02:10.131 --> 00:02:13.460
because they represent
a fundamentally new class of

00:02:13.461 --> 00:02:16.535
hardware security vulnerability
which allows leaking

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:20.655
information across security
boundaries from the browser,

00:02:20.656 --> 00:02:22.360
the hypervisor, et cetera.

00:02:22.361 --> 00:02:25.730
All right.

00:02:25.731 --> 00:02:27.590
So, when we were first
made available of

00:02:27.591 --> 00:02:30.285
these issues in June last year,

00:02:30.286 --> 00:02:33.065
we kicked off a SSIRP
incident response process

00:02:33.066 --> 00:02:34.580
and this is typical for

00:02:34.581 --> 00:02:36.850
whenever we're made aware

00:02:36.851 --> 00:02:40.110
of a critical security
vulnerability,

00:02:40.111 --> 00:02:42.245
either being exploited
in the wild or

00:02:42.246 --> 00:02:44.780
just a high threat which requires

00:02:44.781 --> 00:02:47.120
mobilizing a large number
of people within

00:02:47.121 --> 00:02:49.945
Microsoft to drive
remediation of the fixes.

00:02:49.946 --> 00:02:54.470
So, Spectre and Meltdown.

00:02:54.471 --> 00:02:58.580
Once again, they
have implications

00:02:58.581 --> 00:03:01.100
across nearly every
security boundary

00:03:01.101 --> 00:03:02.525
and allow potentially

00:03:02.526 --> 00:03:06.200
disclosing information
such as passwords in

00:03:06.201 --> 00:03:07.835
the browser or

00:03:07.836 --> 00:03:12.279
a guest-to-guest in
virtualization context.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.680
So, what I'm going to
do now is I'm going

00:03:15.681 --> 00:03:18.400
to break down the
attacks themselves,

00:03:18.401 --> 00:03:21.860
more generally how
speculative execution

00:03:21.861 --> 00:03:23.730
can lead to a side channel.

00:03:23.731 --> 00:03:26.600
Then afterwards, we can
go on to how we might be

00:03:26.601 --> 00:03:29.945
able to mitigate
those. All right.

00:03:29.946 --> 00:03:32.725
So before we can get into
speculative execution itself,

00:03:32.726 --> 00:03:34.250
I'm going to need to
explain a bit about

00:03:34.251 --> 00:03:36.210
how a modern CPU works.

00:03:36.211 --> 00:03:38.795
So, typically, when
we see assembly code

00:03:38.796 --> 00:03:41.880
and we consider how CPU executes,

00:03:41.881 --> 00:03:43.970
we generally think
of each instruction

00:03:43.971 --> 00:03:46.375
executing one after
the other sequentially.

00:03:46.376 --> 00:03:50.065
But in reality, it's a bit
more complicated than this.

00:03:50.066 --> 00:03:52.640
Instructions are first
decoded into a series of

00:03:52.641 --> 00:03:55.905
microoperations which are
placed into a reorder buffer,

00:03:55.906 --> 00:03:57.980
and from there, the CPU
is able to make

00:03:57.981 --> 00:04:00.130
use of several optimizations.

00:04:00.131 --> 00:04:03.115
So, the first of which
is being superscalar,

00:04:03.116 --> 00:04:04.475
is able to execute

00:04:04.476 --> 00:04:09.230
certain micro-operations
in parallel concurrently.

00:04:09.231 --> 00:04:12.319
Second, which is
out-of-order execution

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.770
and this essentially
allows the CPU to

00:04:15.771 --> 00:04:18.460
start executing later
instructions before

00:04:18.461 --> 00:04:20.150
earlier ones to make best use of

00:04:20.151 --> 00:04:22.910
the available execution units.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.450
Yeah, this is faster
than just waiting for

00:04:26.451 --> 00:04:27.650
each instruction to complete

00:04:27.651 --> 00:04:29.690
before the next one can start.

00:04:29.691 --> 00:04:31.880
So, speculative execution is

00:04:31.881 --> 00:04:36.560
just an extension of
out-of-order execution.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.590
So, when the CPU has

00:04:40.591 --> 00:04:44.145
some dependency on
the result of an operation,

00:04:44.146 --> 00:04:45.880
rather than waiting for that

00:04:45.881 --> 00:04:49.005
to resolve and results
be made available,

00:04:49.006 --> 00:04:51.800
it can begin executing
speculatively

00:04:51.801 --> 00:04:55.340
according to a prediction that
it makes on this outcome.

00:04:55.341 --> 00:04:59.960
The reasoning behind this is
that once the result is made

00:04:59.961 --> 00:05:04.895
available and if
the prediction was correct,

00:05:04.896 --> 00:05:08.120
the results of speculative
execution can be committed,

00:05:08.121 --> 00:05:10.640
so that's the calculated
register values

00:05:10.641 --> 00:05:13.805
and any memory
stores for example.

00:05:13.806 --> 00:05:16.790
This is much quicker
than waiting for

00:05:16.791 --> 00:05:18.770
the outcome to be

00:05:18.771 --> 00:05:21.260
made available before
starting execution.

00:05:21.261 --> 00:05:23.160
Conversely, if the prediction,

00:05:23.161 --> 00:05:27.600
that speculation ran
on was incorrect,

00:05:27.601 --> 00:05:31.240
the results will be discarded
and the execution unrolled.

00:05:31.241 --> 00:05:33.610
All right.

00:05:33.611 --> 00:05:35.300
So the fundamental problem

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:36.830
with speculative execution which

00:05:36.831 --> 00:05:39.980
led to the Spectre
and Meltdown vulnerabilities is

00:05:39.981 --> 00:05:42.320
that not everything
is thrown away

00:05:42.321 --> 00:05:47.160
when incorrect speculative
execution is unrolled.

00:05:47.161 --> 00:05:49.410
In particular, changes to

00:05:49.411 --> 00:05:53.350
the cache state are
not always unrolled,

00:05:53.351 --> 00:05:55.850
and that can contain private data

00:05:55.851 --> 00:05:58.870
which an attacker might
be able to later observe.

00:05:58.871 --> 00:06:01.240
All right. So now,

00:06:01.241 --> 00:06:03.760
I'm going to move on to
the variants themselves.

00:06:03.761 --> 00:06:06.120
So, starting with variant one.

00:06:06.121 --> 00:06:11.175
This was where a conditional
branch would mispredict.

00:06:11.176 --> 00:06:16.580
So here, we have
a typical bounds check on

00:06:16.581 --> 00:06:18.530
an untrusted index
before using it

00:06:18.531 --> 00:06:22.470
as an array index
for this buffer.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.750
This is a typical code pattern,

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:28.170
very common in C and C++ code,

00:06:28.171 --> 00:06:33.170
but consider if this bounds
check is mispredicted and

00:06:33.171 --> 00:06:34.820
the inner code is executed was

00:06:34.821 --> 00:06:36.110
untrusted index is actually

00:06:36.111 --> 00:06:38.225
greater than or equal to length.

00:06:38.226 --> 00:06:41.750
In this case, what will
happen is value will be

00:06:41.751 --> 00:06:46.560
read from depending on
the types involved,

00:06:46.561 --> 00:06:48.350
this could be an
arbitrary virtual address

00:06:48.351 --> 00:06:50.120
considering if buffers or by

00:06:50.121 --> 00:06:54.800
pointer and untrusted index
is 64-bit value.

00:06:54.801 --> 00:06:57.020
This could result
in reading value

00:06:57.021 --> 00:06:59.350
from an arbitrary
virtual address.

00:06:59.351 --> 00:07:04.200
Then after that, a secondary
index will be performed

00:07:04.201 --> 00:07:06.515
which loads
a different cache line

00:07:06.516 --> 00:07:09.530
depending on this private value.

00:07:09.531 --> 00:07:11.990
So, the result of this is that if

00:07:11.991 --> 00:07:15.730
an attacker can execute
this code speculatively,

00:07:15.731 --> 00:07:18.979
a different cache line will
be loaded as an artifact

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:23.020
of that secret value that
should not be made available.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:26.050
Variant two was where

00:07:26.051 --> 00:07:29.055
the target of an indirect branch
would be mispredicted.

00:07:29.056 --> 00:07:32.320
So, indirect branches are

00:07:32.321 --> 00:07:35.710
used when the compiler
doesn't know at compile time,

00:07:35.711 --> 00:07:37.390
what the target of
the branch will be.

00:07:37.391 --> 00:07:42.520
So typically, a function pointer
or a vtable for example.

00:07:42.521 --> 00:07:45.610
If speculative execution
executes one of

00:07:45.611 --> 00:07:48.210
these indirect branches
on a register,

00:07:48.211 --> 00:07:52.815
it might jump to
an incorrect target.

00:07:52.816 --> 00:07:55.885
Similar to before,
what might happen

00:07:55.886 --> 00:07:59.080
is reading a byte from

00:07:59.081 --> 00:08:01.570
an attacker controlled
register and then loading

00:08:01.571 --> 00:08:05.850
a cache line according
to this secret value,

00:08:05.851 --> 00:08:08.975
and cache line size is 64 bytes.

00:08:08.976 --> 00:08:10.860
So, in this gadget,

00:08:10.861 --> 00:08:14.770
we simply shifted left six times.

00:08:14.771 --> 00:08:18.020
So, variant three is specific to

00:08:18.021 --> 00:08:20.975
the kernel to user information
disclosure scenario.

00:08:20.976 --> 00:08:24.320
So, if these last three
instructions are

00:08:24.321 --> 00:08:27.840
executing speculatively
due to conditional branch

00:08:27.841 --> 00:08:29.750
mispredict, for example,

00:08:29.751 --> 00:08:34.010
what can happen is
that if we try to load

00:08:34.011 --> 00:08:38.040
it from kernel memory
and userland execution,

00:08:38.041 --> 00:08:42.170
speculative execution will
actually be able to retrieve

00:08:42.171 --> 00:08:45.319
that value and pass onto
subsequent instructions

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:47.480
before the exception
will be triggered,

00:08:47.481 --> 00:08:49.760
and so it can persist

00:08:49.761 --> 00:08:52.345
the results by loading a
cache line for example,

00:08:52.346 --> 00:08:56.115
as we saw it before
and with this variant,

00:08:56.116 --> 00:09:00.130
userland code execution is
able to read kernel memory.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.500
So now, what I'm going
to do is I'm going to

00:09:03.501 --> 00:09:06.080
create a taxonomy
of these attacks,

00:09:06.081 --> 00:09:08.120
and so we can
systematically go through

00:09:08.121 --> 00:09:09.560
the key components required

00:09:09.561 --> 00:09:12.230
and then move on to
the mitigations.

00:09:12.231 --> 00:09:14.840
All right. So there are

00:09:14.841 --> 00:09:16.310
four key components required for

00:09:16.311 --> 00:09:18.615
speculative execution
side channel.

00:09:18.616 --> 00:09:22.205
The first of which is a method
of gaining speculation.

00:09:22.206 --> 00:09:23.660
So, as we saw, that might be

00:09:23.661 --> 00:09:26.360
conditional branch
mispredict for example.

00:09:26.361 --> 00:09:29.865
Second thing we'll need
is a Window and gadget,

00:09:29.866 --> 00:09:34.730
and this is used to extend
how long speculation can run

00:09:34.731 --> 00:09:36.890
before the CPU realizes it was

00:09:36.891 --> 00:09:41.125
speculating with
an incorrect prediction value.

00:09:41.126 --> 00:09:44.570
The third thing we need is
a disclosure gadget to persist

00:09:44.571 --> 00:09:47.320
the results made during
speculative execution.

00:09:47.321 --> 00:09:48.790
So, as we saw,

00:09:48.791 --> 00:09:50.450
that might be
loading a cache line

00:09:50.451 --> 00:09:52.610
according to a private value.

00:09:52.611 --> 00:09:54.590
Finally, we need a way to observe

00:09:54.591 --> 00:09:57.510
those results to
determine, for example,

00:09:57.511 --> 00:09:59.330
which cache line was
loaded and from that,

00:09:59.331 --> 00:10:00.620
infer the secret that

00:10:00.621 --> 00:10:03.820
was loaded during
speculative execution.

00:10:03.821 --> 00:10:07.790
If any one of these four
parts are not present,

00:10:07.791 --> 00:10:10.130
the speculative execution
side channel will not

00:10:10.131 --> 00:10:13.550
be able to succeed.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:17.055
So, starting with
speculation techniques,

00:10:17.056 --> 00:10:21.445
we have the three from
the three variants reported.

00:10:21.446 --> 00:10:24.210
We have conditional
branch mispredicts.

00:10:24.211 --> 00:10:27.310
This doesn't have to
be bounds checked,

00:10:27.311 --> 00:10:29.885
this can be any
conditional branch.

00:10:29.886 --> 00:10:32.370
So, for example, type check could

00:10:32.371 --> 00:10:35.915
lead to speculative type
confusion if mispredicted.

00:10:35.916 --> 00:10:38.615
But the thing is,

00:10:38.616 --> 00:10:40.510
these conditional branches can be

00:10:40.511 --> 00:10:42.475
trained based on past behavior.

00:10:42.476 --> 00:10:45.920
So, we can make it
very likely that

00:10:45.921 --> 00:10:49.355
speculative execution will
take the conditional roots,

00:10:49.356 --> 00:10:53.545
the conditional path we
desire during speculation.

00:10:53.546 --> 00:10:57.085
Variant two was the indirect
branch misprediction.

00:10:57.086 --> 00:10:59.660
Similarly, as the CPU executes,

00:10:59.661 --> 00:11:01.830
it maintains what's
called the BTB,

00:11:01.831 --> 00:11:03.635
the Branch Target Buffer,

00:11:03.636 --> 00:11:05.675
which maintains a list of

00:11:05.676 --> 00:11:08.930
branch targets during execution,

00:11:08.931 --> 00:11:10.550
and speculative
execution will use

00:11:10.551 --> 00:11:14.840
this internal buffer to
predict where to jump.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:19.155
We can also collide
different entries,

00:11:19.156 --> 00:11:21.340
so we can have
two different addresses that

00:11:21.341 --> 00:11:24.200
point to the same
internal BTB entry.

00:11:24.201 --> 00:11:26.550
Finally, Meltdown, was whether

00:11:26.551 --> 00:11:31.485
CPU can perform, for example,

00:11:31.486 --> 00:11:33.180
a kernel load from userland

00:11:33.181 --> 00:11:35.230
and forward the result
of that onto

00:11:35.231 --> 00:11:36.935
subsequent microoperations

00:11:36.936 --> 00:11:40.230
before the permission fault
will be delivered.

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:44.740
So, now that we're able
to trigger speculation,

00:11:44.741 --> 00:11:46.455
we need a windowing gadget.

00:11:46.456 --> 00:11:48.700
Once again, this is required so

00:11:48.701 --> 00:11:53.190
that speculation can execute for

00:11:53.191 --> 00:11:55.215
long enough that we are able to

00:11:55.216 --> 00:11:58.350
persist the results by
reaching a disclosure gadget.

00:11:58.351 --> 00:12:00.340
So, the key point here is that

00:12:00.341 --> 00:12:02.985
windowing gadgets can
naturally occur in code,

00:12:02.986 --> 00:12:04.550
they can be something as simple

00:12:04.551 --> 00:12:07.920
as dependency chain of
arithmetic operations,

00:12:07.921 --> 00:12:10.360
for example, or more commonly

00:12:10.361 --> 00:12:14.050
even just forming
an uncached load.

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:18.520
So, with speculation running,

00:12:18.521 --> 00:12:19.960
we can now begin to see

00:12:19.961 --> 00:12:22.290
how a side channel can
be formed from this.

00:12:22.291 --> 00:12:25.090
So, a side channel has
three stages generally.

00:12:25.091 --> 00:12:26.630
The first is priming the system

00:12:26.631 --> 00:12:28.435
into a known initial state.

00:12:28.436 --> 00:12:30.470
The second is
triggering or waiting

00:12:30.471 --> 00:12:32.955
for some victim
activity to occur.

00:12:32.956 --> 00:12:37.290
Finally, an attacker would
need to observe whether

00:12:37.291 --> 00:12:39.470
the state changed to
infer information

00:12:39.471 --> 00:12:42.690
about what happened during
the victim activity.

00:12:42.691 --> 00:12:46.475
So, in the context of
speculative execution,

00:12:46.476 --> 00:12:50.320
the disclosure gadget
will typically be loading

00:12:50.321 --> 00:12:51.980
a cache line according to

00:12:51.981 --> 00:12:53.700
some secret value
which might have

00:12:53.701 --> 00:12:56.395
been read after
bounds, for example.

00:12:56.396 --> 00:12:59.875
So, for flushing
reload primitive,

00:12:59.876 --> 00:13:01.390
what an attacker
will do is they will

00:13:01.391 --> 00:13:03.655
first flush an array
of cache lines,

00:13:03.656 --> 00:13:05.680
the disclosure gadget
will then load

00:13:05.681 --> 00:13:07.900
one of those according
to a secret,

00:13:07.901 --> 00:13:10.640
and finally the disclosure

00:13:10.641 --> 00:13:13.005
primitive will time how
long it takes to load

00:13:13.006 --> 00:13:16.000
each of those cache lines
and whichever one's

00:13:16.001 --> 00:13:19.375
faster as likely to be
loaded into the cache,

00:13:19.376 --> 00:13:21.310
and from that we can infer what

00:13:21.311 --> 00:13:25.260
the secret value was during
speculative execution.

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:27.920
So, just to sum up again

00:13:27.921 --> 00:13:31.550
the four components of
a speculation attack.

00:13:31.551 --> 00:13:33.530
We have the
speculation primitive,

00:13:33.531 --> 00:13:35.300
the windowing gadget,

00:13:35.301 --> 00:13:38.420
the disclosure gadget and
the disclosure primitive.

00:13:38.421 --> 00:13:40.920
Once again, we need all
four of these to be

00:13:40.921 --> 00:13:44.200
able to leak information
through a side channel.

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:48.790
So, relevance to
software security.

00:13:48.791 --> 00:13:51.395
Variant three is specific to

00:13:51.396 --> 00:13:54.895
the kernel to user information
disclosure scenario,

00:13:54.896 --> 00:13:58.450
that's exception delivery,
but all the others

00:13:58.451 --> 00:14:02.910
generally apply universally
across the board and so,

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:06.450
we're going to need
mitigations for those.

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:10.680
So, now that we
understand exactly how

00:14:10.681 --> 00:14:13.800
speculative execution can lead
to a side channel attack,

00:14:13.801 --> 00:14:15.740
we can begin to go
into the mitigations

00:14:15.741 --> 00:14:18.120
that we can put in
place for these.

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:22.870
So, we have three tactics.

00:14:22.871 --> 00:14:25.515
The first is preventing
speculation techniques.

00:14:25.516 --> 00:14:27.500
Specifically, what we
mean by this is we

00:14:27.501 --> 00:14:29.605
want to prevent
unsafe speculation,

00:14:29.606 --> 00:14:31.590
where speculative execution can

00:14:31.591 --> 00:14:33.320
lead to a disclosure gadget.

00:14:33.321 --> 00:14:36.160
The second is removing
sensitive content from memory.

00:14:36.161 --> 00:14:38.150
So, this is limiting what

00:14:38.151 --> 00:14:41.510
speculative execution
will be able to read.

00:14:41.511 --> 00:14:44.035
This can eliminate
entire scenarios

00:14:44.036 --> 00:14:47.750
or simply reduce the risk
from certain scenarios.

00:14:47.751 --> 00:14:50.810
Finally, removing
observation channels.

00:14:50.811 --> 00:14:52.910
This is making it more difficult

00:14:52.911 --> 00:14:54.660
or even impossible
for an attacker to

00:14:54.661 --> 00:14:56.740
infer what changes were

00:14:56.741 --> 00:15:00.420
made to the cache state
during speculative execution.

00:15:00.421 --> 00:15:04.380
But once again, there's
no silver bullet.

00:15:04.381 --> 00:15:06.540
We require a combination
of different hardware,

00:15:06.541 --> 00:15:08.055
and software, and mitigations

00:15:08.056 --> 00:15:10.710
for each of
the scenarios present.

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:15.455
So, starting with preventing
speculation techniques.

00:15:15.456 --> 00:15:17.645
Once again, the goal
here is to prevent

00:15:17.646 --> 00:15:19.140
a speculation primitive from

00:15:19.141 --> 00:15:21.195
leading to a disclosure gadget.

00:15:21.196 --> 00:15:23.890
First thing we can do
is use some kind of

00:15:23.891 --> 00:15:26.295
serialization of the
instruction pipeline.

00:15:26.296 --> 00:15:29.974
So, on X86, we have
the LFENCE instruction,

00:15:29.975 --> 00:15:31.280
which has the neat property of

00:15:31.281 --> 00:15:33.360
acting as a speculation barrier.

00:15:33.361 --> 00:15:36.180
So, if we go back to variant one,

00:15:36.181 --> 00:15:42.175
we see this bounds check
on an untrusted index.

00:15:42.176 --> 00:15:44.620
What we can do is
insert an LFENCE as

00:15:44.621 --> 00:15:47.140
a speculation barrier
here after the check.

00:15:47.141 --> 00:15:49.550
What this will guarantee is

00:15:49.551 --> 00:15:53.540
that the subsequent two
array indexes will not

00:15:53.541 --> 00:16:01.320
be executed until speculation
has resolved to this point.

00:16:01.321 --> 00:16:04.000
So, this code will never

00:16:04.001 --> 00:16:07.560
execute speculatively with
untrusted index after bounds.

00:16:07.561 --> 00:16:09.540
Second thing we can do is have

00:16:09.541 --> 00:16:11.575
some kind of
implicit sterilization.

00:16:11.576 --> 00:16:13.770
So, this is forcing safe behavior

00:16:13.771 --> 00:16:16.720
down an architecturally
incorrect path.

00:16:16.721 --> 00:16:18.660
So, going back to a variant one,

00:16:18.661 --> 00:16:20.305
what we can do is,

00:16:20.306 --> 00:16:23.960
considering if the inner code

00:16:23.961 --> 00:16:27.095
executes even when
untrusted index is after bounds,

00:16:27.096 --> 00:16:29.880
we can use conditional
move instruction

00:16:29.881 --> 00:16:31.380
to set untrusted index to

00:16:31.381 --> 00:16:33.670
this zeroed register if it

00:16:33.671 --> 00:16:36.680
is greater than or
equal to length.

00:16:36.681 --> 00:16:38.490
What this will do is it will make

00:16:38.491 --> 00:16:41.495
the behavior of
speculative execution

00:16:41.496 --> 00:16:44.450
safe because it will
simply load zero

00:16:44.451 --> 00:16:48.080
from this buffer which is
going to be in bounds.

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:50.840
For doing this, we have

00:16:50.841 --> 00:16:54.150
the Qspectre command
line flag in Visual C++,

00:16:54.151 --> 00:16:56.235
and this will
automatically identify

00:16:56.236 --> 00:16:57.880
potentially vulnerable patterns

00:16:57.881 --> 00:17:00.745
and insert appropriate
serialization.

00:17:00.746 --> 00:17:05.390
Similarly, in Microsoft
Edge we have mitigations in

00:17:05.391 --> 00:17:08.350
the Chakra JavaScript
engine which inserts

00:17:08.351 --> 00:17:10.130
serialization to
prevent an attacker

00:17:10.131 --> 00:17:13.110
from being able to
construct these patents.

00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:17.745
Second thing we can do is
have some workload isolation.

00:17:17.746 --> 00:17:21.820
So, we talk about
the Branch Target Buffer.

00:17:21.821 --> 00:17:24.770
Typically, this
prediction, these kind of

00:17:24.771 --> 00:17:28.055
prediction states
issued either per core

00:17:28.056 --> 00:17:30.780
or per simultaneous multi-thread

00:17:30.781 --> 00:17:33.870
in the case of
simultaneous multithreading,

00:17:33.871 --> 00:17:36.025
such as Intel hyperthreading.

00:17:36.026 --> 00:17:38.290
So, what we can do is,

00:17:38.291 --> 00:17:39.550
in Hyper-V we can use

00:17:39.551 --> 00:17:42.360
CPU groups and many
routes to assign

00:17:42.361 --> 00:17:46.755
a certain core to
a particular guest,

00:17:46.756 --> 00:17:49.125
and then the others for the host.

00:17:49.126 --> 00:17:52.605
What this will do is,

00:17:52.606 --> 00:17:55.790
since the branch prediction state

00:17:55.791 --> 00:17:59.280
is not shared between
the host and the guest,

00:17:59.281 --> 00:18:01.470
a malicious guest
will have no way

00:18:01.471 --> 00:18:04.280
of colliding the branch
prediction state.

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:09.805
So, the next thing is

00:18:09.806 --> 00:18:12.710
the- with the recent

00:18:12.711 --> 00:18:15.804
microcode updates provided
by Intel and AMD,

00:18:15.805 --> 00:18:18.505
we have some new modal
specific registers,

00:18:18.506 --> 00:18:21.870
which can control
indirect branches.

00:18:21.871 --> 00:18:24.795
So, we have IBRS first of all,

00:18:24.796 --> 00:18:26.620
which essentially
acts as a way of

00:18:26.621 --> 00:18:30.850
allowing- of creating
two different privileges.

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:33.710
So, you can set IBRS

00:18:33.711 --> 00:18:35.650
to zero for the less
privileged state,

00:18:35.651 --> 00:18:38.595
and then on kernel entry
for example,

00:18:38.596 --> 00:18:40.115
you can set it to one,

00:18:40.116 --> 00:18:42.100
and this will create
the guarantee that

00:18:42.101 --> 00:18:44.020
the more privileged
state will not be

00:18:44.021 --> 00:18:45.560
able to be influenced by

00:18:45.561 --> 00:18:48.615
predictions made in
the less privileged state.

00:18:48.616 --> 00:18:51.680
The next thing we have is IBPB,

00:18:51.681 --> 00:18:53.625
which essentially allows us to

00:18:53.626 --> 00:18:56.445
flush the prediction state,

00:18:56.446 --> 00:18:58.530
and this can be used
when switching between

00:18:58.531 --> 00:19:01.405
different a hypervisor
contexts for example,

00:19:01.406 --> 00:19:03.680
to prevent different
contexts from

00:19:03.681 --> 00:19:06.510
poisoning each other's
prediction state.

00:19:06.511 --> 00:19:11.005
Finally, we have STIBP
which once again,

00:19:11.006 --> 00:19:14.135
certain prediction state
will be shared among

00:19:14.136 --> 00:19:17.845
two sibling hyper threads
on a single core.

00:19:17.846 --> 00:19:20.330
When we set STIBP to one,

00:19:20.331 --> 00:19:22.705
it just offers the guarantee that

00:19:22.706 --> 00:19:25.040
sibling hyper threads
won't be able

00:19:25.041 --> 00:19:27.855
to poison each other's
branch prediction state.

00:19:27.856 --> 00:19:29.690
All supported versions of

00:19:29.691 --> 00:19:32.890
Windows client make use
of these by default.

00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:37.540
The next thing we can do is use

00:19:37.541 --> 00:19:38.880
the final thing to prevent

00:19:38.881 --> 00:19:41.945
speculation techniques
we can do is,

00:19:41.946 --> 00:19:44.165
you safely speculated or

00:19:44.166 --> 00:19:46.430
non speculated indirect branches.

00:19:46.431 --> 00:19:48.775
So, on Intel CPUs,

00:19:48.776 --> 00:19:51.495
FAR JMP and FAR RET instructions,

00:19:51.496 --> 00:19:54.985
which are indirect jumps
which changed the segment,

00:19:54.986 --> 00:19:56.820
will not be predicted and

00:19:56.821 --> 00:19:59.715
so we can replace
indirect branches with these,

00:19:59.716 --> 00:20:03.925
and that will
prevent variant two.

00:20:03.926 --> 00:20:05.910
Similarly, for AMD we can use

00:20:05.911 --> 00:20:08.279
this elephant
sterilization instruction

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:10.445
which will guarantee
that the behavior

00:20:10.446 --> 00:20:13.070
is safe during speculation.

00:20:13.071 --> 00:20:15.270
Finally, we have
this proposal from Google for

00:20:15.271 --> 00:20:17.665
"retpoline" and this allows us,

00:20:17.666 --> 00:20:19.945
this acts as a way of allowing

00:20:19.946 --> 00:20:24.040
catching speculative execution
in an infinite loop,

00:20:24.041 --> 00:20:25.835
while the architectural route

00:20:25.836 --> 00:20:29.245
will form the indirect
jump as usual.

00:20:29.246 --> 00:20:32.685
For the Hypervisor and Windows
kernel we're exploring

00:20:32.686 --> 00:20:37.410
combination of these to make
best use of performance.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:42.985
For removing sensitive
content from memory,

00:20:42.986 --> 00:20:47.650
the goal is once again limit
entire attack scenarios,

00:20:47.651 --> 00:20:51.180
or just limit the risks
as best possible.

00:20:51.181 --> 00:20:53.390
So, the first thing we can do is

00:20:53.391 --> 00:20:56.095
have Hypervisor
address-space segregation.

00:20:56.096 --> 00:20:59.210
So, what this means is that

00:20:59.211 --> 00:21:00.980
the Hypervisor will
only ever mapped

00:21:00.981 --> 00:21:03.630
guest physical memory on
demand as it's needed,

00:21:03.631 --> 00:21:06.570
and as opposed to
historically where

00:21:06.571 --> 00:21:10.575
offers or guest memory
was always mapped,

00:21:10.576 --> 00:21:13.860
and what this means is that if

00:21:13.861 --> 00:21:15.510
a guest if a guest VM

00:21:15.511 --> 00:21:17.705
forms a hypercalls
into the Hypervisor,

00:21:17.706 --> 00:21:20.425
only its own guest
memory will be mapped,

00:21:20.426 --> 00:21:23.040
speculation in the Hypervisor
will not be able to

00:21:23.041 --> 00:21:26.720
read any memory of other guests.

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:30.205
The next thing we
have is KVA shadow.

00:21:30.206 --> 00:21:33.400
So, this applies specifically
to variant three.

00:21:33.401 --> 00:21:36.950
Previously the end
user mode execution

00:21:36.951 --> 00:21:39.055
we had the kernel page
table entries mapped,

00:21:39.056 --> 00:21:41.120
but just marked as inaccessible.

00:21:41.121 --> 00:21:43.110
What we do with KVA shadow is

00:21:43.111 --> 00:21:45.645
when transitioning between them,

00:21:45.646 --> 00:21:47.730
we ensure that the
user mode execution

00:21:47.731 --> 00:21:50.190
never has the kernel page
table entries mapped.

00:21:50.191 --> 00:21:53.320
What this means is
that speculative execution and

00:21:53.321 --> 00:21:56.805
user mode will not be able
to read kernel memory,

00:21:56.806 --> 00:22:00.130
because it's not
physically present.

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:04.100
All supported versions of Windows

00:22:04.101 --> 00:22:06.340
client make use of this,

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:09.980
and the final tactic we have

00:22:09.981 --> 00:22:12.400
is removing observation channels.

00:22:12.401 --> 00:22:14.680
So, once again
the goal here is to

00:22:14.681 --> 00:22:16.720
make it difficult
or impossible for

00:22:16.721 --> 00:22:18.745
an attacker to observe

00:22:18.746 --> 00:22:22.680
Changes made during
speculative execution.

00:22:22.681 --> 00:22:25.210
Best thing we can
do is we can map

00:22:25.211 --> 00:22:28.605
guest physical memory as
uncache when the Hypervisor.

00:22:28.606 --> 00:22:31.730
So, here we have
some system physical memory

00:22:31.731 --> 00:22:35.665
in the guest is still
mapped as write back cache,

00:22:35.666 --> 00:22:37.380
so there'll be no performance

00:22:37.381 --> 00:22:39.080
impact for the guest itself.

00:22:39.081 --> 00:22:42.120
Within the Hypervisor we
map it is uncacheable.

00:22:42.121 --> 00:22:45.030
What this means is that
a speculative execution and

00:22:45.031 --> 00:22:48.400
the Hypervisor attempts
to perform a load.

00:22:48.401 --> 00:22:51.085
Since it's marked as
uncacheable memory,

00:22:51.086 --> 00:22:55.765
it will never bring that
into the cache and this acts

00:22:55.766 --> 00:23:02.890
as a generic mitigation for
host-guest flush and reload,

00:23:02.891 --> 00:23:05.330
which requires
shared cache lines.

00:23:05.331 --> 00:23:08.630
The next thing we can do
is we can ensure that

00:23:08.631 --> 00:23:11.916
we never share any physical
memory between guests.

00:23:11.917 --> 00:23:17.260
So, similarly, we
want to prevent flush

00:23:17.261 --> 00:23:18.840
and reload between guests,

00:23:18.841 --> 00:23:20.540
so we just ensure that

00:23:20.541 --> 00:23:22.200
each guest has its own copy of

00:23:22.201 --> 00:23:23.730
everything in physical memory,

00:23:23.731 --> 00:23:28.130
and so they can never influence
each other's cache state.

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:31.250
Final thing we can do, is we can

00:23:31.251 --> 00:23:33.190
decrease browser
time of precision.

00:23:33.191 --> 00:23:34.540
So, there was this API

00:23:34.541 --> 00:23:37.225
performance.now accessible
from JavaScript.

00:23:37.226 --> 00:23:40.650
Which could be used to time
single load and determine

00:23:40.651 --> 00:23:42.670
whether that memory that

00:23:42.671 --> 00:23:45.140
it was loading was
in the cache or not.

00:23:45.141 --> 00:23:48.560
What we do is we decreased
the precision of this and

00:23:48.561 --> 00:23:51.565
add random jitter to prevent
clock edging techniques.

00:23:51.566 --> 00:23:53.735
So, it is now impossible
for an attacker

00:23:53.736 --> 00:23:55.905
to infer whether or not

00:23:55.906 --> 00:24:01.120
a single load is in the
cache or not. All right.

00:24:01.121 --> 00:24:05.330
So, closing remarks I just
want to sum up once again,

00:24:05.331 --> 00:24:06.760
that there's no silver bullet.

00:24:06.761 --> 00:24:09.080
For each of the scenarios
present we're going to

00:24:09.081 --> 00:24:12.360
require a different
combination of mitigations.

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:17.020
Once again going
over the variants.

00:24:17.021 --> 00:24:19.320
They're all hardware
vulnerabilities,

00:24:19.321 --> 00:24:22.230
variant one is going to
require software changes.

00:24:22.231 --> 00:24:23.805
So, that might be adding

00:24:23.806 --> 00:24:26.670
appropriate serialization
by the compiler.

00:24:26.671 --> 00:24:29.500
Variant two, is
mitigated by the OS

00:24:29.501 --> 00:24:32.845
making use of the indirect
branch controls as we saw.

00:24:32.846 --> 00:24:35.780
Finally, variant three,
this was the kernel to

00:24:35.781 --> 00:24:38.585
user information
disclosure meltdown,

00:24:38.586 --> 00:24:42.220
and that it's completely
mitigated now with KVA shadow.

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:44.800
All right, so, since
then we've been

00:24:44.801 --> 00:24:46.745
made aware of some new variants,

00:24:46.746 --> 00:24:48.720
we have Speculative Store Bypass,

00:24:48.721 --> 00:24:50.215
which made use of

00:24:50.216 --> 00:24:53.130
miss-predicting data
dependencies between

00:24:53.131 --> 00:24:55.190
load and store instructions.

00:24:55.191 --> 00:24:57.850
This can be mitigated
by identifying

00:24:57.851 --> 00:24:58.920
vulnerable code packing and

00:24:58.921 --> 00:25:02.530
inserting instruction
sterilization once again.

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:06.885
It can also be
mitigated by disabling

00:25:06.886 --> 00:25:10.340
this memory disambiguation
optimization by the CPU.

00:25:10.341 --> 00:25:12.170
But this is not done by default

00:25:12.171 --> 00:25:13.895
because there are currently

00:25:13.896 --> 00:25:15.790
no known exploitable patterns in

00:25:15.791 --> 00:25:18.315
Windows code. Second thing.

00:25:18.316 --> 00:25:21.955
Second variant is lazy
floating-point state restore.

00:25:21.956 --> 00:25:23.720
So, this was
an optimization made by

00:25:23.721 --> 00:25:25.740
the Operating System when

00:25:25.741 --> 00:25:28.175
context switching
between processes,

00:25:28.176 --> 00:25:30.610
the floating point
registers would not be

00:25:30.611 --> 00:25:33.745
copied they would simply
be marked as inaccessible,

00:25:33.746 --> 00:25:35.555
and then the first time they

00:25:35.556 --> 00:25:38.475
made use of this will
trigger an exception,

00:25:38.476 --> 00:25:41.255
where the kernel
would restore them.

00:25:41.256 --> 00:25:43.630
To mitigate this we just disable

00:25:43.631 --> 00:25:44.900
this optimization and

00:25:44.901 --> 00:25:47.560
the floating point registers
are always copied.

00:25:47.561 --> 00:25:50.295
Then we have Bound
Check Bypass Store,

00:25:50.296 --> 00:25:52.785
this was simulated variant one,

00:25:52.786 --> 00:25:54.040
if we have a conditional branch

00:25:54.041 --> 00:25:56.755
miss-predicting leading to
an out of bounds store.

00:25:56.756 --> 00:25:59.845
If that store corrupts
an indirect branch target,

00:25:59.846 --> 00:26:03.385
that can leave an attacker
with arbitrary speculation,

00:26:03.386 --> 00:26:04.890
as an arbitrary address.

00:26:04.891 --> 00:26:06.730
The way we mitigate
that is just by

00:26:06.731 --> 00:26:09.115
adding speculation
barriers again,

00:26:09.116 --> 00:26:11.360
similar to variant one.

00:26:11.361 --> 00:26:13.370
Finally we have NetSpectre,

00:26:13.371 --> 00:26:16.975
which is the first speculative
execution side channel

00:26:16.976 --> 00:26:18.525
not using the cache.

00:26:18.526 --> 00:26:21.320
That was timing
the AVX instructions.

00:26:21.321 --> 00:26:23.500
The mitigation for that
is once again just using

00:26:23.501 --> 00:26:26.975
our speculation barrier,
and vulnerable patterns.

00:26:26.976 --> 00:26:28.750
We expect first

00:26:28.751 --> 00:26:31.110
speculative execution side
channel vulnerabilities

00:26:31.111 --> 00:26:33.545
to be a continuing
source of research,

00:26:33.546 --> 00:26:34.800
and so we have

00:26:34.801 --> 00:26:37.515
our Speculative Execution
Side Channel Bounty,

00:26:37.516 --> 00:26:41.895
max payout is 250k for
new variants.

00:26:41.896 --> 00:26:45.260
We also have on technocrats and

00:26:45.261 --> 00:26:47.100
blogs with more
technical analysis

00:26:47.101 --> 00:26:48.445
of any of the variants,

00:26:48.446 --> 00:26:50.875
as well as develop the guidance.

00:26:50.876 --> 00:26:53.885
All right, so thanks
for listening,

00:26:53.886 --> 00:26:58.230
and thank you to everyone
who has worked on this.

00:26:58.231 --> 00:27:04.330
It's been a tremendous
undertaking. Thank you.

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:13.030
&gt;&gt; Questions?

00:27:18.300 --> 00:27:21.210
&gt;&gt; So, I'm going to ask

00:27:21.211 --> 00:27:23.450
a question if- Oh Chris,
sorry right there.

00:27:23.451 --> 00:27:26.000
Okay, yeah, yeah. Chris.

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:30.690
&gt;&gt; How expensive are
the various mitigations?

00:27:30.691 --> 00:27:31.520
&gt;&gt; Sorry?

00:27:31.521 --> 00:27:34.250
&gt;&gt;How expensive are
the various mitigations?

00:27:34.251 --> 00:27:36.390
&gt;&gt; So that depends on

00:27:36.391 --> 00:27:38.505
the operating system itself

00:27:38.506 --> 00:27:41.325
and your how recent your CPU is.

00:27:41.326 --> 00:27:44.350
So from our analysis,

00:27:44.351 --> 00:27:48.100
the latest Windows 10
with modern processor

00:27:48.101 --> 00:27:56.040
from within two years
is less than 10%.

00:27:56.110 --> 00:27:59.230
For older operating systems
such as Windows

00:27:59.231 --> 00:28:02.150
7 where there are
some differences,

00:28:02.151 --> 00:28:05.130
for example the kernel.

00:28:05.131 --> 00:28:08.470
The kernel does all the font

00:28:08.471 --> 00:28:11.545
pausing there is more kernel
to user transition,

00:28:11.546 --> 00:28:13.275
that's slightly more expensive,

00:28:13.276 --> 00:28:19.290
but yeah for the latest Windows
10 with modern processor,

00:28:19.291 --> 00:28:22.120
the performance impact is

00:28:22.121 --> 00:28:25.790
not that noticeable,
it's single-digit.

00:28:25.791 --> 00:28:29.355
There's more analysis
on our website,

00:28:29.356 --> 00:28:31.720
if you want more details.

00:28:32.790 --> 00:28:37.090
&gt;&gt; So you told us about this
complex grid of mitigations,

00:28:37.091 --> 00:28:39.220
where it seems like
it's hard to tell

00:28:39.221 --> 00:28:41.400
if that you're done filling out

00:28:41.401 --> 00:28:42.780
that grid and coming up with

00:28:42.781 --> 00:28:45.570
all the relevant advice to
avoid security problems.

00:28:45.571 --> 00:28:48.930
I wonder how much of this
you think is coming about

00:28:48.931 --> 00:28:52.720
because the hardware
is close to us,

00:28:52.721 --> 00:28:55.190
and we can't even
principle do an end to

00:28:55.191 --> 00:28:58.370
end foundational
analysis of security.

00:28:58.371 --> 00:28:59.970
Could this be some motivation for

00:28:59.971 --> 00:29:02.385
adopting more
open source hardware?

00:29:02.386 --> 00:29:06.350
&gt;&gt; Good question. So, we have
been working with Intel.

00:29:06.351 --> 00:29:09.745
We have a non-disclosure
agreement with them.

00:29:09.746 --> 00:29:12.030
So we have some information,

00:29:12.031 --> 00:29:15.910
but yeah absolutely some
details are not known to us.

00:29:15.911 --> 00:29:20.330
That's why we rely on
our bounty part partly

00:29:20.331 --> 00:29:23.820
for more information
to be made available

00:29:23.821 --> 00:29:28.100
to us and we'll react
as best we can.

00:29:28.101 --> 00:29:29.925
Thank you for your question.

00:29:29.926 --> 00:29:31.150
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:39.000
&gt;&gt; I also have questions

00:29:39.001 --> 00:29:41.260
along the lines like
sort of similar.

00:29:41.261 --> 00:29:44.740
My question was
a very interviewer question.

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:50.195
Right. I mean, I think
it's not unlikely

00:29:50.196 --> 00:29:52.030
that within the next year
or so there's going to be

00:29:52.031 --> 00:29:55.480
yet another
speculative execution,

00:29:55.481 --> 00:29:58.300
way of doing special
speculative execution to

00:29:58.301 --> 00:30:00.875
exploit kernels. Right so-

00:30:00.876 --> 00:30:01.120
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:30:01.121 --> 00:30:05.010
&gt;&gt; -and it looks like

00:30:05.011 --> 00:30:09.620
the process we have in place
right now is, hopefully,

00:30:09.621 --> 00:30:10.800
they're not going
to release it to

00:30:10.801 --> 00:30:12.670
the public whoever
discovered this and

00:30:12.671 --> 00:30:14.010
hopefully gives time to

00:30:14.011 --> 00:30:15.720
the Microsofts and
Googles of the world

00:30:15.721 --> 00:30:17.460
to go patch the kernel sort of

00:30:17.461 --> 00:30:20.310
in the opposite of the world
to go patch the kernels.

00:30:20.920 --> 00:30:24.025
It seems pretty sad to me.

00:30:24.026 --> 00:30:26.220
I don't know. It seems like
a sad state of affair.

00:30:26.221 --> 00:30:29.630
So, is there any
investment into having

00:30:29.631 --> 00:30:34.370
a more principled solution
to these things like you are

00:30:34.371 --> 00:30:37.240
mentioning sort of disclosing

00:30:37.241 --> 00:30:40.160
open source,
open sourcing hardware.

00:30:40.161 --> 00:30:42.880
It's on some way it's very
nice, on the other hand,

00:30:42.881 --> 00:30:46.760
like Intel is probably not
going to do that anytime soon.

00:30:46.761 --> 00:30:49.410
So it just sort of feels
to me that we're kind of

00:30:49.411 --> 00:30:53.195
stuck with a bad situation
on our hands.

00:30:53.196 --> 00:30:56.400
&gt;&gt; So the mitigations we
have in place are designed

00:30:56.401 --> 00:31:00.580
to not only mitigate
existing vectors,

00:31:00.581 --> 00:31:02.485
but also to proactively

00:31:02.486 --> 00:31:06.565
consider reducing the attack
surface as much as possible.

00:31:06.566 --> 00:31:10.830
So as we saw with
the indirect branch controls,

00:31:10.831 --> 00:31:15.840
we can flush the prediction
state regularly as well

00:31:15.841 --> 00:31:20.900
as others but yeah,

00:31:20.901 --> 00:31:23.050
we have our own
internal research,

00:31:23.051 --> 00:31:25.110
it's ongoing and we'll

00:31:25.111 --> 00:31:27.940
continue to mitigate
it as best possible.

00:31:27.941 --> 00:31:32.640
Thanks.

00:31:32.641 --> 00:31:34.430
&gt;&gt; So I have another question.

00:31:34.431 --> 00:31:38.310
So it seems like a bunch of
the mitigations that you

00:31:38.311 --> 00:31:42.490
mentioned require, for instance,

00:31:42.491 --> 00:31:45.190
modifying binaries so that

00:31:45.191 --> 00:31:47.640
certain instruction
sequences have

00:31:47.641 --> 00:31:52.520
the appropriate
protections yet in them.

00:31:52.540 --> 00:32:01.190
But presumably, there are
situations where customers who

00:32:01.191 --> 00:32:03.770
are potentially even
attackers are allowed

00:32:03.771 --> 00:32:06.590
to load their own code

00:32:06.591 --> 00:32:09.470
that they've compiled
or written in assembly.

00:32:09.471 --> 00:32:14.530
So, in terms of mitigations,

00:32:14.531 --> 00:32:18.030
is there been any thought

00:32:18.031 --> 00:32:19.730
into what can be

00:32:19.731 --> 00:32:22.330
done to address
those kinds of situations?

00:32:22.331 --> 00:32:23.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah, very good question.

00:32:23.931 --> 00:32:26.480
So, you mentioned situations

00:32:26.481 --> 00:32:29.440
where an attacker is able
to supply their own code.

00:32:29.441 --> 00:32:32.690
So for example, one of
those scenarios is in

00:32:32.691 --> 00:32:34.380
the browser where we're

00:32:34.381 --> 00:32:37.500
running arbitrary JavaScript
from an attacker.

00:32:37.670 --> 00:32:40.230
As I mentioned, Chakra,

00:32:40.231 --> 00:32:41.540
the JavaScript engine of Edge,

00:32:41.541 --> 00:32:44.300
has its own heuristics to
detect patterns such as

00:32:44.301 --> 00:32:48.160
variant one and it's
an appropriate serialization.

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:50.865
More generally under that though,

00:32:50.866 --> 00:32:54.290
you mentioned that
within Microsoft,

00:32:54.291 --> 00:32:57.080
we have a lot of code and

00:32:57.081 --> 00:33:00.940
rebuilding the whole world
isn't always possible.

00:33:00.941 --> 00:33:04.140
It's why we have a
combination of mitigations

00:33:04.141 --> 00:33:08.150
just aiming to mitigate the
problem as best as possible.

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:16.030
Yeah. Also for
hypervisor scenario,

00:33:16.031 --> 00:33:18.045
we have mitigations from

00:33:18.046 --> 00:33:20.355
guest to guest as I talked about.

00:33:20.356 --> 00:33:24.280
So, really, it's just
limiting the severity

00:33:24.281 --> 00:33:28.420
of the attacks and yeah,

00:33:28.421 --> 00:33:30.590
doing as much as we can.

00:33:30.750 --> 00:33:33.255
Hopefully that answered
your question.

00:33:33.256 --> 00:33:34.630
&gt;&gt; I guess I'm looking for

00:33:34.631 --> 00:33:38.610
slightly higher level answer
in the following sense.

00:33:38.611 --> 00:33:42.230
Does the current state of
affairs keep you up at night?

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:43.680
&gt;&gt; So, I think-

00:33:43.681 --> 00:33:45.580
&gt;&gt; Or you're relatively
happy with the mitigations?

00:33:45.581 --> 00:33:46.290
&gt;&gt; I think at the moment

00:33:46.291 --> 00:33:47.960
the mitigations
are pretty strong.

00:33:47.961 --> 00:33:49.510
Once again, we have our bounty.

00:33:49.511 --> 00:33:54.260
So, if real-world attacks
are submitted to us they

00:33:54.261 --> 00:33:56.220
might be eligible for
a bounty and we'll try

00:33:56.221 --> 00:33:59.160
to mitigate them as
best as possible.

00:33:59.161 --> 00:34:01.105
But once again,
it's a continuing,

00:34:01.106 --> 00:34:03.610
ongoing matter of research.

00:34:03.611 --> 00:34:06.115
So, at the moment,

00:34:06.116 --> 00:34:07.645
I think we're well protected,

00:34:07.646 --> 00:34:09.570
but we're ready to

00:34:09.571 --> 00:34:13.110
react if more information
becomes available to us.

00:34:14.130 --> 00:34:17.785
&gt;&gt; Are there
any no public instances

00:34:17.786 --> 00:34:19.680
of Meltdown or Spectre

00:34:19.681 --> 00:34:22.270
attacks that have
been rather than

00:34:22.271 --> 00:34:23.700
proof of concepts researchers

00:34:23.701 --> 00:34:26.060
are showing, "Look
we can do this."

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:29.230
&gt;&gt; To my knowledge, we have-.

00:34:29.231 --> 00:34:30.950
&gt;&gt; My question is not
just about Microsoft,

00:34:30.951 --> 00:34:33.640
it's just in general like
if you happen to know.

00:34:33.641 --> 00:34:36.720
&gt;&gt; Yeah. To my knowledge, I
don't think these attacks are

00:34:36.721 --> 00:34:40.170
being actively used in
the world right now,

00:34:40.171 --> 00:34:42.610
but what we see from

00:34:42.611 --> 00:34:45.010
our detections is
only a small sample.

00:34:45.011 --> 00:34:47.220
So, I think it's

00:34:47.221 --> 00:34:49.600
possible in the future
they might be used by

00:34:49.601 --> 00:34:52.600
attackers in real world scenarios

00:34:52.601 --> 00:34:55.980
but I can't comment
