00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:07.840
&gt;&gt; Any other
questions? Okay, let's

00:35:07.841 --> 00:35:09.475
thank the speaker then.

00:35:09.476 --> 00:35:11.090
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:35:16.710 --> 00:35:20.459
&gt;&gt; Okay. Our next speaker is

00:35:20.460 --> 00:35:24.510
Professor Margaret Martonosi
from Princeton University,

00:35:24.511 --> 00:35:27.790
and her research interests
are computer architecture and

00:35:27.791 --> 00:35:29.620
mobile computing with
a particular focus

00:35:29.621 --> 00:35:31.590
on power efficient systems.

00:35:31.591 --> 00:35:33.585
Her current research is focusing

00:35:33.586 --> 00:35:36.610
on hardware software interface

00:35:36.611 --> 00:35:39.420
approaches to managing
heterogeneous parallelism

00:35:39.421 --> 00:35:41.860
and power performance
trade offs in systems,

00:35:41.861 --> 00:35:44.020
ranging all the way
from smart phones,

00:35:44.021 --> 00:35:46.490
all the way up to
large scale data centers.

00:35:46.491 --> 00:35:51.350
Professor Martonosi is
a fellow of IEEE and ACM,

00:35:51.351 --> 00:35:53.230
and she's won numerous awards,

00:35:53.231 --> 00:35:54.915
I'll just mentioned two.

00:35:54.916 --> 00:35:57.310
In 2015, she won the ISCA Long

00:35:57.311 --> 00:35:59.385
Term Influential Paper Award,

00:35:59.386 --> 00:36:02.650
and in 2017,
the ACM SIGMOBILE Test

00:36:02.651 --> 00:36:05.100
of Time award. Take
it away Margaret.

00:36:05.101 --> 00:36:07.410
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
Good morning, everyone.

00:36:07.411 --> 00:36:10.120
So, this follows nicely from

00:36:10.121 --> 00:36:11.660
the wonderful
previous talk because

00:36:11.661 --> 00:36:14.140
the previous talks gave
us the state of play,

00:36:14.141 --> 00:36:15.770
and what I'm going to try to give

00:36:15.771 --> 00:36:18.460
here is some thoughts that relate

00:36:18.461 --> 00:36:19.975
a bit to your question about

00:36:19.976 --> 00:36:22.860
our attempt at a principal
waiting forward.

00:36:22.861 --> 00:36:25.600
So, parts of the story

00:36:25.601 --> 00:36:27.650
do start in January
was spectrum meltdown,

00:36:27.651 --> 00:36:30.035
but a lot of the story
starts much earlier.

00:36:30.036 --> 00:36:32.110
I'm going to take you

00:36:32.111 --> 00:36:35.800
through the flow from
our earlier work,

00:36:35.801 --> 00:36:38.740
Verifying Memory Consistency
Models to our current work,

00:36:38.741 --> 00:36:41.800
Synthesizing Security
Exploits Automatically.

00:36:41.801 --> 00:36:44.075
So, we started in

00:36:44.076 --> 00:36:46.505
about five years ago
with a simple goal.

00:36:46.506 --> 00:36:48.800
Memory Consistency
Models have to do

00:36:48.801 --> 00:36:50.990
with enforcing the ordering of

00:36:50.991 --> 00:36:55.100
memory events in hardware
software systems

00:36:55.101 --> 00:36:57.110
in a well-specified way,

00:36:57.111 --> 00:36:59.270
and we had the goal of saying for

00:36:59.271 --> 00:37:02.620
a particular part of the Memory
Consistency Model namely,

00:37:02.621 --> 00:37:05.740
from the specification
given by the ISA to

00:37:05.741 --> 00:37:09.435
a particular implementation
in hardware, is that correct?

00:37:09.436 --> 00:37:11.930
Does that pipeline
correctly implement,

00:37:11.931 --> 00:37:14.995
say Intel's total store
order memory model,

00:37:14.996 --> 00:37:17.835
or arms weaker memory
model, and so forth?

00:37:17.836 --> 00:37:19.400
We did that based on

00:37:19.401 --> 00:37:20.960
an axiomatic approach that

00:37:20.961 --> 00:37:23.505
I'll go through it in
a little bit of detail.

00:37:23.506 --> 00:37:26.890
After that, we
recognize that actually

00:37:26.891 --> 00:37:30.660
that localized view at
just the microarchitecture

00:37:30.661 --> 00:37:34.325
compared to the ISA was
insufficient in many cases

00:37:34.326 --> 00:37:37.420
because there are
so many other parts

00:37:37.421 --> 00:37:39.915
of the Memory Consistency
model landscape.

00:37:39.916 --> 00:37:42.830
In particular, high level
languages have a memory model.

00:37:42.831 --> 00:37:45.460
C specifies a memory model with

00:37:45.461 --> 00:37:48.595
atomics and sequential
consistency and so forth.

00:37:48.596 --> 00:37:51.210
The compiler and the OS
play a role as well because

00:37:51.211 --> 00:37:54.050
the compiler maps from
those C constructs down

00:37:54.051 --> 00:37:57.260
to assembly language and
the OS manages virtual to

00:37:57.261 --> 00:37:59.240
physical address
translations that also

00:37:59.241 --> 00:38:01.850
have a role to play in
Memory Consistency Models.

00:38:01.851 --> 00:38:04.545
Then lastly, the
microarchitecture

00:38:04.546 --> 00:38:07.705
specified as a pipeline is
only a piece of the puzzle,

00:38:07.706 --> 00:38:08.910
because there's the full

00:38:08.911 --> 00:38:11.165
coherent memory hierarchy
to worry about,

00:38:11.166 --> 00:38:13.390
and there's also the fact
that eventually this gets

00:38:13.391 --> 00:38:15.450
mapped down to Verilog
or something like that,

00:38:15.451 --> 00:38:16.675
and we need to make
sure that that,

00:38:16.676 --> 00:38:20.645
too, represents
a correct implementation.

00:38:20.646 --> 00:38:23.190
So, over the course of
the past five years,

00:38:23.191 --> 00:38:26.090
we've developed a suite
of tools that addressed

00:38:26.091 --> 00:38:27.740
this with this general philosophy

00:38:27.741 --> 00:38:29.750
being unified across all of them.

00:38:29.751 --> 00:38:33.970
The basic approach that we
use across all of them is to

00:38:33.971 --> 00:38:36.090
have an axiomatic
specification that's

00:38:36.091 --> 00:38:39.040
given alongside
the implementation,

00:38:39.041 --> 00:38:41.490
and that can be
automatically translated

00:38:41.491 --> 00:38:43.870
into a set of
Happens-Before graphs.

00:38:43.871 --> 00:38:45.230
Now, Happens-Before
graphs have been

00:38:45.231 --> 00:38:48.380
used by higher level compiler

00:38:48.381 --> 00:38:50.255
and software people for a while,

00:38:50.256 --> 00:38:52.200
where the nodes in them are

00:38:52.201 --> 00:38:55.020
typically instructions
or coarser granularity.

00:38:55.021 --> 00:38:56.980
We're taking those
Happens-Before graphs

00:38:56.981 --> 00:38:58.400
down so the microarchitecture

00:38:58.401 --> 00:38:59.960
and the implementation
level where they

00:38:59.961 --> 00:39:02.580
map more to hardware features.

00:39:02.581 --> 00:39:05.580
The key thing that we're
doing is we're saying,

00:39:05.581 --> 00:39:06.890
if there's an axiom that says

00:39:06.891 --> 00:39:08.635
that A must happen before B,

00:39:08.636 --> 00:39:10.710
then we can draw an edge for it;

00:39:10.711 --> 00:39:12.240
if there's another axiom that

00:39:12.241 --> 00:39:13.580
says if B must happen before C,

00:39:13.581 --> 00:39:14.660
we can draw an edge for it;

00:39:14.661 --> 00:39:16.420
if there's an axiom that
says that A must happen

00:39:16.421 --> 00:39:18.360
before A, we can
draw an edge for it;

00:39:18.361 --> 00:39:21.960
and in fact we can enumerate
this effectively across

00:39:21.961 --> 00:39:24.190
all possible orderings for

00:39:24.191 --> 00:39:27.200
the software running on
a given hardware implementation,

00:39:27.201 --> 00:39:29.360
and so that's why I
show multiple layers

00:39:29.361 --> 00:39:31.115
of these Happens- Before graphs.

00:39:31.116 --> 00:39:34.600
The key thing is that
if A happens before,

00:39:34.601 --> 00:39:35.820
B happens before C,

00:39:35.821 --> 00:39:37.780
and C happens before
A, and that's a cycle.

00:39:37.781 --> 00:39:38.890
That's saying that A is happening

00:39:38.891 --> 00:39:42.025
before itself,
physically impossible.

00:39:42.026 --> 00:39:44.270
So, every time we can show that

00:39:44.271 --> 00:39:46.660
a particular Happens-Before
Graph is cyclic,

00:39:46.661 --> 00:39:49.380
we can show that that is
a physically unobservable event;

00:39:49.381 --> 00:39:50.730
it will not happen.

00:39:50.731 --> 00:39:52.250
So, if there's
something that we're

00:39:52.251 --> 00:39:54.130
verifying that
should be forbidden,

00:39:54.131 --> 00:39:55.470
it should never happen,

00:39:55.471 --> 00:39:58.170
we need to ensure that
every possible interleaving,

00:39:58.171 --> 00:40:01.095
every possible Happens-Before
Graph is cyclic,

00:40:01.096 --> 00:40:03.490
and so that's the secret sauce

00:40:03.491 --> 00:40:05.345
of all of these tools
up and down.

00:40:05.346 --> 00:40:07.820
The key thing that's
relevant here is to

00:40:07.821 --> 00:40:10.645
recognize that
the same sorts of event

00:40:10.646 --> 00:40:13.025
ordering through memory issues

00:40:13.026 --> 00:40:16.550
that make a Memory Consistency
Model correct or incorrect

00:40:16.551 --> 00:40:19.620
also play intricately
in the space of

00:40:19.621 --> 00:40:24.140
these side channel attacks
that we just heard about,

00:40:24.141 --> 00:40:26.350
because the ordering in which you

00:40:26.351 --> 00:40:28.595
access memory is
a key part of it.

00:40:28.596 --> 00:40:31.140
So, over the past two years,

00:40:31.141 --> 00:40:33.110
we did this sort of transition

00:40:33.111 --> 00:40:36.835
from Memory Consistency Models
into the Security Space.

00:40:36.836 --> 00:40:38.820
So, first I want to
tell you a little bit

00:40:38.821 --> 00:40:40.370
about these Axiomatic Models.

00:40:40.371 --> 00:40:43.665
Here's a sort of a simple view
of a dual-core processor.

00:40:43.666 --> 00:40:46.440
Five stage pipelines;
fetch, decode, execute.

00:40:46.441 --> 00:40:48.170
Kind of like your
architecture class for

00:40:48.171 --> 00:40:51.905
undergrad along with some sort
of Coherence Protocol,

00:40:51.906 --> 00:40:55.065
Single Writer Multiple
Reader, and so forth.

00:40:55.066 --> 00:40:58.400
We can take that and we
can ask the designer,

00:40:58.401 --> 00:40:59.980
or we can help automate

00:40:59.981 --> 00:41:03.620
the process of expressing
that as a set of axioms,

00:41:03.621 --> 00:41:05.955
and I've shown
a very simple case here,

00:41:05.956 --> 00:41:07.740
and just two of the axiom.

00:41:07.741 --> 00:41:11.500
So in this case, the top
half of this box is

00:41:11.501 --> 00:41:13.790
an axiom written in
our domain-specific language

00:41:13.791 --> 00:41:16.120
called muSpec that
basically says,

00:41:16.121 --> 00:41:17.995
instructions are
fetched in order.

00:41:17.996 --> 00:41:23.505
Okay. The second half is for
this very simple processor,

00:41:23.506 --> 00:41:25.370
a very simple axiom that says,

00:41:25.371 --> 00:41:27.115
if instructions are
fetched in order,

00:41:27.116 --> 00:41:29.045
they will also be
executed in an order.

00:41:29.046 --> 00:41:31.490
That's all. So, this is

00:41:31.491 --> 00:41:33.890
very simple but we
have actually built

00:41:33.891 --> 00:41:35.910
up axiomatic specifications for

00:41:35.911 --> 00:41:38.970
processors as complex
as Intel Sandy Bridge,

00:41:38.971 --> 00:41:40.610
including the virtual to

00:41:40.611 --> 00:41:45.455
physical address
translation issues

00:41:45.456 --> 00:41:46.680
in which we have parts of

00:41:46.681 --> 00:41:48.720
the specification that
correspond to hardware,

00:41:48.721 --> 00:41:50.550
and other parts of
the specification

00:41:50.551 --> 00:41:52.190
that correspond to
axioms that are

00:41:52.191 --> 00:41:54.195
actually enforced by orderings

00:41:54.196 --> 00:41:56.030
done by the operating system.

00:41:56.031 --> 00:41:58.340
These axioms can be composed so

00:41:58.341 --> 00:42:02.259
that axioms can be written
by an OS specialist,

00:42:02.260 --> 00:42:03.875
and the hardware axioms
can be written by

00:42:03.876 --> 00:42:07.090
a hardware specialist and we
can put the two together.

00:42:07.091 --> 00:42:09.310
So as I said, we have

00:42:09.311 --> 00:42:14.270
a process of effectively,
exhaustively,

00:42:14.271 --> 00:42:15.800
but we're using SMT solver,

00:42:15.801 --> 00:42:17.990
so we aren't sort of stupidly

00:42:17.991 --> 00:42:21.700
exhaustively enumerating
all possible interleadings.

00:42:21.701 --> 00:42:24.090
So, what you can see there
are a whole bunch of

00:42:24.091 --> 00:42:26.515
Happens-Before Graph
starting to be enumerated.

00:42:26.516 --> 00:42:28.760
Each of the nodes in one of

00:42:28.761 --> 00:42:32.060
those columns corresponds
to one stage in a pipeline.

00:42:32.061 --> 00:42:35.250
This is for a microarchitecture
level consideration.

00:42:35.251 --> 00:42:36.785
So as you go down,

00:42:36.786 --> 00:42:37.900
one of those columns,

00:42:37.901 --> 00:42:39.140
that's an instruction
going through

00:42:39.141 --> 00:42:41.090
fetch-decode-execute
and perhaps a

00:42:41.091 --> 00:42:43.035
memory hierarchy stages as well.

00:42:43.036 --> 00:42:47.450
The different columns in
each one of those boxes,

00:42:47.451 --> 00:42:48.570
the different columns here

00:42:48.571 --> 00:42:50.855
correspond to
different instructions,

00:42:50.856 --> 00:42:53.710
and every arrow that's
drawn is drawn based on

00:42:53.711 --> 00:42:56.300
some axiom that we learn
from the specification.

00:42:56.301 --> 00:42:57.650
Nothing is assumed, so we don't

00:42:57.651 --> 00:42:59.360
assume program order
or anything like that,

00:42:59.361 --> 00:43:02.385
we check everything about
the microarchitecture.

00:43:02.386 --> 00:43:04.000
So, we come up with this family

00:43:04.001 --> 00:43:06.000
of microarchitectural
Happens-Before graphs,

00:43:06.001 --> 00:43:08.220
graphs and then we use

00:43:08.221 --> 00:43:10.770
SMT solver techniques to make it

00:43:10.771 --> 00:43:12.970
efficient to check for cyclic

00:43:12.971 --> 00:43:14.740
or acyclicity for each one

00:43:14.741 --> 00:43:16.600
of these many
Happens-Before graphs

00:43:16.601 --> 00:43:18.280
and as long as we find

00:43:18.281 --> 00:43:19.560
a cycle and something
was supposed

00:43:19.561 --> 00:43:21.095
to be forbidden, we're good.

00:43:21.096 --> 00:43:23.290
If there was something
that was supposed to be

00:43:23.291 --> 00:43:25.755
forbidden and we find
an acyclic case,

00:43:25.756 --> 00:43:27.770
we can give that to
the designer and we can

00:43:27.771 --> 00:43:30.245
say, here's your problem.

00:43:30.246 --> 00:43:32.380
We have had cases where we give

00:43:32.381 --> 00:43:34.270
that to a designer
or we look at it

00:43:34.271 --> 00:43:38.545
ourselves and we can figure
out where the erroneous.

00:43:38.546 --> 00:43:41.580
Design aspect was
that caused us to be

00:43:41.581 --> 00:43:42.890
missing an edge that would

00:43:42.891 --> 00:43:44.514
have ordered things
appropriately.

00:43:44.515 --> 00:43:46.500
We've also found cases
where we were missing

00:43:46.501 --> 00:43:48.720
an axiom and had to add an axiom.

00:43:48.721 --> 00:43:50.180
So we can go either way and

00:43:50.181 --> 00:43:52.490
the tools are fast enough
to be interactive.

00:43:52.491 --> 00:43:54.775
For these kinds of
specifications,

00:43:54.776 --> 00:43:57.015
the runs are seconds, minutes,

00:43:57.016 --> 00:43:59.790
occasionally hours,
not too often hours.

00:43:59.791 --> 00:44:02.630
So, we started as I said,

00:44:02.631 --> 00:44:04.060
from that sort of ISA to

00:44:04.061 --> 00:44:07.410
microarchitecture
view, but clearly,

00:44:07.411 --> 00:44:08.680
real system span from

00:44:08.681 --> 00:44:11.250
high-level languages through
OS and compilers and down

00:44:11.251 --> 00:44:13.035
to microarchitecture and below

00:44:13.036 --> 00:44:15.520
and so we wanted
a more comprehensive view.

00:44:15.521 --> 00:44:17.530
A more recent tool
that we started about

00:44:17.531 --> 00:44:20.260
three years ago called TriCheck,

00:44:20.261 --> 00:44:23.220
has this sort of
three-layer view.

00:44:23.221 --> 00:44:24.640
So we start from high-level

00:44:24.641 --> 00:44:26.900
language litmus
tests written in C,

00:44:26.901 --> 00:44:29.375
in our case it could
be another language,

00:44:29.376 --> 00:44:31.305
and we take them through

00:44:31.306 --> 00:44:33.870
some sort of evaluator that
says what is supposed to

00:44:33.871 --> 00:44:35.970
be permitted or
forbidden about that

00:44:35.971 --> 00:44:37.270
litmus tests from

00:44:37.271 --> 00:44:39.375
the high-level language memory
models point of view.

00:44:39.376 --> 00:44:42.100
So we get that permitted or
forbidden output up top.

00:44:42.101 --> 00:44:43.975
We also take them through

00:44:43.976 --> 00:44:45.660
compiler mappings that take from

00:44:45.661 --> 00:44:49.285
C down to an instruction
level view of things,

00:44:49.286 --> 00:44:52.360
and then across through
our axiomatic models to

00:44:52.361 --> 00:44:55.520
a microarchitectural
hardware-aware view of what

00:44:55.521 --> 00:44:57.580
is observable or unobservable

00:44:57.581 --> 00:44:59.505
and we put those two together,

00:44:59.506 --> 00:45:01.290
and you can see this sort of

00:45:01.291 --> 00:45:03.170
matrix that results from this.

00:45:03.171 --> 00:45:05.470
If the software says that
something is supposed to be

00:45:05.471 --> 00:45:09.440
permitted and our model says
it's observable, we're okay.

00:45:09.441 --> 00:45:11.390
If the software says that
something is supposed to be

00:45:11.391 --> 00:45:13.160
forbidden and our model says

00:45:13.161 --> 00:45:14.975
I have enumerated everything

00:45:14.976 --> 00:45:16.770
and every single case is cyclic,

00:45:16.771 --> 00:45:18.200
it will never be observable,

00:45:18.201 --> 00:45:21.095
also okay and those are
the two green boxes.

00:45:21.096 --> 00:45:23.360
If the software model says
something is supposed to be

00:45:23.361 --> 00:45:26.100
permitted and we say it
will never be observed,

00:45:26.101 --> 00:45:28.580
that is overly strict
but not a bug.

00:45:28.581 --> 00:45:30.180
So that's a case where
you might be leaving

00:45:30.181 --> 00:45:32.695
some performance on
the table but it's okay.

00:45:32.696 --> 00:45:35.160
If the software says that
something is supposed to be

00:45:35.161 --> 00:45:38.130
forbidden and we find a case
where it's observable,

00:45:38.131 --> 00:45:42.395
we find an acyclic happens
before graph, that is a bug.

00:45:42.396 --> 00:45:46.295
So, to test out the utility
of this kind of a framework,

00:45:46.296 --> 00:45:49.030
we tried it out on
a new emerging instruction

00:45:49.031 --> 00:45:50.350
set architecture called RISC-V.

00:45:50.351 --> 00:45:52.815
I should also say,

00:45:52.816 --> 00:45:54.600
as I said, this is fast enough.

00:45:54.601 --> 00:45:56.775
These are sort of
minutes of execution.

00:45:56.776 --> 00:45:58.050
This is fast enough that you can

00:45:58.051 --> 00:46:01.465
iteratively run through
design processes.

00:46:01.466 --> 00:46:04.515
You can decide when
you find a bug,

00:46:04.516 --> 00:46:05.720
what do you want to change?

00:46:05.721 --> 00:46:07.480
Do you want to change
the ISA itself,

00:46:07.481 --> 00:46:09.540
the compiler or the
microarchitecture?

00:46:09.541 --> 00:46:12.420
We actually found bugs basically
up and down the stack.

00:46:12.421 --> 00:46:14.445
We have found bugs in compilers,

00:46:14.446 --> 00:46:15.850
we have found bugs in

00:46:15.851 --> 00:46:18.800
microarchitectures and as
I will talk about now,

00:46:18.801 --> 00:46:20.230
we have found bugs
in an instruction

00:46:20.231 --> 00:46:22.235
set architecture, namely RISC-V.

00:46:22.236 --> 00:46:24.960
So for the RISC-V case study,

00:46:24.961 --> 00:46:27.115
we started a couple years ago

00:46:27.116 --> 00:46:29.805
when RISC-Vs instruction
set architecture,

00:46:29.806 --> 00:46:32.435
this is now so widely known,

00:46:32.436 --> 00:46:34.520
open-source instruction
set architecture.

00:46:34.521 --> 00:46:37.975
At the time it was in
a draft specification mode,

00:46:37.976 --> 00:46:41.345
but it was still being widely
used and talked about.

00:46:41.346 --> 00:46:46.950
We took 1700 different
C11 programs as

00:46:46.951 --> 00:46:50.725
our high-level language
litmus tests and we

00:46:50.726 --> 00:46:52.370
developed axioms for

00:46:52.371 --> 00:46:55.210
seven distinct RISC-V
implementations.

00:46:55.211 --> 00:46:56.230
So of each of these would be

00:46:56.231 --> 00:46:59.180
a legal processor
within the RISC-V spec,

00:46:59.181 --> 00:47:01.190
but different amounts
of out of orderness.

00:47:01.191 --> 00:47:02.360
So you can imagine one being

00:47:02.361 --> 00:47:04.500
a simple in-order
single-issue processor

00:47:04.501 --> 00:47:06.590
with no speculation all the way

00:47:06.591 --> 00:47:09.220
up to fancy out-of-order
pipelines with

00:47:09.221 --> 00:47:12.170
lots of reordering
and speculation.

00:47:12.171 --> 00:47:13.760
They all abided by
the specs though,

00:47:13.761 --> 00:47:15.805
but they varied in reordering.

00:47:15.806 --> 00:47:20.170
What we found when we went
through this process,

00:47:20.171 --> 00:47:23.610
hundreds of times we were
ending up in the red square,

00:47:23.611 --> 00:47:25.900
the buggy outcome square.

00:47:27.830 --> 00:47:29.900
It was true both for

00:47:29.901 --> 00:47:31.560
the base specification of

00:47:31.561 --> 00:47:33.790
the ISA as well as
for one that had

00:47:33.791 --> 00:47:35.360
additional support for atomics

00:47:35.361 --> 00:47:36.430
that was supposed to actually

00:47:36.431 --> 00:47:39.990
help with exactly these
kinds of problems;

00:47:39.991 --> 00:47:42.610
providing appropriate
fences and so forth.

00:47:42.611 --> 00:47:44.510
The problem was that it

00:47:44.511 --> 00:47:47.560
actually didn't provide
appropriate fences.

00:47:47.561 --> 00:47:50.065
So in the previous talk,
for example,

00:47:50.066 --> 00:47:52.420
Christopher talked about
inserting L fences at

00:47:52.421 --> 00:47:55.525
key points to
order parts of the code.

00:47:55.526 --> 00:47:58.215
RISC-V did not have

00:47:58.216 --> 00:48:01.460
a sufficiently what's
called cumulative type of

00:48:01.461 --> 00:48:03.160
fence of that sort to bring

00:48:03.161 --> 00:48:05.315
back ordering when it
was needed and in fact,

00:48:05.316 --> 00:48:07.130
it could not legally

00:48:07.131 --> 00:48:10.085
compile many C programs
as a result of that.

00:48:10.086 --> 00:48:11.970
Because there are C constructs

00:48:11.971 --> 00:48:13.930
in the C11 memory model that

00:48:13.931 --> 00:48:15.885
say that a programmer is
supposed to be able to

00:48:15.886 --> 00:48:18.270
ask for sequential consistency,

00:48:18.271 --> 00:48:20.370
and if you don't have
the right kind of

00:48:20.371 --> 00:48:22.775
fence to actually
implement that ordering,

00:48:22.776 --> 00:48:25.245
you can never compile
that program correctly.

00:48:25.246 --> 00:48:28.960
So that's one of several
issues that were found,

00:48:28.961 --> 00:48:32.655
that led to these kinds
of buggy outcome results.

00:48:32.656 --> 00:48:36.640
We worked to get RISC-Vs
attention and eventually,

00:48:36.641 --> 00:48:37.670
after our paper was published,

00:48:37.671 --> 00:48:39.120
we did get their attention,

00:48:39.121 --> 00:48:42.110
and a memory model
working group was formed

00:48:42.111 --> 00:48:46.320
about a year ago to address
these issues and it's really

00:48:46.321 --> 00:48:49.545
a nice sort of win-win situation

00:48:49.546 --> 00:48:51.600
in the sense that

00:48:51.601 --> 00:48:54.110
the memory model working group
was able to work through

00:48:54.111 --> 00:48:56.320
the issues and create

00:48:56.321 --> 00:48:59.175
a memory model that's
not just a more correct

00:48:59.176 --> 00:49:00.500
than before but is also formally

00:49:00.501 --> 00:49:04.995
specified and a lot
cleaner than before.

00:49:04.996 --> 00:49:07.020
Just last week,

00:49:07.021 --> 00:49:09.870
the memory model working group
and the RISC-V consortium

00:49:09.871 --> 00:49:11.660
members voted to ratify

00:49:11.661 --> 00:49:14.250
this new improved
RISC-V memory model.

00:49:14.251 --> 00:49:17.190
We're going through
the final dotting

00:49:17.191 --> 00:49:20.115
the I's and crossing the
T's of making that real,

00:49:20.116 --> 00:49:23.355
that ratification
real. So that's great.

00:49:23.356 --> 00:49:25.830
What about spectrum meltdown?

00:49:25.831 --> 00:49:28.610
So as I said, about a year ago,

00:49:28.611 --> 00:49:30.490
we were making
this mental transition

00:49:30.491 --> 00:49:33.650
from the memory ordering
issues that you worry

00:49:33.651 --> 00:49:35.490
about for Memory
Consistency Models to

00:49:35.491 --> 00:49:38.300
the memory ordering issues

00:49:38.301 --> 00:49:40.415
that you worry
about for security.

00:49:40.416 --> 00:49:43.380
So here's my one
slide simplistic version

00:49:43.381 --> 00:49:45.760
of what you just heard
a half an hour about.

00:49:45.761 --> 00:49:48.020
Spectre and Meltdown
are essentially,

00:49:48.021 --> 00:49:51.145
take a well-known cache
side-channel attack

00:49:51.146 --> 00:49:53.150
in this case, flush and reload.

00:49:53.151 --> 00:49:56.750
Mix it with a widely used
hardware feature speculation

00:49:56.751 --> 00:49:58.079
and what was surprising

00:49:58.080 --> 00:50:00.780
was not either of
those on their own,

00:50:00.781 --> 00:50:04.860
but the facility at which new
exploits could be created

00:50:04.861 --> 00:50:08.745
and so clearly there was
an awful lot of news that broke.

00:50:08.746 --> 00:50:10.850
We had actually already
been working for

00:50:10.851 --> 00:50:13.855
about six months at
that point on a tool

00:50:13.856 --> 00:50:16.360
that would build off of

00:50:16.361 --> 00:50:18.190
TryCheck and address some of

00:50:18.191 --> 00:50:20.020
these issues and so in January,

00:50:20.021 --> 00:50:22.730
we set to work to recreate

00:50:22.731 --> 00:50:24.090
Spectre and Meltdown and see what

00:50:24.091 --> 00:50:25.915
else we could find along the way.

00:50:25.916 --> 00:50:28.760
The basic principled approach

00:50:28.761 --> 00:50:31.540
that we wanted was to step
away from the idea of

00:50:31.541 --> 00:50:33.990
security being kind of "close

00:50:33.991 --> 00:50:37.570
the door after the horse
is out of the barn" to

00:50:37.571 --> 00:50:40.810
a more principled
forward-looking approach

00:50:40.811 --> 00:50:42.130
where we could give designers

00:50:42.131 --> 00:50:43.610
tools that would help
them reason about

00:50:43.611 --> 00:50:46.280
their systems in advance
and more automatically.

00:50:46.281 --> 00:50:48.090
So that you don't have
to stare so much at

00:50:48.091 --> 00:50:49.830
individual designs
but instead you

00:50:49.831 --> 00:50:52.065
can have more automated analysis.

00:50:52.066 --> 00:50:53.360
Our goal was the following;

00:50:53.361 --> 00:50:56.325
we wanted to be able to
give someone- give a

00:50:56.326 --> 00:51:00.770
system a good specification
of the system to study,

00:51:00.771 --> 00:51:03.300
in a specification of a class of

00:51:03.301 --> 00:51:06.825
attack patterns and
then from that say,

00:51:06.826 --> 00:51:10.075
go, analyze, synthesize,
tell me what you find.

00:51:10.076 --> 00:51:12.385
Can you find that attack pattern

00:51:12.386 --> 00:51:16.445
exploitable in that specified
system? That was the idea.

00:51:16.446 --> 00:51:18.980
Either output synthesized attacks

00:51:18.981 --> 00:51:20.745
or determine that
none are possible.

00:51:20.746 --> 00:51:25.715
Now you could say that this
is a malware generator.

00:51:25.716 --> 00:51:29.000
It kind of is, but the goal
is to have this be in

00:51:29.001 --> 00:51:30.600
the hands of designers
rather than in

00:51:30.601 --> 00:51:32.865
the hands of people
who want malware.

00:51:32.866 --> 00:51:35.210
So what we did is we did that.

00:51:35.211 --> 00:51:38.660
We developed a tool called
CheckMate to do this based

00:51:38.661 --> 00:51:41.000
on the microarchitectural happens

00:51:41.001 --> 00:51:43.450
before graphs that I
already talked about,

00:51:43.451 --> 00:51:47.310
and the too long didn't read
version of this is that

00:51:47.311 --> 00:51:49.260
we- the tool automatically

00:51:49.261 --> 00:51:51.095
synthesize Spectre and Meltdown,

00:51:51.096 --> 00:51:56.315
as well as, two new distinct
exploits and many variants.

00:51:56.316 --> 00:51:59.400
The top link here

00:51:59.401 --> 00:52:01.520
is our archive paper from

00:52:01.521 --> 00:52:03.450
January where we talk just about

00:52:03.451 --> 00:52:06.220
the two new variants and then

00:52:06.221 --> 00:52:08.200
the bottom link is

00:52:08.201 --> 00:52:10.670
the draft hot off
the process of a paper

00:52:10.671 --> 00:52:15.035
that will get published
in October about

00:52:15.036 --> 00:52:17.690
the actual tool by

00:52:17.691 --> 00:52:20.290
which we did this and
techniques by which we do this,

00:52:20.291 --> 00:52:21.380
that I'm going to
talk about next.

00:52:21.381 --> 00:52:22.810
So in more detail,

00:52:22.811 --> 00:52:25.000
the idea is to frame
these classes of

00:52:25.001 --> 00:52:27.290
attacks as patterns of
event interleavings,

00:52:27.291 --> 00:52:28.530
but hey, that's what memory

00:52:28.531 --> 00:52:30.245
consistency models
are already doing.

00:52:30.246 --> 00:52:32.475
That's what our happens before
graphs are already doing.

00:52:32.476 --> 00:52:34.295
So essentially,
we're saying here is

00:52:34.296 --> 00:52:36.285
a fragment of a
happens before graph.

00:52:36.286 --> 00:52:39.735
Do you find it anywhere
in an execution?

00:52:39.736 --> 00:52:41.400
And second of all,

00:52:41.401 --> 00:52:44.080
we want the executions
to be hardware specific.

00:52:44.081 --> 00:52:45.680
We want to know if the attack is

00:52:45.681 --> 00:52:48.185
realizable on a given
hardware implementation.

00:52:48.186 --> 00:52:50.510
So we need a way of specifying
hardware and we do that

00:52:50.511 --> 00:52:53.355
with the new spec axioms
the same as before.

00:52:53.356 --> 00:52:55.970
So as before,

00:52:55.971 --> 00:52:59.420
we have the ability to
take a microarchitecture.

00:53:01.050 --> 00:53:03.710
Take a microarchitecture
and turn it into

00:53:03.711 --> 00:53:05.575
axioms and we have this ability,

00:53:05.576 --> 00:53:07.760
unlike before, to
give it a pattern.

00:53:07.761 --> 00:53:09.270
So, instead of saying take

00:53:09.271 --> 00:53:12.055
the axioms and tell me
if you find a cycle,

00:53:12.056 --> 00:53:14.500
it's take the axioms
and tell me if you find

00:53:14.501 --> 00:53:17.820
this pattern in
an acyclic execution.

00:53:17.821 --> 00:53:19.600
So, it's a little bit beyond,

00:53:19.601 --> 00:53:21.040
it's actually a lot beyond

00:53:21.041 --> 00:53:22.750
where we were before because it's

00:53:22.751 --> 00:53:27.170
a cycle check with a pattern
finding action as well.

00:53:27.171 --> 00:53:29.690
So, for example, one

00:53:29.691 --> 00:53:31.860
of the things that I
didn't have time to talk

00:53:31.861 --> 00:53:35.390
about in the Memory Consistency
model space is we have

00:53:35.391 --> 00:53:39.380
a notion of how to
manage cache lifetime.

00:53:39.381 --> 00:53:41.450
So, when a value comes

00:53:41.451 --> 00:53:43.980
from cache line and where
it was sourced from.

00:53:43.981 --> 00:53:48.115
We call those values in
cache lifetimes or ViCLs.

00:53:48.116 --> 00:53:50.320
So, we can come up
with constructs

00:53:50.321 --> 00:53:52.270
that allow us to reason

00:53:52.271 --> 00:53:54.330
about the possible
sources of a value.

00:53:54.331 --> 00:53:55.955
Did it come from the store buffer

00:53:55.956 --> 00:53:58.025
or did it come from the cache?

00:53:58.026 --> 00:53:59.920
If so, was it an eviction out of

00:53:59.921 --> 00:54:01.420
the cache in between
and so forth?

00:54:01.421 --> 00:54:03.830
So, ViCL creates the correspond

00:54:03.831 --> 00:54:06.290
of something new in the
cache and ViCL expires,

00:54:06.291 --> 00:54:08.030
it corresponds to something

00:54:08.031 --> 00:54:10.325
being evicted out of the cache.

00:54:10.326 --> 00:54:13.445
So, you take your
microarchitectural axioms,

00:54:13.446 --> 00:54:15.665
you take a pattern that
you're looking for,

00:54:15.666 --> 00:54:18.660
and you take some constraints
on the number of cores,

00:54:18.661 --> 00:54:19.780
the number of threads,

00:54:19.781 --> 00:54:22.845
the number of instructions
to keep things tractable,

00:54:22.846 --> 00:54:25.230
and you send that into
your tool CheckMate.

00:54:25.231 --> 00:54:30.945
Again, it enumerates
possible execution graphs that,

00:54:30.946 --> 00:54:32.660
A, are acyclic and that,

00:54:32.661 --> 00:54:33.970
B, shows this pattern.

00:54:33.971 --> 00:54:36.020
Now I think you can
see that this is

00:54:36.021 --> 00:54:38.310
a case where
automation is a huge,

00:54:38.311 --> 00:54:40.165
sort of, brain helper.

00:54:40.166 --> 00:54:42.610
You would hate to
have to stare at

00:54:42.611 --> 00:54:45.080
complicated graphs and look
for that pattern in them,

00:54:45.081 --> 00:54:47.180
you want some help with this.

00:54:47.181 --> 00:54:52.310
So, specification is
essentially the same as before.

00:54:52.350 --> 00:54:54.970
Speculation is something that

00:54:54.971 --> 00:54:57.795
new spec is already supported.

00:54:57.796 --> 00:55:01.660
Basically, we can allow for
items to be brought into

00:55:01.661 --> 00:55:03.600
the cache in a way that isn't

00:55:03.601 --> 00:55:06.430
necessarily ordered with
instruction execution, right?

00:55:06.431 --> 00:55:07.710
We can allow for items to be

00:55:07.711 --> 00:55:09.040
brought into the
cache in a way that

00:55:09.041 --> 00:55:11.630
isn't particularly ordered
with branch instructions.

00:55:11.631 --> 00:55:13.210
So, those are the kinds
of things that

00:55:13.211 --> 00:55:16.015
actually were raised
in the previous talk.

00:55:16.016 --> 00:55:19.535
In addition, because we have
this full-stack analysis,

00:55:19.536 --> 00:55:22.440
we can handle user-level
software operating system

00:55:22.441 --> 00:55:24.155
or hardware events and locations,

00:55:24.156 --> 00:55:26.070
user-level software
operating system

00:55:26.071 --> 00:55:28.245
and hardware ordering details.

00:55:28.246 --> 00:55:31.620
Hardware optimizations like
speculation fit in well

00:55:31.621 --> 00:55:33.980
because we've handle
operating systems in

00:55:33.981 --> 00:55:36.360
the past in a tool
called COATCheck.

00:55:36.361 --> 00:55:38.540
We can handle processes
and resource-sharing,

00:55:38.541 --> 00:55:41.175
and memory hierarchies and
cache coherence protocols.

00:55:41.176 --> 00:55:42.700
I don't want to oversell this.

00:55:42.701 --> 00:55:45.000
We are not going down to
Verilog in this tool,

00:55:45.001 --> 00:55:46.925
although we do in other tools.

00:55:46.926 --> 00:55:49.460
But we do think that once

00:55:49.461 --> 00:55:51.680
you're operating in
this axiomatic landscape,

00:55:51.681 --> 00:55:55.065
this is a huge help in
automating the analysis.

00:55:55.066 --> 00:55:57.790
So, the last piece of
this puzzle is how do we

00:55:57.791 --> 00:55:59.790
do these pattern enumerations,

00:55:59.791 --> 00:56:02.295
and that's with
Relational Model Finding.

00:56:02.296 --> 00:56:04.940
So, in the first half
of the talk,

00:56:04.941 --> 00:56:08.365
we did the cycle analysis
using SMT techniques.

00:56:08.366 --> 00:56:09.980
This is Relational Model Finding

00:56:09.981 --> 00:56:11.635
because we need to
find a pattern,

00:56:11.636 --> 00:56:13.195
not just a cycle.

00:56:13.196 --> 00:56:15.980
So, RMF essentially tries to find

00:56:15.981 --> 00:56:17.780
the satisfying instances or

00:56:17.781 --> 00:56:20.950
sub-parts within a larger graph.

00:56:20.951 --> 00:56:25.320
In this case, we're doing
this by taking the new spec,

00:56:25.321 --> 00:56:27.570
translating it into Alloy,

00:56:27.571 --> 00:56:29.970
which is a domain-specific
language that's intended

00:56:29.971 --> 00:56:33.885
to fit into a relational model
finding approach.

00:56:33.886 --> 00:56:35.600
The RMF problems get mapped

00:56:35.601 --> 00:56:37.535
onto a model-finder
called Kodkod,

00:56:37.536 --> 00:56:40.475
which in turn uses
off-the-shelf SAT solvers.

00:56:40.476 --> 00:56:42.160
So, in this way,

00:56:42.161 --> 00:56:45.060
we can feed together and
automate the process.

00:56:45.061 --> 00:56:49.670
So, here is what Spectre
looks like in our world.

00:56:49.671 --> 00:56:52.720
So, as before, these columns

00:56:52.721 --> 00:56:55.915
of nodes correspond to
instructions being executed.

00:56:55.916 --> 00:56:57.380
You can see by the label on

00:56:57.381 --> 00:57:03.600
the top the different
threads that are involved.

00:57:03.601 --> 00:57:06.550
You can see, for example,

00:57:06.551 --> 00:57:08.220
the Spectre was based on

00:57:08.221 --> 00:57:10.170
a Flush and
Reload threat pattern.

00:57:10.171 --> 00:57:11.705
So, in the upper right,

00:57:11.706 --> 00:57:12.930
that's the pattern that

00:57:12.931 --> 00:57:16.055
the Relational Model Finding
technique is looking for.

00:57:16.056 --> 00:57:19.370
You can see that you
would not want to analyze

00:57:19.371 --> 00:57:20.950
that graph to look
for that pattern in

00:57:20.951 --> 00:57:22.780
there, but it's in there.

00:57:22.781 --> 00:57:25.590
Follow the red arrows,
and you can find it.

00:57:25.591 --> 00:57:27.950
The last thing that it

00:57:27.951 --> 00:57:30.110
does is it generates
the skeleton or

00:57:30.111 --> 00:57:32.630
the security litmus test

00:57:32.631 --> 00:57:36.940
that would correspond to
a version of Spectre.

00:57:36.941 --> 00:57:39.610
This is a template for code.

00:57:39.611 --> 00:57:41.750
You still have to make it

00:57:41.751 --> 00:57:44.280
concrete with particular
addresses and so forth.

00:57:44.281 --> 00:57:49.275
But the step from here to
a real piece of Spectre code is

00:57:49.276 --> 00:57:53.520
pretty straightforward piece of

00:57:53.521 --> 00:57:55.110
programming work for
someone who is familiar

00:57:55.111 --> 00:57:57.335
with the instruction set
that they're operating in.

00:57:57.336 --> 00:57:59.900
Okay. So, that's Spectre.

00:58:00.040 --> 00:58:03.460
One of the things that we
wanted to do was to say,

00:58:03.461 --> 00:58:05.650
Spectre was based on one class of

00:58:05.651 --> 00:58:08.120
exploits called a Flush
and Reload threat pattern.

00:58:08.121 --> 00:58:10.810
We wanted to see what about
other threat patterns?

00:58:10.811 --> 00:58:12.235
Prime and Probe being one,

00:58:12.236 --> 00:58:14.360
that has been talked about
a lot in the literature.

00:58:14.361 --> 00:58:16.800
So, we said, "What if we put
in this different pattern,

00:58:16.801 --> 00:58:18.255
the Prime and Probe pattern,

00:58:18.256 --> 00:58:20.920
in. What happens then?"

00:58:20.921 --> 00:58:23.325
In fact, what happened
then was that we found

00:58:23.326 --> 00:58:26.125
two distinct variants
of the exploit.

00:58:26.126 --> 00:58:29.840
In this case, using
invalidation patterns

00:58:29.841 --> 00:58:33.270
between two cores
rather than Flush and

00:58:33.271 --> 00:58:37.285
Reload patterns on a single core
to create a new case

00:58:37.286 --> 00:58:42.815
of almost identical to Spectre,

00:58:42.816 --> 00:58:44.560
but with invalidations being

00:58:44.561 --> 00:58:46.230
the way that things
were evicted out of

00:58:46.231 --> 00:58:50.245
the cache rather than
flushes on a single core.

00:58:50.246 --> 00:58:53.110
Again, the tool generates

00:58:53.111 --> 00:58:55.475
the security litmus
tests automatically.

00:58:55.476 --> 00:58:58.455
We call them security litmus
tests rather than

00:58:58.456 --> 00:59:01.430
malware because the idea

00:59:01.431 --> 00:59:03.955
is that just as in
Memory Consistency Models,

00:59:03.956 --> 00:59:07.430
we have built up as a community
a suite of tests over

00:59:07.431 --> 00:59:11.725
time that designers use to
stress test their systems.

00:59:11.726 --> 00:59:13.800
We view the ability to generate

00:59:13.801 --> 00:59:16.710
security litmus tests as
an important construct for

00:59:16.711 --> 00:59:19.320
designers to help
them design against

00:59:19.321 --> 00:59:22.430
these threats and to explore
new classes of threats.

00:59:22.431 --> 00:59:24.080
So, one of the things
that we want to

00:59:24.081 --> 00:59:25.585
do in subsequent work is to

00:59:25.586 --> 00:59:27.870
automatically generate
the threat patterns

00:59:27.871 --> 00:59:29.170
that might be most interesting,

00:59:29.171 --> 00:59:31.340
and that's actually
where we are right now.

00:59:31.341 --> 00:59:35.705
Okay. So, second to last slide,

00:59:35.706 --> 00:59:38.810
this is the money
shot in some ways.

00:59:38.811 --> 00:59:40.500
So, the top part of

00:59:40.501 --> 00:59:42.950
this table are the Flush
and Reload patterns,

00:59:42.951 --> 00:59:45.525
the bottom part of this table
are Prime and Probe,

00:59:45.526 --> 00:59:47.480
it's the exploit pattern.

00:59:47.481 --> 00:59:50.380
The number of instructions
that we place does a bound in

00:59:50.381 --> 00:59:52.130
the Relational Model Finding
because that does

00:59:52.131 --> 00:59:54.695
affect the execution time
of all these tools.

00:59:54.696 --> 00:59:56.580
But what you can see is that with

00:59:56.581 --> 00:59:58.405
relatively small
instruction counts,

00:59:58.406 --> 01:00:02.400
you can generate pretty real
exploits such as Spectre,

01:00:02.401 --> 01:00:05.055
Meltdown, and the new variants.

01:00:05.056 --> 01:00:09.265
The minutes to synthesize
the first exploit, that is,

01:00:09.266 --> 01:00:10.860
this thing is relational model

01:00:10.861 --> 01:00:13.115
finding and finding exploit,

01:00:13.116 --> 01:00:18.045
the first one is five minutes
to a couple hours.

01:00:18.046 --> 01:00:20.610
It continues to run until it

01:00:20.611 --> 01:00:23.090
has found all the
possible exploits,

01:00:23.091 --> 01:00:24.900
all the possible ways that

01:00:24.901 --> 01:00:26.180
that pattern can be found in

01:00:26.181 --> 01:00:27.670
all the possible graphs
that could be

01:00:27.671 --> 01:00:34.050
enumerated in that seven minutes
to three or four hours.

01:00:34.051 --> 01:00:36.700
The number of exploits

01:00:36.701 --> 01:00:38.240
that were synthesized
correspond to

01:00:38.241 --> 01:00:40.560
all the different balletic
ways that you can

01:00:40.561 --> 01:00:43.637
create something that's
Spectre-like or SpectrePrime-like,

01:00:43.638 --> 01:00:46.499
and you can see that
those numbers get quite high.

01:00:46.500 --> 01:00:48.000
So, one of the values
that we see in

01:00:48.001 --> 01:00:50.275
this work is the ability
to give someone

01:00:50.276 --> 01:00:52.315
the reassurance that
they found not just

01:00:52.316 --> 01:00:55.255
a way that someone could
exploit their code,

01:00:55.256 --> 01:00:57.260
but hopefully
a whole bunch of ways that

01:00:57.261 --> 01:01:00.100
someone could exploit
their hardware.

01:01:00.240 --> 01:01:04.895
So, in terms of takeaways,

01:01:04.896 --> 01:01:07.460
yes, we found two new variants,

01:01:07.461 --> 01:01:09.395
SpectrePrime and MeltdownPrime,

01:01:09.396 --> 01:01:10.940
that use cache invalidations

01:01:10.941 --> 01:01:15.560
rather than C FFlush.

01:01:15.561 --> 01:01:17.560
But more importantly to us is

01:01:17.561 --> 01:01:19.090
this key overall philosophy

01:01:19.091 --> 01:01:21.090
that the event ordering issues of

01:01:21.091 --> 01:01:23.910
security exploit patterns
aligns strongly with

01:01:23.911 --> 01:01:25.750
the Memory Consistency
model analysis

01:01:25.751 --> 01:01:27.865
that we've already been doing.

01:01:27.866 --> 01:01:30.915
It's a very principled step from

01:01:30.916 --> 01:01:32.940
ad hoc one-off analysis of

01:01:32.941 --> 01:01:35.665
different exploits to
formal automated synthesis.

01:01:35.666 --> 01:01:37.040
The goal has always been to

01:01:37.041 --> 01:01:38.510
span software, operating system,

01:01:38.511 --> 01:01:42.365
and hardware for a holistic,
but hardware-aware analysis.

01:01:42.366 --> 01:01:46.610
Those are the two papers that
I am inviting you to read.

01:01:46.611 --> 01:01:48.780
If you remember nothing else,

01:01:48.781 --> 01:01:52.325
please wake up and look at
the two names in red because

01:01:52.326 --> 01:01:54.150
everyone in the room
should want to

01:01:54.151 --> 01:01:56.395
hire these two
wonderful students.

01:01:56.396 --> 01:01:58.480
Caroline Trippel sat down

01:01:58.481 --> 01:02:00.210
after our last TriCheck
paper and said,

01:02:00.211 --> 01:02:01.750
"I want to work on security."

01:02:01.751 --> 01:02:04.230
Within six months,
she was doing this.

01:02:04.231 --> 01:02:06.340
She is an amazing
PhD student who will

01:02:06.341 --> 01:02:08.425
be on the markets this year.

01:02:08.426 --> 01:02:10.620
Yatin Manerkar also did

01:02:10.621 --> 01:02:12.360
a lot of the work that
I talked about today,

01:02:12.361 --> 01:02:16.060
including finding through
our toolset errors,

01:02:16.061 --> 01:02:20.550
and to formally proven
correct compiler mapping proofs,

01:02:20.551 --> 01:02:22.945
and errors in compilers
that go with it,

01:02:22.946 --> 01:02:24.550
and some other universal

01:02:24.551 --> 01:02:26.260
Memory Consistency model analysis

01:02:26.261 --> 01:02:27.350
beyond witness test that

01:02:27.351 --> 01:02:28.985
I didn't have time to talk about.

01:02:28.986 --> 01:02:31.220
So, with that, I'm happy
to answer some questions.

01:02:31.221 --> 01:02:31.550
Thanks.

01:02:31.551 --> 01:02:43.690
&gt;&gt; That was a really
interesting talk.

01:02:43.691 --> 01:02:46.410
Thank you. So, one
question I do have is,

01:02:46.411 --> 01:02:49.670
you talk about having
this specific microarchitecture

01:02:49.671 --> 01:02:50.930
happens before graphs.

01:02:50.931 --> 01:02:52.500
Once you've got one
of those, you can go,

01:02:52.501 --> 01:02:55.300
such person, synthesize
a whole bunch of examples.

01:02:55.301 --> 01:02:57.735
This was a natural follow-up
question, which is,

01:02:57.736 --> 01:03:00.695
if you look at something
like the NetSpectre attack,

01:03:00.696 --> 01:03:04.950
that discussed
a side channel based on

01:03:04.951 --> 01:03:06.450
the power state VA VX two

01:03:06.451 --> 01:03:10.030
units or if you'd look
at variant force,

01:03:10.031 --> 01:03:12.355
those are about
memory dependent speculation.

01:03:12.356 --> 01:03:13.920
So, there's this
natural question of,

01:03:13.921 --> 01:03:15.560
can you take what you've got here

01:03:15.561 --> 01:03:17.155
and actually go and find

01:03:17.156 --> 01:03:18.520
these new classes or

01:03:18.521 --> 01:03:19.640
is it something where
you do need to know

01:03:19.641 --> 01:03:20.990
all these uhbs upfront and

01:03:20.991 --> 01:03:22.790
then once you've got them,
you can go find them?

01:03:22.791 --> 01:03:26.150
&gt;&gt; So, as I said,

01:03:26.151 --> 01:03:30.350
we have found bugs that
span OS to hardware.

01:03:30.351 --> 01:03:35.035
We have found bugs that
span between cores.

01:03:35.036 --> 01:03:38.030
So, one thing I
want to stress is,

01:03:38.031 --> 01:03:40.360
we aren't doing this per graph,

01:03:40.361 --> 01:03:42.995
we're doing this for
a set of axioms.

01:03:42.996 --> 01:03:45.975
So, as long as one
can write the axioms,

01:03:45.976 --> 01:03:48.635
we can enumerate the graphs.

01:03:48.636 --> 01:03:51.545
So, that's one thing.
The second thing

01:03:51.546 --> 01:03:54.030
is the things like
memory dependence,

01:03:54.031 --> 01:03:56.905
there already within
our model. So, yes.

01:03:56.906 --> 01:04:02.450
NetSpectre, I am pretty

01:04:02.451 --> 01:04:04.300
sure we could write axioms to

01:04:04.301 --> 01:04:06.400
handle how the packet processing

01:04:06.401 --> 01:04:08.180
feeds through the rest
of the system.

01:04:08.181 --> 01:04:10.825
&gt;&gt; No. To clarify my question.

01:04:10.826 --> 01:04:13.770
I mean, you synthesize

01:04:13.771 --> 01:04:16.180
Prime and Probe or you
synthesize this Spectre Prime,

01:04:16.181 --> 01:04:18.290
Meltdown Prime, things like this.

01:04:18.450 --> 01:04:22.475
Essentially, either this tool
should have spat out that

01:04:22.476 --> 01:04:23.530
you can have memory

01:04:23.531 --> 01:04:26.055
dependence-based speculation
bugs or it didn't.

01:04:26.056 --> 01:04:27.360
Either way is interesting.

01:04:27.361 --> 01:04:29.160
&gt;&gt; So, for example,

01:04:29.161 --> 01:04:31.610
as far as I know, NetSpectre
is still Flush and Reload.

01:04:31.611 --> 01:04:34.940
It's just a different
style of flush and

01:04:34.941 --> 01:04:38.875
reload that causes the invocation
to happen differently.

01:04:38.876 --> 01:04:40.120
&gt;&gt; We can take this offline.

01:04:40.121 --> 01:04:41.025
&gt;&gt; Let me finish.

01:04:41.026 --> 01:04:41.495
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:04:41.496 --> 01:04:43.800
&gt;&gt; So, if it's within
Flush and Reload,

01:04:43.801 --> 01:04:46.300
then with the right axioms
we can synthesize it.

01:04:46.301 --> 01:04:49.670
I agree and I said at the end
that we do want to be able

01:04:49.671 --> 01:04:54.890
to enumerate new classes
of exploits.

01:04:54.891 --> 01:05:03.450
That's what we're
working on now. Stefan.

01:05:06.020 --> 01:05:09.620
&gt;&gt; So based on your experience
with CheckMate,

01:05:09.621 --> 01:05:12.895
do you have any advice
for hardware designers,

01:05:12.896 --> 01:05:15.250
for how to design a CPU

01:05:15.251 --> 01:05:18.630
so that CheckMate won't
find any exploits?

01:05:19.600 --> 01:05:23.570
&gt;&gt; When we started
this work five years ago,

01:05:23.571 --> 01:05:26.680
people I think were very
reluctant at the idea of

01:05:26.681 --> 01:05:28.830
having to have axiomatic
specifications

01:05:28.831 --> 01:05:30.950
alongside their design.

01:05:30.951 --> 01:05:33.860
I'm hoping that over the set

01:05:33.861 --> 01:05:37.095
of observations that we've
made over these five years,

01:05:37.096 --> 01:05:41.010
we're increasingly
finding designers

01:05:41.011 --> 01:05:43.090
more open to having

01:05:43.091 --> 01:05:45.120
that be a key part of
the design process.

01:05:45.121 --> 01:05:47.840
So, one analogy I
make is 20 years ago,

01:05:47.841 --> 01:05:49.965
architects didn't
think about power.

01:05:49.966 --> 01:05:52.305
They were encouraged not
to think about power.

01:05:52.306 --> 01:05:54.640
That was for later
in the design chain.

01:05:54.641 --> 01:06:01.410
Today, if I say
the word verification,

01:06:01.411 --> 01:06:02.730
people think about
something that's very

01:06:02.731 --> 01:06:04.715
late in the hardware
design process.

01:06:04.716 --> 01:06:06.780
One of our goals is
to make tools that

01:06:06.781 --> 01:06:09.080
are amenable to being
used earlier in

01:06:09.081 --> 01:06:10.340
the design process so

01:06:10.341 --> 01:06:12.140
that hardware designers
will be more

01:06:12.141 --> 01:06:13.490
open to using them
because they will

01:06:13.491 --> 01:06:15.310
be at interactive speeds.

01:06:15.311 --> 01:06:18.230
So, we think that
the axiomatic approach,

01:06:18.231 --> 01:06:21.025
while not natural to
today's architects,

01:06:21.026 --> 01:06:23.235
is helpful enough that

01:06:23.236 --> 01:06:26.430
people should be
coming around to it.

01:06:27.800 --> 01:06:30.470
We can talk about whether to do

01:06:30.471 --> 01:06:32.690
a correct by-construction
flow where

01:06:32.691 --> 01:06:36.350
the axioms follow
automatically from

01:06:36.351 --> 01:06:38.745
synthesis tools or whether
the axioms are written

01:06:38.746 --> 01:06:41.555
alongside a traditional design.

01:06:41.556 --> 01:06:43.705
I'm okay with either one.

01:06:43.706 --> 01:06:45.430
The main thing is I think we need

01:06:45.431 --> 01:06:48.225
these interface specifications
that let us say,

01:06:48.226 --> 01:06:49.400
"At this point, here are

01:06:49.401 --> 01:06:51.365
some rules you should
be able to count on."

01:06:51.366 --> 01:06:55.160
I think that's key.
If we start to have

01:06:55.161 --> 01:07:00.480
these interface specifications
with corresponding axioms,

01:07:00.481 --> 01:07:02.750
then we can automate different

01:07:02.751 --> 01:07:04.450
analysis techniques
some of which would

01:07:04.451 --> 01:07:08.295
be synthesis driven or
correct by construction,

01:07:08.296 --> 01:07:10.290
and some of which would be

01:07:10.291 --> 01:07:13.950
ancillary but still
formal documentation.

01:07:13.951 --> 01:07:16.450
One of the key things is
most Memory Consistency Models,

01:07:16.451 --> 01:07:18.735
for example, are still
written in English.

01:07:18.736 --> 01:07:20.745
But increasingly,

01:07:20.746 --> 01:07:22.740
people are coming around to
the idea that they should

01:07:22.741 --> 01:07:24.570
be written in a way that can

01:07:24.571 --> 01:07:27.980
be automatically
analyzed and verified.

01:07:27.981 --> 01:07:29.880
So increasingly, for example,

01:07:29.881 --> 01:07:33.390
RISC-V went from not

01:07:33.391 --> 01:07:36.440
very correct and written
in English spec,

01:07:36.441 --> 01:07:40.160
to now being something that
is formally specified.

01:07:40.161 --> 01:07:41.780
There are formal models for it.

01:07:41.781 --> 01:07:50.555
I think that's a good sign. Yes.

01:07:50.556 --> 01:07:53.250
&gt;&gt; Let's go back to five
years ago where we don't know

01:07:53.251 --> 01:07:55.790
how to exploit
speculative execution.

01:07:55.791 --> 01:07:58.385
Do you think your
methodology can identify

01:07:58.386 --> 01:08:01.100
any kind of exploit variants
that we know right now?

01:08:01.101 --> 01:08:10.150
&gt;&gt; So, I'm not going to say that.

01:08:11.480 --> 01:08:15.270
There's a chicken and egg aspect
of this of modelling

01:08:15.271 --> 01:08:18.780
enough to be able
to find the things.

01:08:18.781 --> 01:08:23.500
We were finding bugs before

01:08:23.501 --> 01:08:25.610
Spectre and Meltdown broke and we

01:08:25.611 --> 01:08:28.835
found different bugs
after the news broke.

01:08:28.836 --> 01:08:32.050
We hadn't been using

01:08:32.051 --> 01:08:35.050
speculation in all of our
models before January.

01:08:35.051 --> 01:08:38.220
So we added it in afterwards.

01:08:38.221 --> 01:08:39.715
There could be something that

01:08:39.716 --> 01:08:41.770
we are choosing to abstract away

01:08:41.771 --> 01:08:46.645
now that we should include
in a model going forward.

01:08:46.646 --> 01:08:50.300
But the basics of Spectre and

01:08:50.301 --> 01:08:53.820
Meltdown have been
known for a while.

01:08:53.821 --> 01:08:57.110
Like speculation and Flush

01:08:57.111 --> 01:08:58.750
and Reload are both concepts

01:08:58.751 --> 01:09:00.610
that have been known for
more than five years.

01:09:00.611 --> 01:09:01.725
So in that sense,

01:09:01.726 --> 01:09:03.675
people should have known,

01:09:03.676 --> 01:09:06.040
but I think people
were unaware of

01:09:06.041 --> 01:09:08.480
the facility with which
they could be exploited.

01:09:08.481 --> 01:09:11.445
&gt;&gt; It seems that there's
some area that we

01:09:11.446 --> 01:09:14.840
couldn't identify the problem
by using your methodology.

01:09:14.841 --> 01:09:16.785
It looks very foremost
and very nice.

01:09:16.786 --> 01:09:18.510
Do you have any idea what you can

01:09:18.511 --> 01:09:20.150
provide through this method

01:09:20.151 --> 01:09:21.470
in general and what are the thing

01:09:21.471 --> 01:09:23.390
that you couldn't provide?

01:09:23.391 --> 01:09:24.920
So at the end of today,
we're going to see it on

01:09:24.921 --> 01:09:26.835
other type of that channel,

01:09:26.836 --> 01:09:28.540
probably completely
different variant

01:09:28.541 --> 01:09:30.675
from the speculative execution.

01:09:30.676 --> 01:09:32.820
But we couldn't tell whether

01:09:32.821 --> 01:09:35.060
they're actually such a thing
so you could just,

01:09:35.061 --> 01:09:37.190
by using your methodology.

01:09:37.191 --> 01:09:38.735
&gt;&gt; So as I've said,

01:09:38.736 --> 01:09:42.140
we are looking at ways to
automatically generate

01:09:42.141 --> 01:09:44.460
the new attack pattern classes

01:09:44.461 --> 01:09:46.040
so that we can, for example,

01:09:46.041 --> 01:09:48.580
you can imagine genetic
algorithms or something that

01:09:48.581 --> 01:09:52.920
creates new graph snippets

01:09:52.921 --> 01:09:55.370
and then says "Is this
an intact class or not?"

01:09:55.371 --> 01:09:59.595
So it's an ongoing thing

01:09:59.596 --> 01:10:02.340
but the ability to

01:10:02.341 --> 01:10:04.560
automatically analyze once you

01:10:04.561 --> 01:10:05.780
have an attack class seems

01:10:05.781 --> 01:10:08.020
like an important step forward.

01:10:13.290 --> 01:10:16.375
&gt;&gt; Great talk. I really
enjoyed your talk.

01:10:16.376 --> 01:10:18.250
My question is a variant of,

01:10:18.251 --> 01:10:19.800
I don't know if I'm
asking the same question

01:10:19.801 --> 01:10:21.420
or not actually,

01:10:21.421 --> 01:10:25.260
but so you mentioned
the two side channel attacks you

01:10:25.261 --> 01:10:29.315
mentioned are based
on memory caches.

01:10:29.316 --> 01:10:34.555
So the Prime and Probe
and the Flush and Reload.

01:10:34.556 --> 01:10:36.480
There are a lot of caches in

01:10:36.481 --> 01:10:38.310
the architecture, not just that.

01:10:38.311 --> 01:10:43.750
The question is how amenable
is your technique to

01:10:43.751 --> 01:10:49.420
actually reapply these things

01:10:49.421 --> 01:10:52.390
to other caches in
the CPU or elsewhere?

01:10:52.391 --> 01:10:56.230
&gt;&gt; I think everyone in
the room would like it

01:10:56.231 --> 01:11:00.865
if there was one big answer.

01:11:00.866 --> 01:11:02.740
There clearly isn't.

01:11:02.741 --> 01:11:05.425
It steps on the way.

01:11:05.426 --> 01:11:10.220
I feel that we have all

01:11:10.221 --> 01:11:13.150
been lied to about

01:11:13.151 --> 01:11:16.560
what architectural state
is. Let's be honest.

01:11:16.561 --> 01:11:19.570
When I teach an undergrad
architecture class,

01:11:19.571 --> 01:11:21.110
we talk about architectural state

01:11:21.111 --> 01:11:22.360
being what the software can

01:11:22.361 --> 01:11:28.140
see but that is
an extremely nebulous thing.

01:11:28.141 --> 01:11:29.620
So for example,
Christopher's talk

01:11:29.621 --> 01:11:31.075
talked about timing jitter.

01:11:31.076 --> 01:11:32.700
That's because there's a form of

01:11:32.701 --> 01:11:35.365
observability that comes
from what you can time.

01:11:35.366 --> 01:11:37.120
One of the things
that we're working on

01:11:37.121 --> 01:11:38.940
right now is ways to take

01:11:38.941 --> 01:11:43.655
these graphs and put quantifiers
onto the edges to say,

01:11:43.656 --> 01:11:45.235
"This is an exploit."

01:11:45.236 --> 01:11:48.250
If the timing sequence is

01:11:48.251 --> 01:11:51.790
sufficiently observable
and that has to do

01:11:51.791 --> 01:11:56.735
with the timing analysis
granularity against

01:11:56.736 --> 01:12:00.740
the performance variations
but you could just

01:12:00.741 --> 01:12:02.750
as we could imagine
an observer model that

01:12:02.751 --> 01:12:05.125
puts edge weights based on time,

01:12:05.126 --> 01:12:07.080
you could get
edge weights based on

01:12:07.081 --> 01:12:09.655
power dissipation,
on radio emanations,

01:12:09.656 --> 01:12:13.215
on temperature and say that,

01:12:13.216 --> 01:12:15.060
"If someone's in the room
and could measure

01:12:15.061 --> 01:12:17.325
temperature variations
across the chip,

01:12:17.326 --> 01:12:18.610
then this becomes

01:12:18.611 --> 01:12:20.220
a side channel that we
need to worry about."

01:12:20.221 --> 01:12:23.000
So there are ways to add
quantifiers to some of

01:12:23.001 --> 01:12:26.245
this that seem
promising but I'm not,

01:12:26.246 --> 01:12:27.570
we're not done with that yet."

01:12:27.571 --> 01:12:31.210
[inaudible] and see what happens.

01:12:31.211 --> 01:12:33.405
&gt;&gt; In some sense
that's my question is

01:12:33.406 --> 01:12:36.860
this work at
the level of you need

01:12:36.861 --> 01:12:39.340
the caroline in the room
to actually do this or

01:12:39.341 --> 01:12:42.875
more engineers that can
actually use this tool?

01:12:42.876 --> 01:12:46.080
&gt;&gt; The goal is to have
them be engineers.

01:12:46.081 --> 01:12:47.180
We gave a tutorial at

01:12:47.181 --> 01:12:48.875
a school about a year
and a half ago.

01:12:48.876 --> 01:12:50.450
Our materials are online.

01:12:50.451 --> 01:12:51.980
The tools, Checkmate is

01:12:51.981 --> 01:12:53.400
an open sourced tier but

01:12:53.401 --> 01:12:55.350
the Tricheck and
Type-check and so forth,

01:12:55.351 --> 01:12:57.315
those tools are all open sourced.

01:12:57.316 --> 01:13:00.055
The DSL is available.

01:13:00.056 --> 01:13:02.495
I'll be honest. It's still

01:13:02.496 --> 01:13:05.380
a pair programming experience
probably at its best.

01:13:05.381 --> 01:13:09.065
You're sitting alongside
someone who knows what's what.

01:13:09.066 --> 01:13:11.350
But the goal is to
have it be something

01:13:11.351 --> 01:13:14.300
that a hardware designer
can use on their own.

01:13:16.040 --> 01:13:18.405
&gt;&gt; All right. Let's
thank the speaker.

01:13:18.406 --> 01:13:19.900
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

01:13:37.430 --> 01:13:40.290
&gt;&gt; Okay, our next talk
is going to be

01:13:40.291 --> 01:13:42.310
given by Onor Mutlu.

01:13:42.311 --> 01:13:44.960
Onor is a Professor of
Computer Science at

01:13:44.961 --> 01:13:46.680
ETH Zurich and he's also on

01:13:46.681 --> 01:13:49.590
the faculty at
Carnegie Mellon University.

01:13:49.591 --> 01:13:51.740
Onur`s broad research
interests are in

01:13:51.741 --> 01:13:55.125
computer architecture systems
and bio for informatics.

01:13:55.126 --> 01:13:57.230
A major current focus of his is

01:13:57.231 --> 01:13:59.695
on memory and storage systems.

01:13:59.696 --> 01:14:01.865
He's going to talk today
about memory systems.

01:14:01.866 --> 01:14:03.820
So, we're going to be
changing a little bit from

01:14:03.821 --> 01:14:07.540
Meltdown Inspector to
RowHammer and Beyond.

01:14:07.890 --> 01:14:10.610
Onur has a history with us at

01:14:10.611 --> 01:14:12.670
Microsoft Research in fact he was

01:14:12.671 --> 01:14:13.960
the first member of

01:14:13.961 --> 01:14:15.410
the Computer
Architecture Group at

01:14:15.411 --> 01:14:18.510
Microsoft Research back in 2006.

01:14:18.540 --> 01:14:21.170
Onur has won numerous awards

01:14:21.171 --> 01:14:23.800
and I'll just mention one here.

01:14:23.801 --> 01:14:28.775
He was the winner of the
inaugural IEEE Computer Society,

01:14:28.776 --> 01:14:32.280
Young Computer Architect Award.

01:14:32.281 --> 01:14:34.020
So, take it away Onur.

01:14:34.021 --> 01:14:38.210
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much
Alex. Is this working?

01:14:38.211 --> 01:14:43.235
Okay. It's great to be back
here at Microsoft as always,

01:14:43.236 --> 01:14:45.670
and thanks for the invitation
Stefan and Alex.

01:14:45.671 --> 01:14:47.395
I'll talk about RowHammer.

01:14:47.396 --> 01:14:49.590
I actually see that
it's going to be

01:14:49.591 --> 01:14:52.100
a change compared to

01:14:52.101 --> 01:14:54.940
the previous things but I
actually see these as related,

01:14:54.941 --> 01:14:57.820
because it's about
the mindset that hardware

01:14:57.821 --> 01:15:01.040
is not as vulnerable and you
can actually attack things.

01:15:01.041 --> 01:15:03.745
I think there's a history
if we had time,

01:15:03.746 --> 01:15:06.925
that we could go over of
these hardware related attacks.

01:15:06.926 --> 01:15:09.140
I think Meltdown Inspector
happened because

01:15:09.141 --> 01:15:11.300
some things like
RowHammer for example,

01:15:11.301 --> 01:15:14.370
instigated some people to
actually examine issues

01:15:14.371 --> 01:15:15.670
in hardware and they

01:15:15.671 --> 01:15:17.660
actually found out
other issues in hardware.

01:15:17.661 --> 01:15:19.260
We can talk about
that separately.

01:15:19.261 --> 01:15:22.850
But before, let me see.
This is not working.

01:15:22.851 --> 01:15:25.410
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

01:15:25.411 --> 01:15:32.400
&gt;&gt; Oh, okay.

01:15:32.401 --> 01:15:34.670
So, before I go into RowHammer,

01:15:34.671 --> 01:15:35.510
basically we're going to talk

01:15:35.511 --> 01:15:36.640
about the main memory system.

01:15:36.641 --> 01:15:37.850
It's a critical component of

01:15:37.851 --> 01:15:39.415
all systems that
we designed today.

01:15:39.416 --> 01:15:40.550
Whatever you're
designing you got to

01:15:40.551 --> 01:15:41.955
have some working storage.

01:15:41.956 --> 01:15:44.080
This system must scale
in many dimensions in

01:15:44.081 --> 01:15:45.990
terms of size,
technology, efficiency,

01:15:45.991 --> 01:15:48.820
cost algorithms we used
to manage it et cetera,

01:15:48.821 --> 01:15:50.470
to maintain the
performance goals and

01:15:50.471 --> 01:15:52.625
the scaling benefits that
have been used to so far.

01:15:52.626 --> 01:15:54.950
In regards to whatever you
attached to main memory,

01:15:54.951 --> 01:15:58.325
your bottleneck by
that interface the main memory.

01:15:58.326 --> 01:16:00.650
I'll very quickly go over
some trends that are

01:16:00.651 --> 01:16:02.355
affecting main memory
to set the stage,

01:16:02.356 --> 01:16:04.795
and how we came to a RowHammer.

01:16:04.796 --> 01:16:06.240
Basically, these are
three major trends that are

01:16:06.241 --> 01:16:07.950
affecting main memory
as I see them.

01:16:07.951 --> 01:16:09.770
We want more capacity,
more bandwidth,

01:16:09.771 --> 01:16:12.315
more quality of service,
more performance.

01:16:12.316 --> 01:16:15.660
This what I think evidence in
[inaudible] with the beast and

01:16:15.661 --> 01:16:17.160
the megabeast engines that

01:16:17.161 --> 01:16:20.360
had terabytes and terabytes
of memory actually.

01:16:20.361 --> 01:16:22.750
Energy and power is
a key system design concern

01:16:22.751 --> 01:16:24.090
and technology scaling is ending.

01:16:24.091 --> 01:16:26.280
This talk is going to be
about technology scaling.

01:16:26.281 --> 01:16:27.510
But, to understand that,

01:16:27.511 --> 01:16:29.820
I think we need to cover
the other trends also.

01:16:29.821 --> 01:16:33.660
We were able to put a lot
of course on machines as

01:16:33.661 --> 01:16:35.160
applications are
becoming increasing data

01:16:35.161 --> 01:16:37.530
intensive and we want to
consolidate more and more.

01:16:37.531 --> 01:16:39.650
That's driving the capacity
bandwidth and quality of

01:16:39.651 --> 01:16:42.615
service requirements up and
up performance requirements.

01:16:42.616 --> 01:16:44.055
This is one example.

01:16:44.056 --> 01:16:45.840
This was actually from a paper

01:16:45.841 --> 01:16:47.725
by HP Labs at
University of Michigan.

01:16:47.726 --> 01:16:49.420
They've shown that core count is

01:16:49.421 --> 01:16:52.175
increasing much faster
than DRAM capacity.

01:16:52.176 --> 01:16:54.260
That's why we are bottlenecked
by DRAM capacity.

01:16:54.261 --> 01:16:56.380
You could argue with all of
the numbers on this graph,

01:16:56.381 --> 01:16:58.830
and you could say that all
this trend is not continuing,

01:16:58.831 --> 01:17:00.140
but if you think
about why the thread

01:17:00.141 --> 01:17:01.320
may not be continuing,

01:17:01.321 --> 01:17:02.710
we may not be able to feed

01:17:02.711 --> 01:17:04.560
the course with
the data they need.

01:17:04.561 --> 01:17:06.610
So we actually may not be placing

01:17:06.611 --> 01:17:10.520
course as much as we
were doing in the past.

01:17:10.521 --> 01:17:11.910
But the trend is
actually increasing

01:17:11.911 --> 01:17:14.100
is similar to this and GPUs.

01:17:14.101 --> 01:17:17.055
Anyway, we want more
capacity for memory and

01:17:17.056 --> 01:17:20.220
that drives the capacity
of the DRAM chip.

01:17:20.221 --> 01:17:22.420
Let's take a look at
the history of the DRAM in

01:17:22.421 --> 01:17:24.425
the last 18 years in terms of,

01:17:24.426 --> 01:17:27.065
how much capacity bandwidth
and latency has improved?

01:17:27.066 --> 01:17:30.415
This has been always a
capacity focused business.

01:17:30.416 --> 01:17:32.860
If you look at this,
capacity has improved by

01:17:32.861 --> 01:17:36.085
more than 100x in
the last 18 years.

01:17:36.086 --> 01:17:38.655
You can see that in
the last few years,

01:17:38.656 --> 01:17:40.530
the trend is not exponential,

01:17:40.531 --> 01:17:42.255
it's actually staggering
a little bit.

01:17:42.256 --> 01:17:44.150
So we're having difficulties
in DRAM scaling.

01:17:44.151 --> 01:17:45.475
This is an evidence of that.

01:17:45.476 --> 01:17:48.035
I'll give you more
evidence in the stock.

01:17:48.036 --> 01:17:50.085
Bandwidth has not
improved as much,

01:17:50.086 --> 01:17:51.410
but you could
potentially improve it.

01:17:51.411 --> 01:17:53.340
What do you think of latency?

01:17:53.341 --> 01:17:55.865
How much has it improved
in the last 18 years?

01:17:55.866 --> 01:17:58.970
This much? Yeah, I agree it's
this much in this graph.

01:17:58.971 --> 01:18:01.115
It's basically 30 percent
think about DRAM.

01:18:01.116 --> 01:18:02.910
If you want to pay
for it you can of

01:18:02.911 --> 01:18:05.995
course give your arm and
leg and you can pay for it.

01:18:05.996 --> 01:18:07.930
But latency is almost constant,

01:18:07.931 --> 01:18:09.785
but DRAM is critical
for performance,

01:18:09.786 --> 01:18:11.520
capacity, latency, bandwidth,

01:18:11.521 --> 01:18:13.060
different applications have
different requirements.

01:18:13.061 --> 01:18:14.710
I think these are
backward-looking applications,

01:18:14.711 --> 01:18:16.470
we have many more
forward-looking applications

01:18:16.471 --> 01:18:16.880
that are going to

01:18:16.881 --> 01:18:20.365
put even more pressure on DRAM.

01:18:20.366 --> 01:18:22.620
The second major trend energy,

01:18:22.621 --> 01:18:24.190
is a key system
design concern and

01:18:24.191 --> 01:18:25.955
memory consumes
a lot of the energy.

01:18:25.956 --> 01:18:28.310
This is a paper from IBM in 2003,

01:18:28.311 --> 01:18:31.925
where they showed that in
their big iron servers,

01:18:31.926 --> 01:18:33.790
40 to 50 percent of

01:18:33.791 --> 01:18:34.840
the entire system energy

01:18:34.841 --> 01:18:36.955
spent on the off-chip
memory hierarchy.

01:18:36.956 --> 01:18:39.300
Fast-forward to today, their

01:18:39.301 --> 01:18:41.570
reports from IBM
again in power eight,

01:18:41.571 --> 01:18:43.240
more than 40 percent of the power

01:18:43.241 --> 01:18:45.155
is spent just solely in DRAM.

01:18:45.156 --> 01:18:47.460
That's true for GPUs also
that other paper from

01:18:47.461 --> 01:18:48.820
ISCO 2015 is from

01:18:48.821 --> 01:18:51.125
GPUs and our results are
actually show that also.

01:18:51.126 --> 01:18:53.545
So, memory energy is
becoming a big concern

01:18:53.546 --> 01:18:54.800
and one of the issues is

01:18:54.801 --> 01:18:56.510
the unconsumed power
when it's not used,

01:18:56.511 --> 01:18:57.880
you need to periodically
refresh it and

01:18:57.881 --> 01:18:59.340
this turns out to be
a scaling problem

01:18:59.341 --> 01:19:02.440
also which we may get to
toward the end of the talk.

01:19:02.441 --> 01:19:03.770
So, on top of all of this, we're

01:19:03.771 --> 01:19:05.150
requiring a lot more from memory

01:19:05.151 --> 01:19:06.530
going forward we're
going to require even

01:19:06.531 --> 01:19:08.175
more with the new applications,

01:19:08.176 --> 01:19:09.360
but on top of this were having

01:19:09.361 --> 01:19:11.415
difficulties with
the DRAM technology scaling.

01:19:11.416 --> 01:19:14.020
Basically, we relied on reducing

01:19:14.021 --> 01:19:16.780
the size of the DRAM cell
to increase the capacity,

01:19:16.781 --> 01:19:17.980
but this is ending.

01:19:17.981 --> 01:19:20.710
Basically ITRS has been
projecting for a long time

01:19:20.711 --> 01:19:23.730
that DRAM will not scale
below X nanometers.

01:19:23.731 --> 01:19:25.370
I like keeping X over here,

01:19:25.371 --> 01:19:26.600
because I don't need to change

01:19:26.601 --> 01:19:29.420
my slides but they change
their projections of course.

01:19:29.421 --> 01:19:32.035
I'll give you the numbers
for X in the next slide.

01:19:32.036 --> 01:19:34.445
But scaling has enabled
us to get more capacity,

01:19:34.446 --> 01:19:36.700
reasonable energy
scaling, lower cost.

01:19:36.701 --> 01:19:38.460
It didn't help us with
latency that much

01:19:38.461 --> 01:19:41.645
but it did help
with other things.

01:19:41.646 --> 01:19:43.760
So, what is the scaling problem

01:19:43.761 --> 01:19:44.950
that we're having with DRAM?

01:19:44.951 --> 01:19:46.680
For any memory to work,

01:19:46.681 --> 01:19:48.735
you need to have
a storage device,

01:19:48.736 --> 01:19:50.800
in DRAM the storage device
is the capacitor,

01:19:50.801 --> 01:19:52.200
and you time an access device.

01:19:52.201 --> 01:19:53.310
In DRAM the access device,

01:19:53.311 --> 01:19:54.590
the access transistor, the byte

01:19:54.591 --> 01:19:55.990
line and the sense amplifier.

01:19:55.991 --> 01:19:57.500
Both of these components
need to work

01:19:57.501 --> 01:19:59.285
reliably for any memory to work.

01:19:59.286 --> 01:20:00.980
In DRAM, this capacitor must be

01:20:00.981 --> 01:20:02.715
large enough for
reliable sensing,

01:20:02.716 --> 01:20:04.170
and this access transistor and

01:20:04.171 --> 01:20:05.650
the sensing structures
must be large

01:20:05.651 --> 01:20:08.910
enough for low leakage
and high retention time.

01:20:08.911 --> 01:20:10.820
This was the value
that was assigned text

01:20:10.821 --> 01:20:12.690
by ITRS in 2013.

01:20:12.691 --> 01:20:14.450
They basically said scaling

01:20:14.451 --> 01:20:17.350
below 35 nanometers
is challenging.

01:20:19.850 --> 01:20:22.290
What do you guys
think where we are

01:20:22.291 --> 01:20:25.475
at memory feature size today?

01:20:25.476 --> 01:20:27.950
Is it 35 nanometers?

01:20:27.951 --> 01:20:30.725
This is the dimensions
of the cell.

01:20:30.726 --> 01:20:32.910
Ten? Any guesses?

01:20:32.911 --> 01:20:33.880
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

01:20:33.881 --> 01:20:35.945
&gt;&gt; Ramsey, that's good.

01:20:35.946 --> 01:20:38.760
Yes  we're about
maybe 17 nanometers or so.

01:20:38.761 --> 01:20:41.390
Clearly we've gone
below 35 nanometers.

01:20:41.391 --> 01:20:43.370
But we've had issues.

01:20:43.371 --> 01:20:45.870
So, basically, DRAM scaling
has become increasingly

01:20:45.871 --> 01:20:47.200
difficult and we're
going to talk about one

01:20:47.201 --> 01:20:48.905
of the big problems
in DRAM scaling.

01:20:48.906 --> 01:20:50.625
So, what have people
done about it?

01:20:50.626 --> 01:20:52.070
Basically, this has led to

01:20:52.071 --> 01:20:54.355
the proliferation of
different types of DRAM,

01:20:54.356 --> 01:20:55.710
both the application requirements

01:20:55.711 --> 01:20:57.060
and the requirements
from the bottom.

01:20:57.061 --> 01:20:59.620
As a result there are
many emerging technologies.

01:20:59.621 --> 01:21:01.605
You can see that their
3D Stacked DRAM,

01:21:01.606 --> 01:21:03.585
you get higher bandwidth
reduce latency DRAM,

01:21:03.586 --> 01:21:06.160
Low-Power DRAM,
Non-Volatile Memory.

01:21:06.161 --> 01:21:07.750
They all have greens,

01:21:07.751 --> 01:21:09.785
but they all have reds also.

01:21:09.786 --> 01:21:12.090
So there is no single memory
that's good at anything.

01:21:12.091 --> 01:21:13.600
As a result, one major trend has

01:21:13.601 --> 01:21:15.920
been going into
hybrid memory technologies,

01:21:15.921 --> 01:21:17.590
where you have multiple
different technologies.

01:21:17.591 --> 01:21:19.310
Potentially, multiple
different DRAMs.

01:21:19.311 --> 01:21:20.990
You design the hardware
and the software

01:21:20.991 --> 01:21:23.230
to manage data allocation
and movements such that

01:21:23.231 --> 01:21:24.860
you achieve the greens as much as

01:21:24.861 --> 01:21:27.645
possible while avoiding
the reds as much as possible.

01:21:27.646 --> 01:21:29.730
This requires clearly changes

01:21:29.731 --> 01:21:30.960
to the interface and changes

01:21:30.961 --> 01:21:32.610
to become more intelligent

01:21:32.611 --> 01:21:34.255
in terms of how we manage memory.

01:21:34.256 --> 01:21:36.350
But this doesn't change
the fact that we need to

01:21:36.351 --> 01:21:37.640
have a memory in

01:21:37.641 --> 01:21:39.300
the system and the memory
needs to scale.

01:21:39.301 --> 01:21:41.925
This is one way of
trying to scale memory,

01:21:41.926 --> 01:21:43.710
but it turns out it's very

01:21:43.711 --> 01:21:45.560
difficult to get rid of
DRAM from the system.

01:21:45.561 --> 01:21:49.210
People have looked at
using MM, for example,

01:21:49.211 --> 01:21:50.680
or PCM Phase-Change Memory,

01:21:50.681 --> 01:21:52.050
but it's going to be
very difficult to

01:21:52.051 --> 01:21:54.640
get rid of all of
the DRAM from the system.

01:21:54.660 --> 01:21:56.750
Let's go a little bit more into

01:21:56.751 --> 01:21:58.250
detail in the memory
scaling problem.

01:21:58.251 --> 01:21:59.500
There's a lot in
the memory problem,

01:21:59.501 --> 01:22:01.350
memory space and we're
working on a lot.

01:22:01.351 --> 01:22:03.155
But I'll start with
the security part of it,

01:22:03.156 --> 01:22:04.280
or reliability and safety.

01:22:04.281 --> 01:22:05.860
I see these as interconnected.

01:22:05.861 --> 01:22:07.510
I'm going to make the connection.

01:22:07.511 --> 01:22:08.580
But there's a lot more to do in

01:22:08.581 --> 01:22:10.735
the memory areas you can see.

01:22:10.736 --> 01:22:12.185
Why start with security?

01:22:12.186 --> 01:22:14.815
I like tying this to
human lives also.

01:22:14.816 --> 01:22:18.000
How many people here know
about Abraham Maslow?

01:22:18.001 --> 01:22:20.930
That's great. He was a very
famous American psychologist.

01:22:20.931 --> 01:22:23.145
He dedicated his life
to understanding

01:22:23.146 --> 01:22:26.065
why people do things
they do, as a result,

01:22:26.066 --> 01:22:29.595
basically, this is
his major work,

01:22:29.596 --> 01:22:32.480
that book that he iterated
over during his lifetime.

01:22:32.481 --> 01:22:34.680
He's probably more famous
for this one essentially,

01:22:34.681 --> 01:22:36.480
which is Maslow's
Hierarchy of Needs.

01:22:36.481 --> 01:22:37.890
He basically said that,

01:22:37.891 --> 01:22:39.290
"We need to start
with reliability and

01:22:39.291 --> 01:22:41.280
security," because if you're
not reliable and secure,

01:22:41.281 --> 01:22:43.510
you cannot think
about relationships,

01:22:43.511 --> 01:22:45.590
friends and you definitely
don't care about

01:22:45.591 --> 01:22:46.995
higher levels of art if you're

01:22:46.996 --> 01:22:48.885
about to die at this moment.

01:22:48.886 --> 01:22:50.370
So, that's why we need to start

01:22:50.371 --> 01:22:51.430
with reliability and security.

01:22:51.431 --> 01:22:54.185
This is another thing that I
use actually in my classes.

01:22:54.186 --> 01:22:55.520
Probably this should
be familiar with

01:22:55.521 --> 01:22:57.260
people who are living in
the Washington State.

01:22:57.261 --> 01:22:58.830
This the Tacoma Narrows Bridge

01:22:58.831 --> 01:23:00.125
that doesn't exist anymore.

01:23:00.126 --> 01:23:01.760
This was built in 1940,

01:23:01.761 --> 01:23:02.965
and six months later,

01:23:02.966 --> 01:23:05.895
it collapsed this way because
of aeroelastic flutter.

01:23:05.896 --> 01:23:09.330
The new bridges are
actually doubled bridges.

01:23:09.650 --> 01:23:12.280
It was actually put in there for

01:23:12.281 --> 01:23:14.340
bandwidth reasons but it's
good for reliability also,

01:23:14.341 --> 01:23:16.590
having two bridges over there.

01:23:18.890 --> 01:23:21.100
While I was at
Microsoft Research,

01:23:21.101 --> 01:23:23.135
I interacted with a lot
of security people

01:23:23.136 --> 01:23:25.390
and this definition of
security I like a lot.

01:23:25.391 --> 01:23:28.560
It's really about preventing
unforeseen consequences.

01:23:28.561 --> 01:23:31.120
I see the previous talk,

01:23:31.121 --> 01:23:33.700
and the previous two talks
actually thinking about

01:23:33.701 --> 01:23:35.570
potentially unforeseen
consequences and

01:23:35.571 --> 01:23:36.970
how can we prevent them.

01:23:36.971 --> 01:23:38.990
Let me tie back into
the DRAM scaling problem,

01:23:38.991 --> 01:23:41.345
this is a slide I showed earlier.

01:23:41.346 --> 01:23:42.940
Basically, we are having
difficulties with

01:23:42.941 --> 01:23:44.635
reducing the size of the circuit.

01:23:44.636 --> 01:23:46.980
As we reduce the size
of the circuit,

01:23:46.981 --> 01:23:49.605
both of the
reliability properties

01:23:49.606 --> 01:23:52.175
are difficult to maintain.

01:23:52.176 --> 01:23:54.390
Essentially, this capacitor
becomes unreliable,

01:23:54.391 --> 01:23:56.805
it becomes more
vulnerable to noise,

01:23:56.806 --> 01:23:58.270
and this access
transistor becomes

01:23:58.271 --> 01:23:59.860
more leaky and more
vulnerable to noise.

01:23:59.861 --> 01:24:01.360
As a result, it's

01:24:01.361 --> 01:24:03.505
really difficult to reduce
the size of the circuit.

01:24:03.506 --> 01:24:04.915
We've been doing
a lot of studies,

01:24:04.916 --> 01:24:05.940
both at the large scale,

01:24:05.941 --> 01:24:07.085
I'll give you one example of the

01:24:07.086 --> 01:24:08.160
large scale that
we've essentially

01:24:08.161 --> 01:24:11.785
analyzed in this paper from 2015.

01:24:11.786 --> 01:24:14.390
All of the memory errors
that Facebook has

01:24:14.391 --> 01:24:15.670
recorded over the course of

01:24:15.671 --> 01:24:17.500
the year in their
entire server fleet,

01:24:17.501 --> 01:24:18.925
this is a lot of
servers actually.

01:24:18.926 --> 01:24:21.525
This is a correlational study
as you can see.

01:24:21.526 --> 01:24:24.205
It turns out as
chip density increases,

01:24:24.206 --> 01:24:25.820
the server failure
rate increases.

01:24:25.821 --> 01:24:28.325
This is because of memory errors
not due to other errors.

01:24:28.326 --> 01:24:30.270
So, there's a clear
correlation between

01:24:30.271 --> 01:24:33.635
higher capacity
and higher errors.

01:24:33.636 --> 01:24:35.110
There's a lot more data
in this paper if

01:24:35.111 --> 01:24:37.080
you're interested which
I'm not going to cover.

01:24:37.081 --> 01:24:40.070
When we first started studying
the DRAM scaling problem,

01:24:40.071 --> 01:24:42.180
we also wanted to do
the small-scale studies,

01:24:42.181 --> 01:24:43.890
and we built this infrastructure

01:24:43.891 --> 01:24:46.845
which is essentially an FPGA
based memory controller,

01:24:46.846 --> 01:24:48.160
where we could do a lot of

01:24:48.161 --> 01:24:50.390
tests using this
memory controller.

01:24:50.391 --> 01:24:51.530
We could configure anything we

01:24:51.531 --> 01:24:53.755
wanted and we keep
improving this.

01:24:53.756 --> 01:24:56.610
We wanted to study first
retention issues but we

01:24:56.611 --> 01:24:58.395
discovered the RowHammer problem

01:24:58.396 --> 01:24:59.820
by building this infrastructure.

01:24:59.821 --> 01:25:01.050
Actually, this was
the infrastructure

01:25:01.051 --> 01:25:02.360
where we discovered
the RowHammer problem,

01:25:02.361 --> 01:25:05.980
you could do many tests in
parallel with different FPGAs.

01:25:05.981 --> 01:25:07.820
We opened sources infrastructure

01:25:07.821 --> 01:25:08.850
so if you're interested you

01:25:08.851 --> 01:25:10.720
can download it at C plus plus

01:25:10.721 --> 01:25:12.985
program but now it's much
more programmer-friendly,

01:25:12.986 --> 01:25:15.075
and you can do
the studies on the FPGAs.

01:25:15.076 --> 01:25:18.020
We don't provide the FPGAs.
That you got to buy.

01:25:18.021 --> 01:25:19.490
So, with this kind
of instruction,

01:25:19.491 --> 01:25:21.985
you can actually do a lot of
studies on real DRAM chips.

01:25:21.986 --> 01:25:23.490
We've studied DRAM Retention,

01:25:23.491 --> 01:25:24.400
I'm not going to talk about,

01:25:24.401 --> 01:25:25.895
this is really interesting,

01:25:25.896 --> 01:25:27.745
and this is really
the fundamental

01:25:27.746 --> 01:25:29.285
scaling issue with DRAM.

01:25:29.286 --> 01:25:30.895
As you reduce the size
of the circuit,

01:25:30.896 --> 01:25:34.180
data becomes very difficult
to maintain inside a cell.

01:25:34.181 --> 01:25:36.140
So, a charge escapes
and charge leaks.

01:25:36.141 --> 01:25:37.670
How do you figure out how long

01:25:37.671 --> 01:25:39.125
the charge will stay in that,

01:25:39.126 --> 01:25:40.910
so that you can determine
your refresh rate?

01:25:40.911 --> 01:25:42.595
We'll get back to
that if we have time.

01:25:42.596 --> 01:25:44.130
But while we were actually

01:25:44.131 --> 01:25:46.095
doing studies in
this infrastructure,

01:25:46.096 --> 01:25:47.930
we were inspired by
other studies that we

01:25:47.931 --> 01:25:49.905
were doing in flush memory.

01:25:49.906 --> 01:25:52.440
Flush memory is very much
prone to real disturbance.

01:25:52.441 --> 01:25:54.050
We said, "Oh, maybe there are

01:25:54.051 --> 01:25:55.680
real disturbance in DRAM also.

01:25:55.681 --> 01:25:57.780
Let's test it using
this infrastructure."

01:25:57.781 --> 01:26:00.710
What we found was actually
curious at that time.

01:26:00.711 --> 01:26:03.050
We basically found that you can

01:26:03.051 --> 01:26:05.740
predictably induce memory errors,

01:26:05.741 --> 01:26:09.240
bit flips, in most DRAM
memory chips at the time.

01:26:09.241 --> 01:26:11.250
This is called DRAM
RowHammer problem.

01:26:11.251 --> 01:26:13.935
It's essentially a simple
hardware failure mechanism

01:26:13.936 --> 01:26:15.190
that can create a widespread

01:26:15.191 --> 01:26:17.310
system security vulnerability.

01:26:17.311 --> 01:26:19.620
You can do it in
a programmatic way.

01:26:19.621 --> 01:26:22.295
People wrote things like

01:26:22.296 --> 01:26:24.145
this as one of
the examples, I like this,

01:26:24.146 --> 01:26:26.420
I put it over here because
I like the title it says,

01:26:26.421 --> 01:26:29.175
"Forget software-now hackers
are exploiting physics."

01:26:29.176 --> 01:26:31.135
This actually I think explains

01:26:31.136 --> 01:26:33.350
the problem in a nice way.
So, what is the problem?

01:26:33.351 --> 01:26:35.515
If you look at DRAM,

01:26:35.516 --> 01:26:37.435
it consists of a bunch of rows,

01:26:37.436 --> 01:26:39.765
and if you want to
read data from a row,

01:26:39.766 --> 01:26:41.135
you need to activate that row,

01:26:41.136 --> 01:26:42.250
which means that you
need to apply high

01:26:42.251 --> 01:26:43.830
voltage to that red line.

01:26:43.831 --> 01:26:45.460
If you want to read
some other row,

01:26:45.461 --> 01:26:47.580
you need to deactivate
that row or this called

01:26:47.581 --> 01:26:49.745
the pre-charge in DRAM
apply low voltage.

01:26:49.746 --> 01:26:51.765
Now if you keep doing
this repeatedly,

01:26:51.766 --> 01:26:53.715
activate pre-charge,
activate pre-charge,

01:26:53.716 --> 01:26:55.530
activate pre-charge,
activate pre-charge.

01:26:55.531 --> 01:26:57.700
Before the cells get refreshed,

01:26:57.701 --> 01:26:59.150
and if you do it enough times,

01:26:59.151 --> 01:27:01.680
it turns out in
most modern DRAM chips

01:27:01.681 --> 01:27:04.485
adjacent rows get bit flips.

01:27:04.486 --> 01:27:06.520
Some bits flip from one

01:27:06.521 --> 01:27:09.045
to zero or zero to one
depending on the encoding.

01:27:09.046 --> 01:27:11.005
Now, that's not supposed
to happen clearly

01:27:11.006 --> 01:27:13.200
because you were not
even writing to memory,

01:27:13.201 --> 01:27:14.970
you're reading from memory,

01:27:14.971 --> 01:27:17.570
and you're affecting the
cells that are around you.

01:27:17.571 --> 01:27:18.950
Those cells could be belonging

01:27:18.951 --> 01:27:20.080
to some of the application,

01:27:20.081 --> 01:27:21.295
to the operating system.

01:27:21.296 --> 01:27:23.095
Essentially, there's
a reliability problem but

01:27:23.096 --> 01:27:25.055
this could also be
a security vulnerability.

01:27:25.056 --> 01:27:26.520
So, we call these
the Hammered Row,

01:27:26.521 --> 01:27:28.195
we call these the Victim Rows.

01:27:28.196 --> 01:27:31.200
It turns out that
most real DRAM chips

01:27:31.201 --> 01:27:32.430
that you can buy in the market,

01:27:32.431 --> 01:27:34.025
more than 80% of them,

01:27:34.026 --> 01:27:36.990
at the time we've done
these tests were vulnerable.

01:27:36.991 --> 01:27:39.330
We could predictably
induce these errors.

01:27:39.331 --> 01:27:42.430
This is actually
a scaling problem

01:27:42.431 --> 01:27:45.015
because this didn't
happen before 2010.

01:27:45.016 --> 01:27:47.715
The first instance that
we saw was in 2010,

01:27:47.716 --> 01:27:49.720
and all of the chips
that were manufactured

01:27:49.721 --> 01:27:51.480
between 2012 and 2013

01:27:51.481 --> 01:27:53.875
that we tested, were
actually vulnerable.

01:27:53.876 --> 01:27:55.240
Why is this scaling problem?

01:27:55.241 --> 01:27:57.680
Essentially, cells got
too close to each other.

01:27:57.681 --> 01:28:01.740
They're not enough isolated
electrically from each other.

01:28:01.741 --> 01:28:04.505
I'll talk about the causes
very briefly later on but

01:28:04.506 --> 01:28:05.840
one intuitive cause is

01:28:05.841 --> 01:28:07.720
essentially
electromagnetic coupling.

01:28:07.721 --> 01:28:10.385
Because one red line is too
close to the other red line,

01:28:10.386 --> 01:28:12.810
whenever you toggle
this red line,

01:28:12.811 --> 01:28:14.650
apply high voltage
the other red line

01:28:14.651 --> 01:28:16.275
is not electrically
isolated enough,

01:28:16.276 --> 01:28:18.120
you're toggling it
a little bit as

01:28:18.121 --> 01:28:20.215
a result you're
opening that red line.

01:28:20.216 --> 01:28:21.910
Which means that the cells
that are vulnerable to

01:28:21.911 --> 01:28:23.805
this effect are
leaking a little bit,

01:28:23.806 --> 01:28:25.170
and if you do it enough times,

01:28:25.171 --> 01:28:27.315
they leak a little
bit enough times.

01:28:27.316 --> 01:28:29.705
If you do it enough times
before the cells get refreshed,

01:28:29.706 --> 01:28:31.740
you basically depleted the charge

01:28:31.741 --> 01:28:33.280
on some of the cells over there.

01:28:33.281 --> 01:28:35.305
If the cells weren't too
close to each other,

01:28:35.306 --> 01:28:37.910
meaning back in 2008 you
didn't have this problem.

01:28:37.911 --> 01:28:40.490
This is a very fundamental
problem in any kind of memory,

01:28:40.491 --> 01:28:42.340
actually any kind of
memory when it scales

01:28:42.341 --> 01:28:44.390
you get this sort of
real disturbance issues.

01:28:44.391 --> 01:28:45.690
If we have time, we'll talk about

01:28:45.691 --> 01:28:47.475
flush memory but we won't
have time for that.

01:28:47.476 --> 01:28:50.200
So, what's more interesting
about this being in DRAM

01:28:50.201 --> 01:28:52.715
is DRAM is directly exposed
to the programming language,

01:28:52.716 --> 01:28:53.465
this is one example of

01:28:53.466 --> 01:28:55.075
programming language,
assembly language.

01:28:55.076 --> 01:28:56.625
So, we wrote this code

01:28:56.626 --> 01:28:59.280
which essentially execute
to the user level.

01:28:59.281 --> 01:29:01.430
What it does is it basically

01:29:01.431 --> 01:29:04.625
avoids cache hits for
these two addresses,

01:29:04.626 --> 01:29:06.740
avoid row hits for
those two addresses,

01:29:06.741 --> 01:29:10.185
and it basically ping pongs
activates to X and Y,

01:29:10.186 --> 01:29:12.735
to the same bank, and if
the chip is vulnerable,

01:29:12.736 --> 01:29:14.740
it'll essentially
get these errors.

01:29:14.741 --> 01:29:17.175
You can download this code
and write it on your laptop.

01:29:17.176 --> 01:29:20.220
Actually, you can download
Google's code which improved

01:29:20.221 --> 01:29:24.340
our code and you're more
likely to discover bit flips.

01:29:24.341 --> 01:29:26.280
At the time we did these studies,

01:29:26.281 --> 01:29:29.200
this was around 2012, basically,

01:29:29.201 --> 01:29:30.865
we ran it on real systems

01:29:30.866 --> 01:29:32.160
and you can see that
as long as you have

01:29:32.161 --> 01:29:35.110
a memory controller that's
good at activating fast,

01:29:35.111 --> 01:29:37.480
that's able to
access memory fast,

01:29:37.481 --> 01:29:38.865
you're able to
induce these errors.

01:29:38.866 --> 01:29:40.950
There's nothing special
about Intel and AMD.

01:29:40.951 --> 01:29:42.450
All of the memory
controllers that are out

01:29:42.451 --> 01:29:43.950
in the market are capable of

01:29:43.951 --> 01:29:47.075
doing that in
real processors today.

01:29:47.076 --> 01:29:49.475
So, it's a real reliability
and security issue.

01:29:49.476 --> 01:29:51.080
In fact, we thought it was

01:29:51.081 --> 01:29:52.965
more of a security issue
than reliability issue.

01:29:52.966 --> 01:29:56.270
When we wrote the paper,
the first sentence we used was,

01:29:56.271 --> 01:29:57.850
"Memory isolation is
a key property of

01:29:57.851 --> 01:29:59.425
a reliable and secure
computing system,

01:29:59.426 --> 01:30:01.035
and access to one memory
address should not have

01:30:01.036 --> 01:30:02.110
unintended side effects on

01:30:02.111 --> 01:30:03.360
data stored in other addresses."

01:30:03.361 --> 01:30:05.465
I still believe this. I think
this is very fundamental.

01:30:05.466 --> 01:30:07.100
We should keep this invariant.

01:30:07.101 --> 01:30:08.810
We also said that
you could actually

01:30:08.811 --> 01:30:10.330
design an attack that could take

01:30:10.331 --> 01:30:13.835
over an entire system by
exploiting the bit flips.

01:30:13.836 --> 01:30:17.390
The good folks at Google
Project Zero did exactly that.

01:30:17.391 --> 01:30:19.110
They published
this beautiful blog post,

01:30:19.111 --> 01:30:20.870
it's beautiful system
security engineering,

01:30:20.871 --> 01:30:23.580
where they said they exploited
the DRAM RowHammer bug,

01:30:23.581 --> 01:30:24.875
I don't like the term bug I think

01:30:24.876 --> 01:30:27.290
a failure mechanism is
a nicer one over here,

01:30:27.291 --> 01:30:29.840
to gain kernel privileges.

01:30:29.841 --> 01:30:31.890
This is directly
copied and pasted from

01:30:31.891 --> 01:30:34.270
their blog posts from 2015.

01:30:34.271 --> 01:30:36.175
They basically test
a selection of laptops

01:30:36.176 --> 01:30:38.310
and found a subset of them,

01:30:38.311 --> 01:30:40.085
exhibit the problem,
and they built

01:30:40.086 --> 01:30:42.399
two working privilege
escalation exploits.

01:30:42.400 --> 01:30:44.100
One of them is less
interesting to me,

01:30:44.101 --> 01:30:45.650
it's actually
Google Native Client.

01:30:45.651 --> 01:30:47.430
The other one essentially is able

01:30:47.431 --> 01:30:50.090
to run a user level process,

01:30:50.091 --> 01:30:52.700
and it's able to induce
these bit flips.

01:30:52.701 --> 01:30:54.685
They were able to
induce bit flips

01:30:54.686 --> 01:30:56.010
in the page table entries

01:30:56.011 --> 01:30:57.410
of that user level process that

01:30:57.411 --> 01:30:59.180
point to their own page table.

01:30:59.181 --> 01:31:01.780
If you're able to
actually do that,

01:31:01.781 --> 01:31:04.725
now you can change the contents
of your own page table.

01:31:04.726 --> 01:31:06.870
For example, you can gain
right to enable access to

01:31:06.871 --> 01:31:09.455
your own page table and
once you have that access,

01:31:09.456 --> 01:31:12.745
you have full access
to the entire memory.

01:31:12.746 --> 01:31:14.810
That's essentially what they did.

01:31:14.811 --> 01:31:16.925
They were able to do this
successfully on I believe

01:31:16.926 --> 01:31:20.540
50 percent of the machines
that they've tested, laptops.

01:31:20.541 --> 01:31:23.070
This became even more
interesting at that time,

01:31:23.071 --> 01:31:24.620
it's called RowHammer
Vulnerability and

01:31:24.621 --> 01:31:26.655
people started drawing
pictures like this.

01:31:26.656 --> 01:31:27.930
I like analogies and this is

01:31:27.931 --> 01:31:30.610
a beautiful analogy that
someone had on Twitter,

01:31:30.611 --> 01:31:33.030
"It's like breaking into
an apartment by repeatedly

01:31:33.031 --> 01:31:35.380
slamming neighbor's door until

01:31:35.381 --> 01:31:37.635
the vibrations open the door
that you were really after.

01:31:37.636 --> 01:31:38.350
"So, if you want to escape

01:31:38.351 --> 01:31:39.480
from here you might want to start

01:31:39.481 --> 01:31:42.315
banging on these walls over here.

01:31:42.316 --> 01:31:44.890
There's a lot of

01:31:44.891 --> 01:31:46.675
attacks that were
developed on top of this,

01:31:46.676 --> 01:31:48.435
I'm not going to go over this,

01:31:48.436 --> 01:31:50.120
these slides are available.

01:31:50.121 --> 01:31:51.190
You can go over
these, people have

01:31:51.191 --> 01:31:52.345
developed a lot of attacks

01:31:52.346 --> 01:31:55.075
over the years even
very recently.

01:31:55.076 --> 01:31:56.940
I'm going to highlight
a couple of them,

01:31:56.941 --> 01:31:59.140
this is one of the
attacks from TU Graz,

01:31:59.141 --> 01:32:00.510
these are actually
the same folks who

01:32:00.511 --> 01:32:03.335
developed Meltdown
Inspector later on.

01:32:03.336 --> 01:32:05.220
They basically show
that you could

01:32:05.221 --> 01:32:07.805
remotely gain access to
the system of the website

01:32:07.806 --> 01:32:11.510
visited by inducing
RowHammer induced

01:32:11.511 --> 01:32:13.075
bit flips through JavaScript.

01:32:13.076 --> 01:32:15.220
Very interesting.
This is another one,

01:32:15.221 --> 01:32:16.755
this basically show that
you could do this on

01:32:16.756 --> 01:32:18.955
an Android system and
an arm processor,

01:32:18.956 --> 01:32:21.240
and what they did was
they were able to,

01:32:21.241 --> 01:32:23.050
because they knew how
the operating system

01:32:23.051 --> 01:32:24.470
actually allocated pages,

01:32:24.471 --> 01:32:26.410
they were able to figure out

01:32:26.411 --> 01:32:28.430
which pages are
vulnerable to RowHammer,

01:32:28.431 --> 01:32:30.030
they through a profiling process.

01:32:30.031 --> 01:32:33.990
They were able to fool
the operating system into

01:32:33.991 --> 01:32:36.930
allocating a page table

01:32:36.931 --> 01:32:40.820
into a page that they knew
was vulnerable to RowHammer,

01:32:40.821 --> 01:32:43.185
and they would hammer that
and they would gain access

01:32:43.186 --> 01:32:46.660
deterministically to
many cell phones this way.

01:32:46.661 --> 01:32:48.400
That's another beautiful
paper actually,

01:32:48.401 --> 01:32:49.820
and you can download
their app I think.

01:32:49.821 --> 01:32:51.275
I don't know if this
is still functional,

01:32:51.276 --> 01:32:53.790
if you'd like to be hacked.

01:32:53.791 --> 01:32:55.570
This actually more recent.

01:32:55.571 --> 01:32:58.340
This is May 2018,

01:32:58.341 --> 01:33:01.345
the same folks at Amsterdam.

01:33:01.346 --> 01:33:03.310
They basically show
that you could do this

01:33:03.311 --> 01:33:05.000
through the GPU in an integrated,

01:33:05.001 --> 01:33:07.055
again, in a mobile system.

01:33:07.056 --> 01:33:10.120
A GPU is much more because
it can access memory much

01:33:10.121 --> 01:33:11.215
faster you can actually induce

01:33:11.216 --> 01:33:13.425
these bit flips much better.

01:33:13.426 --> 01:33:15.215
You could actually do
it over the network

01:33:15.216 --> 01:33:18.625
also through the RDMA
by exploiting RDMA.

01:33:18.626 --> 01:33:20.940
I believe there's more to come,

01:33:20.941 --> 01:33:22.730
maybe one solution to RowHammers.

01:33:22.731 --> 01:33:26.210
This is another attack that
could drive people crazy.

01:33:26.211 --> 01:33:27.730
I don't think it's
a good solution.

01:33:27.731 --> 01:33:29.470
Let me very quickly go over

01:33:29.471 --> 01:33:30.890
understanding RowHammer
and then we'll

01:33:30.891 --> 01:33:32.130
talk about solutions and

01:33:32.131 --> 01:33:34.084
then maybe some future
vulnerabilities.

01:33:34.085 --> 01:33:36.070
So, as I said there
are a bunch of causes,

01:33:36.071 --> 01:33:38.915
the sector complex problem
as circuit becomes smaller.

01:33:38.916 --> 01:33:41.400
You have many failure
mechanisms that affect

01:33:41.401 --> 01:33:44.115
this that in combination
lead to RowHammer.

01:33:44.116 --> 01:33:45.755
I'm not going to go
into this in detail,

01:33:45.756 --> 01:33:47.010
but manufacturers are very well

01:33:47.011 --> 01:33:49.055
aware of it and we're
in touch with them.

01:33:49.056 --> 01:33:51.300
If you have this infrastructure,
you can do many,

01:33:51.301 --> 01:33:52.375
many studies, and I'm

01:33:52.376 --> 01:33:54.025
going to talk about
a couple of these.

01:33:54.026 --> 01:33:57.360
Basically, what is
the difference between

01:33:57.361 --> 01:33:59.050
the address of a row that

01:33:59.051 --> 01:34:00.835
you're hammering and
the victim rows?

01:34:00.836 --> 01:34:02.950
We did the study and it turns out

01:34:02.951 --> 01:34:05.205
most of them are
adjacent rows as expected,

01:34:05.206 --> 01:34:06.800
but some of them are
not adjacent because

01:34:06.801 --> 01:34:08.350
there is some internal
remapping that

01:34:08.351 --> 01:34:11.460
the address remapping that
DRAM does internally.

01:34:11.461 --> 01:34:13.680
So, if you want to
hammer really perfectly,

01:34:13.681 --> 01:34:15.105
you may want to know
this address mapping,

01:34:15.106 --> 01:34:16.480
or if you want to protect.

01:34:16.481 --> 01:34:18.975
The access interval
today you can access

01:34:18.976 --> 01:34:21.345
memory every 55 nanoseconds,

01:34:21.346 --> 01:34:24.290
that's the TRC or cycling
delayed as a single bank.

01:34:24.291 --> 01:34:26.405
If you actually prohibit this,

01:34:26.406 --> 01:34:27.600
you can get rid of the errors,

01:34:27.601 --> 01:34:28.760
clear this is one solution.

01:34:28.761 --> 01:34:30.280
You can throttle
the accesses to memory

01:34:30.281 --> 01:34:32.560
by reducing the access rate,

01:34:32.561 --> 01:34:35.845
so this is clear,
you can do that.

01:34:35.846 --> 01:34:37.320
This is not a good
solution I believe because

01:34:37.321 --> 01:34:39.075
this reduces your
performance clearly.

01:34:39.076 --> 01:34:40.500
Refresh Interval is another

01:34:40.501 --> 01:34:41.840
parameter that you can play with.

01:34:41.841 --> 01:34:44.590
Clearly, if you refresh
the DRAM more often,

01:34:44.591 --> 01:34:46.345
the probability of
attack reduces.

01:34:46.346 --> 01:34:49.380
This is you reduce
the refreshes by seven x,

01:34:49.381 --> 01:34:51.130
it gets rid of every single error

01:34:51.131 --> 01:34:52.265
that we see in the DRAM,

01:34:52.266 --> 01:34:54.955
but increasing the refreshes by

01:34:54.956 --> 01:34:56.120
seven x is probably not

01:34:56.121 --> 01:34:58.710
a good solution, even though
it solves the problem.

01:34:58.711 --> 01:35:00.180
This is very interesting because

01:35:00.181 --> 01:35:02.360
the attack is actually much more

01:35:02.361 --> 01:35:04.235
possible if your data pattern

01:35:04.236 --> 01:35:06.910
is conducive to the attacks.

01:35:06.911 --> 01:35:08.600
So, if your data pattern
is solid like this,

01:35:08.601 --> 01:35:09.860
you don't get a lot of errors,

01:35:09.861 --> 01:35:12.190
but if your data pattern
is this way which induces

01:35:12.191 --> 01:35:13.595
much more coupling between

01:35:13.596 --> 01:35:15.820
the different cells that
are adjacent to each other,

01:35:15.821 --> 01:35:18.115
you get many, many more errors.

01:35:18.116 --> 01:35:20.700
Okay, so there are
a bunch of other results.

01:35:20.701 --> 01:35:22.410
I'm not going to go
through this. The red ones

01:35:22.411 --> 01:35:23.640
are the important ones
for security.

01:35:23.641 --> 01:35:26.695
Errors are repeatable if you
can actually flip a bit,

01:35:26.696 --> 01:35:27.835
you're going to flip it again and

01:35:27.836 --> 01:35:29.575
again and again and again.

01:35:29.576 --> 01:35:31.280
You can actually get many errors

01:35:31.281 --> 01:35:32.610
per cache-line which means that

01:35:32.611 --> 01:35:34.200
simple error-correcting codes are

01:35:34.201 --> 01:35:36.005
not able to get rid
of all of the errors,

01:35:36.006 --> 01:35:38.410
you need more sophisticated
error-correcting codes.

01:35:38.411 --> 01:35:40.130
Cells are actually affected by

01:35:40.131 --> 01:35:42.180
two aggressor rows
on either side.

01:35:42.181 --> 01:35:44.300
This is actually what
Google exploited to make

01:35:44.301 --> 01:35:46.910
the attack much more powerful.

01:35:46.911 --> 01:35:48.335
They basically did
this double-sided

01:35:48.336 --> 01:35:49.840
Rowhammering and they hammered

01:35:49.841 --> 01:35:51.290
a single row by sandwiching it

01:35:51.291 --> 01:35:53.895
between two things
that are hammered.

01:35:53.896 --> 01:35:56.510
There's been a lot more
in RowHammer analysis in

01:35:56.511 --> 01:35:58.645
this paper and a recent paper
that I've written.

01:35:58.646 --> 01:36:00.240
I'd be happy to talk about
that separately also.

01:36:00.241 --> 01:36:02.055
But, let's talk about
solutions a little bit.

01:36:02.056 --> 01:36:03.720
These are more
traditional solutions,

01:36:03.721 --> 01:36:06.435
I think, which all
have downsides.

01:36:06.436 --> 01:36:08.090
Clearly, you can make
better DRAM chips,

01:36:08.091 --> 01:36:10.645
but that's going to
be difficult to do.

01:36:10.646 --> 01:36:12.935
You can refresh frequently,
we'll get back to that.

01:36:12.936 --> 01:36:14.770
You can have sophisticated ECC,

01:36:14.771 --> 01:36:16.915
and you can have access
counters to throttle.

01:36:16.916 --> 01:36:18.180
But, all of these actually come

01:36:18.181 --> 01:36:19.575
with downsides, I believe.

01:36:19.576 --> 01:36:22.890
So, we want to have simple
solutions to the problem and

01:36:22.891 --> 01:36:24.775
our paper actually looked

01:36:24.776 --> 01:36:26.090
at all of these
different solutions.

01:36:26.091 --> 01:36:27.840
Let me tell you about what is

01:36:27.841 --> 01:36:30.410
employed in existing systems
because in existing systems,

01:36:30.411 --> 01:36:32.695
you have to employ something
to be able to patch it.

01:36:32.696 --> 01:36:34.980
This is Apple's patch
for RowHammer.

01:36:34.981 --> 01:36:37.530
Basically, they said
that they mitigated

01:36:37.531 --> 01:36:38.755
the RowHammer issue by increasing

01:36:38.756 --> 01:36:40.185
the memory refresh rates.

01:36:40.186 --> 01:36:42.085
This is, I think,
employed by industry.

01:36:42.086 --> 01:36:43.900
This is the
configurability that we

01:36:43.901 --> 01:36:46.050
have in our memory
controllers today.

01:36:46.051 --> 01:36:48.900
We can do it and as
a result we do it.

01:36:48.901 --> 01:36:50.550
I believe, there is
a reasonable solution,

01:36:50.551 --> 01:36:51.960
which is much simpler than

01:36:51.961 --> 01:36:53.280
the software-based
solutions that could

01:36:53.281 --> 01:36:54.980
potentially detect the attacks.

01:36:54.981 --> 01:36:56.740
Of course, the downside is

01:36:56.741 --> 01:36:58.660
we actually don't want to
increase the refresh rates.

01:36:58.661 --> 01:36:59.910
In real systems, we
want to get rid of

01:36:59.911 --> 01:37:01.495
refresh as much as possible.

01:37:01.496 --> 01:37:03.835
If you increase the refresh
rates you're increasing

01:37:03.836 --> 01:37:06.860
the performance impact
and also power impact.

01:37:06.861 --> 01:37:09.215
So, our solution was
a more probabilistic,

01:37:09.216 --> 01:37:11.310
we call that the probabilistic
adjacent row activation.

01:37:11.311 --> 01:37:13.795
The idea is after
you close the row,

01:37:13.796 --> 01:37:15.680
you activate one of
the neighbors or

01:37:15.681 --> 01:37:18.700
both of the neighbors with
very low probability.

01:37:19.160 --> 01:37:22.370
This gives you a reliability
guarantee that's better than

01:37:22.371 --> 01:37:23.700
the reliability guarantee
that you have

01:37:23.701 --> 01:37:25.085
for hard disks for today,

01:37:25.086 --> 01:37:27.010
so this is pretty strong
depends on, of course,

01:37:27.011 --> 01:37:29.315
how you set your p
probability over here.

01:37:29.316 --> 01:37:31.390
But, the big advantage of this is

01:37:31.391 --> 01:37:33.350
you don't refresh
the entire memory,

01:37:33.351 --> 01:37:35.375
you refresh only in

01:37:35.376 --> 01:37:37.920
a targeted way and very,
very infrequently.

01:37:37.921 --> 01:37:39.385
As a result, the overheads are

01:37:39.386 --> 01:37:41.095
very low and also stateless,

01:37:41.096 --> 01:37:42.530
you don't need to keep track of

01:37:42.531 --> 01:37:44.585
any state to be able to do that

01:37:44.586 --> 01:37:46.145
because you know which
way you're closing

01:37:46.146 --> 01:37:48.600
before you refresh it
probabilistically.

01:37:48.601 --> 01:37:51.450
So, there are multiple ways
of actually implementing it.

01:37:51.451 --> 01:37:52.885
The first one is
actually employed

01:37:52.886 --> 01:37:55.230
in DRAM chips going forward.

01:37:55.231 --> 01:37:57.610
I'm not sure if this is
a really good idea inside

01:37:57.611 --> 01:38:00.140
the DRAM chip going forward
fully because the way it's

01:38:00.141 --> 01:38:02.200
employed in existing DRAM chips
without changing

01:38:02.201 --> 01:38:03.510
the interface is by

01:38:03.511 --> 01:38:05.435
exploiting the slack
and timing parameters.

01:38:05.436 --> 01:38:07.420
Whenever you close all
there's enough slack in

01:38:07.421 --> 01:38:09.705
the timing parameters that
the DRAM manufacturers can

01:38:09.706 --> 01:38:11.835
sneak in a refresh to

01:38:11.836 --> 01:38:14.640
the adjacent rows or one
of the adjacent rows.

01:38:14.641 --> 01:38:17.420
So, we've actually shown
that there's plenty of slack

01:38:17.421 --> 01:38:18.610
today that you can exploit to be

01:38:18.611 --> 01:38:19.920
able to do this reliably.

01:38:19.921 --> 01:38:20.990
But, going forward, we actually

01:38:20.991 --> 01:38:22.295
want to remove that slack also,

01:38:22.296 --> 01:38:24.450
so that we can make
DRAM lower latency.

01:38:24.451 --> 01:38:26.155
So, I don't believe this
is a really good solution

01:38:26.156 --> 01:38:27.480
without changing the interface.

01:38:27.481 --> 01:38:29.650
The second solution is doing
it in the memory controller,

01:38:29.651 --> 01:38:32.375
having a more intelligent
memory controller that

01:38:32.376 --> 01:38:34.650
basically knows which rows

01:38:34.651 --> 01:38:36.095
are physically adjacent
to each other.

01:38:36.096 --> 01:38:37.420
This information is not known to

01:38:37.421 --> 01:38:39.535
the memory controller
today because DRAM

01:38:39.536 --> 01:38:41.215
actually does remapping of

01:38:41.216 --> 01:38:43.510
rows internally for
various reasons.

01:38:43.511 --> 01:38:44.900
But, if this information is

01:38:44.901 --> 01:38:46.085
communicated to
the memory controller,

01:38:46.086 --> 01:38:48.055
I believe there could be
much better solutions.

01:38:48.056 --> 01:38:49.760
So, we need a better
DRAM interface and

01:38:49.761 --> 01:38:51.260
more intelligent memory
controllers to solve

01:38:51.261 --> 01:38:53.935
these problems in
a nice way I think.

01:38:53.936 --> 01:38:57.440
So, this was actually
something that I recently saw.

01:38:57.441 --> 01:38:59.520
Apparently, this is
one of the Thinkpads.

01:38:59.521 --> 01:39:03.695
In the BIOS, we can have
different RowHammer solutions.

01:39:03.696 --> 01:39:04.860
You can either double

01:39:04.861 --> 01:39:06.385
your refresh rate or have

01:39:06.386 --> 01:39:08.150
this hardware
RowHammer protection,

01:39:08.151 --> 01:39:10.050
which is kind of
mysterious, but clearly,

01:39:10.051 --> 01:39:12.620
they're doing some
probabilistic solution.

01:39:12.621 --> 01:39:14.070
So, you can actually
change the RowHammer

01:39:14.071 --> 01:39:16.550
activation probability
in some way.

01:39:16.900 --> 01:39:19.515
You can decide
your protection level

01:39:19.516 --> 01:39:21.160
if you will over here,

01:39:21.161 --> 01:39:23.050
it was fun to see this.

01:39:23.051 --> 01:39:25.480
Okay, so industry is

01:39:25.481 --> 01:39:27.135
actually writing
papers about it, too.

01:39:27.136 --> 01:39:28.970
This is not related to RowHammer,

01:39:28.971 --> 01:39:30.060
but this talks about the DRAM

01:39:30.061 --> 01:39:31.780
scaling challenges in general.

01:39:31.781 --> 01:39:35.550
It focus on what I said is
the real scaling chance,

01:39:35.551 --> 01:39:38.770
the refresh problem and the
variable retention time bomb,

01:39:38.771 --> 01:39:40.950
which we will cover if
you have time still.

01:39:40.951 --> 01:39:43.405
But, the key point that
I want to make is,

01:39:43.406 --> 01:39:45.640
rather than recommending
this paper that was written by

01:39:45.641 --> 01:39:47.690
two unlikely partners that

01:39:47.691 --> 01:39:49.210
will ever write a paper together,

01:39:49.211 --> 01:39:53.335
Samsung and Intel, they also
say a good solution for

01:39:53.336 --> 01:39:55.215
them is actually
co-architecting DRAM and

01:39:55.216 --> 01:39:56.280
controllers together and

01:39:56.281 --> 01:39:57.545
having an intelligent controller.

01:39:57.546 --> 01:39:58.975
This paper actually proposed

01:39:58.976 --> 01:40:01.605
Error-Correcting Codes
to be inside the DRAM.

01:40:01.606 --> 01:40:03.045
If you went to the
DRAM manufacturers

01:40:03.046 --> 01:40:04.090
10 years ago and said I

01:40:04.091 --> 01:40:05.505
want error-correcting
codes in your chip,

01:40:05.506 --> 01:40:07.820
you would be kicked out of
the door as soon as possible,

01:40:07.821 --> 01:40:09.150
probably because they don't want

01:40:09.151 --> 01:40:11.270
to reduce their capacity.

01:40:11.271 --> 01:40:12.810
But, now actually, DRAM chips

01:40:12.811 --> 01:40:14.480
going forward will have
error-correcting codes.

01:40:14.481 --> 01:40:16.530
But, as I said,
error-correcting codes are

01:40:16.531 --> 01:40:18.570
good at solving random issue.

01:40:18.571 --> 01:40:21.055
They're actually costly solution.

01:40:21.056 --> 01:40:22.560
We want to really target

01:40:22.561 --> 01:40:24.670
the solutions to
the problems at hand.

01:40:24.671 --> 01:40:25.970
So, I think, RowHammer
can be sold in

01:40:25.971 --> 01:40:27.720
a much easier way than
error-correcting codes.

01:40:27.721 --> 01:40:29.460
The reason they're putting
error-correcting codes is

01:40:29.461 --> 01:40:31.635
because of retention
because they they're not

01:40:31.636 --> 01:40:33.550
able to determine
the retention times really

01:40:33.551 --> 01:40:35.700
easily and as a result

01:40:35.701 --> 01:40:38.170
error-correcting
codes can correct

01:40:38.171 --> 01:40:39.280
some of those areas that are

01:40:39.281 --> 01:40:41.425
happening because of
retention issues.

01:40:41.426 --> 01:40:44.375
Okay. So, I

01:40:44.376 --> 01:40:46.070
said Intelligent Memory Controls
is one solution,

01:40:46.071 --> 01:40:47.510
and we know actually
how to build this.

01:40:47.511 --> 01:40:48.795
We've actually been building this

01:40:48.796 --> 01:40:50.525
for Flush memory for a long time.

01:40:50.526 --> 01:40:51.900
I believe DRAM is going to look

01:40:51.901 --> 01:40:53.710
increasingly more
like Flush memory,

01:40:53.711 --> 01:40:55.400
as it scales down.

01:40:55.401 --> 01:40:57.350
If you look at
the Flush memory Controller,

01:40:57.351 --> 01:40:59.680
there is a paper that we
recently written based

01:40:59.681 --> 01:41:00.680
on about eight years of

01:41:00.681 --> 01:41:02.675
research that we've
done in the field.

01:41:02.676 --> 01:41:05.690
There's a lot of
error correction mechanisms

01:41:05.691 --> 01:41:07.485
that goes into the
Memory Controller.

01:41:07.486 --> 01:41:08.850
Memory Controller
really understands

01:41:08.851 --> 01:41:10.205
the different types of errors,

01:41:10.206 --> 01:41:12.570
and actually targets the error
correction mechanisms to

01:41:12.571 --> 01:41:13.585
the different types of errors,

01:41:13.586 --> 01:41:15.765
specializes it's mechanisms.

01:41:15.766 --> 01:41:19.065
I'd be happy to talk about
that in more detail certainly.

01:41:19.066 --> 01:41:20.960
So, basically, a key
takeaway, I think,

01:41:20.961 --> 01:41:22.925
to solve these issues
going forward is,

01:41:22.926 --> 01:41:24.640
we want the Intelligent
Memory Controllers.

01:41:24.641 --> 01:41:25.860
Clearly, we have a challenge

01:41:25.861 --> 01:41:27.600
and opportunity going forward.

01:41:27.601 --> 01:41:29.400
How do we design
fundamentally secure,

01:41:29.401 --> 01:41:32.205
reliable, and safe
computing architectures?

