both as specification,

01:46:54.006 --> 01:46:57.840
so that manufacturers
can comply with,

01:46:57.841 --> 01:46:59.400
but not all of
those details are open.

01:46:59.401 --> 01:47:01.835
&gt;&gt; Exactly. I think that's
part of the problem.

01:47:01.836 --> 01:47:03.100
If the details are not open,

01:47:03.101 --> 01:47:04.295
it's very hard to reason about

01:47:04.296 --> 01:47:07.325
the efficacy of the solutions.

01:47:07.326 --> 01:47:08.315
&gt;&gt; I just want to
know what you heard.

01:47:08.316 --> 01:47:11.940
&gt;&gt; Yeah. That's all I can say.

01:47:11.990 --> 01:47:15.315
&gt;&gt; So, you briefly
mentioned SRAM.

01:47:15.316 --> 01:47:18.790
Have there been any observations

01:47:18.791 --> 01:47:23.025
of row hammer like
vulnerabilities in SRAM?

01:47:23.026 --> 01:47:25.300
&gt;&gt; As far as I know,
not in real systems,

01:47:25.301 --> 01:47:28.280
but a lot of people have
shown that when they build

01:47:28.281 --> 01:47:31.720
circuits that are
very small feature sizes,

01:47:31.721 --> 01:47:34.020
SRAM also is vulnerable
to read disturb errors.

01:47:34.021 --> 01:47:35.810
But, their protection
mechanisms I believe in

01:47:35.811 --> 01:47:37.365
existing SRAMs

01:47:37.366 --> 01:47:39.950
in the processors because
they're easy to do.

01:47:39.951 --> 01:47:41.440
Right? You don't need
change any interfaces

01:47:41.441 --> 01:47:43.760
for those protection mechanisms.

01:47:46.890 --> 01:47:49.040
&gt;&gt; So, you mentioned the boat

01:47:49.041 --> 01:47:51.075
bringing down the refresh time,

01:47:51.076 --> 01:47:53.120
but for that don't
you think you need to

01:47:53.121 --> 01:47:54.750
know the retention times
of each rows,

01:47:54.751 --> 01:47:57.800
which may be widely
variable across rows?

01:47:57.801 --> 01:47:59.640
&gt;&gt; You mean as a solution
to [inaudible]?

01:47:59.641 --> 01:47:59.975
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:47:59.976 --> 01:48:01.490
&gt;&gt; So, they're basically

01:48:01.491 --> 01:48:04.600
increasing the refresh frequency.

01:48:04.750 --> 01:48:07.890
Basically, you're
refreshing more often.

01:48:07.891 --> 01:48:08.860
That's not a problem.

01:48:08.861 --> 01:48:10.070
&gt;&gt; No, but how often?

01:48:10.071 --> 01:48:11.530
Because the different
rows will have

01:48:11.531 --> 01:48:12.785
variable retention times

01:48:12.786 --> 01:48:14.290
due to manufacturing
variabilities.

01:48:14.291 --> 01:48:16.080
&gt;&gt; That' true. But their goal

01:48:16.081 --> 01:48:17.560
is to basically refresh
more frequently

01:48:17.561 --> 01:48:19.080
such that you cannot do

01:48:19.081 --> 01:48:20.935
as many activates with
their refresh interval.

01:48:20.936 --> 01:48:21.275
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:48:21.276 --> 01:48:22.840
&gt;&gt; But it doesn't matter
what the retention time of

01:48:22.841 --> 01:48:23.560
their orbits as long as

01:48:23.561 --> 01:48:24.710
you're refreshing
more frequently,

01:48:24.711 --> 01:48:26.700
you don't have
any correctness issues

01:48:26.701 --> 01:48:28.440
in terms of retention time loss.

01:48:28.441 --> 01:48:33.765
But you prevent
RowHammer attacks.

01:48:33.766 --> 01:48:35.925
But your question I think, how

01:48:35.926 --> 01:48:38.070
much should you increase
your refresh interval.

01:48:38.071 --> 01:48:39.490
According to
our results, if you're

01:48:39.491 --> 01:48:41.685
only solution is refresh,

01:48:41.686 --> 01:48:44.060
if you want to get rid of
every single error that we've

01:48:44.061 --> 01:48:46.635
seen in our in our dense,

01:48:46.636 --> 01:48:48.935
you want to increase
refresh rate by 7x.

01:48:48.936 --> 01:48:50.610
Clearly they're not doing 7x,

01:48:50.611 --> 01:48:52.170
they are doing 2x in my opinion.

01:48:52.171 --> 01:48:53.890
The picture that
I showed you from

01:48:53.891 --> 01:48:56.805
ThinkPad BIOS was 2x,
that was only option.

01:48:56.806 --> 01:48:58.350
Is 2x enough to get rid of

01:48:58.351 --> 01:49:00.505
all of the errors?
That's a good question.

01:49:00.506 --> 01:49:02.260
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

01:49:07.490 --> 01:49:10.005
&gt;&gt; Sorry, got to
ask this question.

01:49:10.006 --> 01:49:13.880
Otherwise I couldn't follow
what you were talking about.

01:49:13.881 --> 01:49:18.770
So, a while back when I'm
still in the the memory area.

01:49:18.771 --> 01:49:21.060
My understanding is that
RowHammer is caused

01:49:21.061 --> 01:49:28.325
by broken state in the cell,

01:49:28.326 --> 01:49:30.030
the band gap and then when

01:49:30.031 --> 01:49:33.030
you just due to the metal layer.

01:49:33.031 --> 01:49:35.110
If you use poly-silicon
gate to problem

01:49:35.111 --> 01:49:37.345
would have gone away, right?

01:49:37.346 --> 01:49:40.140
So, are you saying
that even today,

01:49:40.141 --> 01:49:43.050
Samsung still using metal gate,

01:49:43.051 --> 01:49:46.245
that's why you say I have this
problem. Is that the case?

01:49:46.246 --> 01:49:47.600
&gt;&gt; So, I cannot
speak for Samsung,

01:49:47.601 --> 01:49:49.525
but I think the causes
that I mentioned.

01:49:49.526 --> 01:49:50.750
I think the cause
that you mentioned

01:49:50.751 --> 01:49:52.230
is still certainly valid.

01:49:52.231 --> 01:49:52.910
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:49:52.911 --> 01:49:54.620
&gt;&gt; But the but the cause
that I mentioned,

01:49:54.621 --> 01:49:56.415
it's really a combination
of those reasons.

01:49:56.416 --> 01:49:56.660
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:49:56.661 --> 01:49:59.015
&gt;&gt; There are multiple reasons
that as far as we know.

01:49:59.016 --> 01:50:00.775
&gt;&gt; So you'll
experiment was done on

01:50:00.776 --> 01:50:03.225
later than even
the latest memory,

01:50:03.226 --> 01:50:05.270
the DRAM, you still
see the problem?

01:50:05.271 --> 01:50:08.040
&gt;&gt; So the experiments
that I reported

01:50:08.041 --> 01:50:11.250
are from 2012 to 2014,

01:50:11.251 --> 01:50:13.345
when we discovered the problem.

01:50:13.346 --> 01:50:15.525
The paper was published in 2014.

01:50:15.526 --> 01:50:18.605
The latest DRAM, we're
looking into it.

01:50:18.606 --> 01:50:20.630
There are reports
that latest DRAM

01:50:20.631 --> 01:50:23.120
also has these areas
that Stefan mentioned.

01:50:23.121 --> 01:50:27.830
But we didn't do
those studies ourselves.

01:50:28.710 --> 01:50:31.360
I agree with you if you can solve

01:50:31.361 --> 01:50:33.715
the problem with
changing the gate,

01:50:33.716 --> 01:50:35.215
that we ideal,

01:50:35.216 --> 01:50:36.765
I'm not sure if it's
going to be very easy.

01:50:36.766 --> 01:50:41.120
Yeah, I agree.

01:50:41.121 --> 01:50:42.815
ECC is not a good
solution to this problem.

01:50:42.816 --> 01:50:44.900
But, I think the
probabilistic solution

01:50:44.901 --> 01:50:48.210
is maybe cheaper than
the gate solutions,

01:50:48.211 --> 01:50:50.250
depending on the constraints.

01:50:50.640 --> 01:50:55.480
Okay, so let me use the last
few minutes to conclude.

01:50:55.481 --> 01:50:57.680
I think we had a good discussion.

01:50:57.681 --> 01:50:58.670
I'm not going to go over

01:50:58.671 --> 01:51:00.845
these future challenges
unfortunately,

01:51:00.846 --> 01:51:02.940
I think there are a bunch but

01:51:02.941 --> 01:51:04.375
you can take a look
at the slides.

01:51:04.376 --> 01:51:05.640
Clearly refresh is going to be

01:51:05.641 --> 01:51:06.900
a challenge and these slides

01:51:06.901 --> 01:51:08.200
actually have a lot of detail on

01:51:08.201 --> 01:51:10.155
refresh if you're
interested in that.

01:51:10.156 --> 01:51:14.245
I believe actually there
retention time issues

01:51:14.246 --> 01:51:16.565
that may be slipping
into the fields,

01:51:16.566 --> 01:51:18.635
but they may be harder to exploit

01:51:18.636 --> 01:51:22.930
than RowHammer at
the moment at least.

01:51:22.931 --> 01:51:24.910
So, how do we keep memory secure?

01:51:24.911 --> 01:51:28.850
I think clearly we
have issues with DRAM,

01:51:28.851 --> 01:51:30.035
we have issued this Flush memory,

01:51:30.036 --> 01:51:31.190
or Flush memory is a little bit

01:51:31.191 --> 01:51:33.520
far from the system today.

01:51:33.521 --> 01:51:34.890
But emerging memory
technologies actually

01:51:34.891 --> 01:51:36.290
all have their
reliability problems.

01:51:36.291 --> 01:51:37.720
Read disturb, write disturb.

01:51:37.721 --> 01:51:40.405
Many, many different
reliability problems.

01:51:40.406 --> 01:51:43.220
I think we need
some principled approaches.

01:51:43.221 --> 01:51:44.960
We need to somehow
predict and prevent

01:51:44.961 --> 01:51:47.505
site safety issues and I go
back to the Galloping Gertie,

01:51:47.506 --> 01:51:50.315
which is the Tacoma
Narrows Bridge.

01:51:50.316 --> 01:51:53.990
People have developed
principle designs for this.

01:51:53.991 --> 01:51:55.895
This actually taught
and civil engineering,

01:51:55.896 --> 01:51:59.410
and physics classes this
particular bridge if you will.

01:51:59.411 --> 01:52:01.045
So, how do we do it for memory?

01:52:01.046 --> 01:52:02.670
This is my proposal.

01:52:02.671 --> 01:52:04.785
I think we want to
first understand.

01:52:04.786 --> 01:52:07.365
It's very difficult to
really model these effects.

01:52:07.366 --> 01:52:08.480
If you want to go
through, we've done

01:52:08.481 --> 01:52:09.600
a lot of circuit simulations.

01:52:09.601 --> 01:52:11.650
It's very, very difficult
to model something

01:52:11.651 --> 01:52:14.050
like RowHammer in circuits.

01:52:14.051 --> 01:52:15.380
You really need to somehow

01:52:15.381 --> 01:52:17.890
predicts based on
other technologies,

01:52:17.891 --> 01:52:19.270
based on past experience.

01:52:19.271 --> 01:52:20.570
So we want solid methodologies

01:52:20.571 --> 01:52:22.360
for failure modeling
and discovery.

01:52:22.361 --> 01:52:24.900
I believe this has to
come from real devices,

01:52:24.901 --> 01:52:26.860
both at the small scale
and large scale.

01:52:26.861 --> 01:52:29.475
We want to build models that
can predict the future.

01:52:29.476 --> 01:52:31.250
We want to build models
that can predict

01:52:31.251 --> 01:52:33.510
from different devices
potentially.

01:52:33.511 --> 01:52:34.410
How do we do that?

01:52:34.411 --> 01:52:35.670
I think that's
an open research question.

01:52:35.671 --> 01:52:36.970
I mean you do want to develop

01:52:36.971 --> 01:52:38.810
metrics for secure
architectures and

01:52:38.811 --> 01:52:40.210
I say secure over here but I

01:52:40.211 --> 01:52:42.380
think RowHammer
demonstrated that,

01:52:42.381 --> 01:52:44.980
reliability, safety and
security are really

01:52:44.981 --> 01:52:46.550
very much related to each

01:52:46.551 --> 01:52:49.255
other in this particular context.

01:52:49.256 --> 01:52:51.255
On top of this, I
believe it's architect.

01:52:51.256 --> 01:52:52.890
We need to have
principled co-architecting

01:52:52.891 --> 01:52:53.970
of the system and memory.

01:52:53.971 --> 01:52:55.180
We need to have
a good partitioning

01:52:55.181 --> 01:52:56.455
of duties across the stack.

01:52:56.456 --> 01:52:59.250
So, I believe ECC is not a good
solution because it's not

01:52:59.251 --> 01:53:01.025
a good partitioning
of the duties for the

01:53:01.026 --> 01:53:03.215
given for the given
problem of RowHammer.

01:53:03.216 --> 01:53:04.840
So, for each problem
we need to find

01:53:04.841 --> 01:53:06.430
the good partitioning and
I believe Flush memory is

01:53:06.431 --> 01:53:07.735
a very good example where

01:53:07.736 --> 01:53:10.415
people actually find
the right partitioning.

01:53:10.416 --> 01:53:12.850
So, they saw some of
the problems of ECC,

01:53:12.851 --> 01:53:14.060
but they sold a lot
of the problems with

01:53:14.061 --> 01:53:16.170
voltage scaling as well.

01:53:16.171 --> 01:53:20.030
I believe a good architecting
requires figuring out

01:53:20.031 --> 01:53:21.720
these or

01:53:21.721 --> 01:53:24.430
potentially preventing
these unforeseen consequences.

01:53:24.431 --> 01:53:27.560
So how do you prevent for
unforeseen consequences?

01:53:27.561 --> 01:53:29.530
I believe if we
had better program

01:53:29.531 --> 01:53:30.940
built in our memory controller,

01:53:30.941 --> 01:53:34.755
we wouldn't be refreshing
our entire memory by 2x or 4x.

01:53:34.756 --> 01:53:36.400
So, if you had
a better programmability

01:53:36.401 --> 01:53:38.940
or better patch-ability
in the field,

01:53:38.941 --> 01:53:40.585
we would be doing better today.

01:53:40.586 --> 01:53:43.115
I think this design
needs to change.

01:53:43.116 --> 01:53:45.690
Basically, today we're not really

01:53:45.691 --> 01:53:47.450
thinking about security in

01:53:47.451 --> 01:53:48.690
our designs and
the hardware design.

01:53:48.691 --> 01:53:50.350
We don't really
designed with security.

01:53:50.351 --> 01:53:53.740
I believe we need to
design change that also.

01:53:53.741 --> 01:53:55.810
I didn't talk about it,
but one of the ways

01:53:55.811 --> 01:53:58.290
of having a design that can

01:53:58.291 --> 01:54:00.160
over time fix some of

01:54:00.161 --> 01:54:01.580
these reliability
issues is having

01:54:01.581 --> 01:54:03.205
a design that can
do online testing.

01:54:03.206 --> 01:54:05.610
Which is essentially what
Flush memory is doing today.

01:54:05.611 --> 01:54:07.300
If we have a mechanism to do

01:54:07.301 --> 01:54:10.130
online testing in
a low overhead manner in DRAM,

01:54:10.131 --> 01:54:11.970
I think that would go a long way.

01:54:11.971 --> 01:54:15.295
Because that can enable also
patch-ability potentially.

01:54:15.296 --> 01:54:17.110
So, that's what
we've been doing to

01:54:17.111 --> 01:54:19.010
understand we built
these infrastructures,

01:54:19.011 --> 01:54:20.980
both were flashed memory
and DRAM and

01:54:20.981 --> 01:54:23.000
we've been doing large-scale
and small-scale studies.

01:54:23.001 --> 01:54:24.420
I believe they're
actually vulnerabilities

01:54:24.421 --> 01:54:25.545
in Flush memory also.

01:54:25.546 --> 01:54:28.265
We've been exploring some
of these that are similar.

01:54:28.266 --> 01:54:30.180
Read disturb is
one example over there,

01:54:30.181 --> 01:54:31.505
but this is much much more,

01:54:31.506 --> 01:54:32.600
much much harder to exploit

01:54:32.601 --> 01:54:34.205
because Flush memory
is much harder.

01:54:34.206 --> 01:54:35.470
It's not directly exposed to

01:54:35.471 --> 01:54:37.255
the programming model
basically today.

01:54:37.256 --> 01:54:39.025
But there's a lot
to do over there.

01:54:39.026 --> 01:54:40.970
I'm not going to cover this.

01:54:40.971 --> 01:54:43.200
I think there are two other
solution reactions that I

01:54:43.201 --> 01:54:45.310
will briefly talk about.
One is new technology.

01:54:45.311 --> 01:54:46.370
You can say, "Oh,
why don't we get

01:54:46.371 --> 01:54:47.560
rid of DRAM and come up with

01:54:47.561 --> 01:54:48.650
some other techniques
that doesn't have

01:54:48.651 --> 01:54:50.805
these problems?" Good luck.

01:54:50.806 --> 01:54:52.050
I think it's definitely good

01:54:52.051 --> 01:54:53.850
to explore these technologies,

01:54:53.851 --> 01:54:55.150
but all of these technologies

01:54:55.151 --> 01:54:56.800
as they scale to small size,

01:54:56.801 --> 01:54:58.820
they will have
reliability problems.

01:54:58.821 --> 01:55:01.845
Actually some of them have
endurance problems also.

01:55:01.846 --> 01:55:04.620
Maybe the second solution
is even more interesting.

01:55:04.621 --> 01:55:06.505
You can embrace unreliability,

01:55:06.506 --> 01:55:08.770
but if you've got to
do it very carefully.

01:55:08.771 --> 01:55:10.240
Basically, you can
design memories with

01:55:10.241 --> 01:55:11.430
different reliability and store

01:55:11.431 --> 01:55:12.755
data intelligently across them,

01:55:12.756 --> 01:55:16.910
your secure data may be in a
very, very reliable memory,

01:55:16.911 --> 01:55:18.600
that's much more expensive and

01:55:18.601 --> 01:55:21.130
your unsecure data that doesn't
require a lot of security

01:55:21.131 --> 01:55:23.700
or reliability may be
in the masses of memory

01:55:23.701 --> 01:55:26.600
that's not so reliable,
but very low cost.

01:55:26.601 --> 01:55:28.915
As long as you did that
partitioning right,

01:55:28.916 --> 01:55:30.340
I think that's
a really good opportunity.

01:55:30.341 --> 01:55:31.590
But how do you do
that partitioning

01:55:31.591 --> 01:55:33.185
right is a difficult question.

01:55:33.186 --> 01:55:35.280
I believe both of
these solutions over

01:55:35.281 --> 01:55:37.990
here require co-design
across the hierarchy.

01:55:37.991 --> 01:55:39.630
So, it may not be that easy to

01:55:39.631 --> 01:55:42.480
adopt both of these solutions.

01:55:42.481 --> 01:55:44.210
But I think there's
a lot more to do in

01:55:44.211 --> 01:55:46.145
this heterogeneous
reliability memory area,

01:55:46.146 --> 01:55:49.120
that may be a good solution.
So, let me conclude.

01:55:49.121 --> 01:55:51.160
I believe memory
reliabilities reducing,

01:55:51.161 --> 01:55:52.715
there's a lot of data that

01:55:52.716 --> 01:55:55.055
is in the field and
that I've shown you.

01:55:55.056 --> 01:55:56.740
Reliability issues open up

01:55:56.741 --> 01:55:58.390
security vulnerabilities as well.

01:55:58.391 --> 01:56:01.160
These are very hard
to defend against,

01:56:01.161 --> 01:56:03.050
or you come up with

01:56:03.051 --> 01:56:04.400
very suboptimal solutions like

01:56:04.401 --> 01:56:06.640
increasing the refresh
rates across the board.

01:56:06.641 --> 01:56:08.185
RowHammer is an example.

01:56:08.186 --> 01:56:09.775
I believe there will
be more examples.

01:56:09.776 --> 01:56:11.870
I believe the RowHammer

01:56:11.871 --> 01:56:14.700
implicational system security
research are tremendous and

01:56:14.701 --> 01:56:17.340
exciting and there
continues to be a lot of

01:56:17.341 --> 01:56:20.410
papers that are being written
on RowHammer these days.

01:56:20.411 --> 01:56:23.205
So, there's good news, we have
a lot more to do clearly.

01:56:23.206 --> 01:56:24.480
I believe we need to come up with

01:56:24.481 --> 01:56:25.620
principles methodologies and

01:56:25.621 --> 01:56:28.430
designers to be able to
solve problems like this,

01:56:28.431 --> 01:56:32.610
like RowHammer and whatever
comes next after RowHammer.

01:56:32.611 --> 01:56:35.235
I think this is
one principle that we

01:56:35.236 --> 01:56:37.530
will need to adopt
going forward somehow.

01:56:37.531 --> 01:56:40.645
We need to change the processor
memory interface somehow,

01:56:40.646 --> 01:56:44.390
and have more intelligence
in the memory controller.

01:56:44.391 --> 01:56:48.240
Okay. Thank you.

